// Auto-generated offline chapter bundle for Junior Coders Book
window.BOOK_MYSQL_CHAPTERS = {
  "part-01-introduction/ch01-what-is-a-database.md": "# Chapter 1: What is a Database?\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: A database is a tidy, safe and permanent digital filing cabinet. It lets an app store, search and protect a lot of information very quickly.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nPicture the school library on a busy morning. Mr. Imran keeps every borrowing record on loose paper slips in a drawer. A parent calls to ask which book Zayd took home. Mr. Imran has to dig through hundreds of slips. Then a slip falls behind the cupboard, and nobody knows the book is missing. A database is the library's answer: one organised place where every record can be found in a blink.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a database is and why apps need one.\r\n- Compare a database with a spreadsheet.\r\n- Describe what an RDBMS and SQL are.\r\n- Read a very short SQL question and guess what it does.\r\n- Explain why stored data is a trust (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📂 The Filing Cabinet Analogy\r\n\r\nImagine a community academy with 500 students. Each student's name, class and attendance is written on loose sheets in desk drawers.\r\n\r\n- Finding one student's details means digging through hundreds of sheets.\r\n- Two teachers cannot update the same folder at the same time.\r\n- If water spills on the drawer, the records are lost for good.\r\n\r\n```\r\nPaper slips / text files\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ notes.txt                │ → slow to search\r\n│ names, classes, books... │ → easy to lose\r\n└──────────────────────────┘\r\n\r\nDatabase (MySQL)\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ Database server          │ → fast lookups\r\n│ many tidy tables         │ → many users\r\n└──────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nA **database** is an organised collection of data. It is looked after by special **server** software (a program that waits for requests and answers them). That software is built for four things: **speed**, **reliability**, **security** and **many users at once**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📊 Database vs. Spreadsheet\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/database-vs-spreadsheet.svg\" alt=\"Spreadsheet vs Relational Database\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nMany beginners ask: *\"Why not keep everything in Excel or Google Sheets?\"*\r\n\r\nSpreadsheets are great for small lists, quick sums and simple charts. But a real app, such as a school portal or a hospital appointment system, needs more:\r\n\r\n| Feature | Spreadsheet | Database (MySQL) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Size** | Slows down with very many rows. | Searches millions of rows quickly. |\r\n| **Many users** | Edits at the same time can overwrite each other. | Many users can read and write safely. |\r\n| **Correct data** | Anyone can type letters into a number column. | Rules for each column block wrong data. |\r\n| **Privacy (*Amānah*)** | Usually all-or-nothing access to the file. | Each user gets only the access they need. |\r\n| **Links between lists** | Fragile formulas between sheets. | Tables link cleanly with keys and JOINs. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗄️ What is an RDBMS?\r\n\r\n**RDBMS** stands for **Relational Database Management System**.\r\n\r\n- **Relational** means data is kept in **tables** (grids of rows and columns) that can be linked, or *related*, to each other.\r\n- **MySQL** is one of the most popular free, open-source RDBMS programs. **MariaDB** is its close cousin. XAMPP actually installs MariaDB, and it understands the same SQL. Large sites such as Wikipedia run on MariaDB.\r\n\r\n```\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ App (PHP website, phone) │\r\n└────────────┬─────────────┘\r\n             │ sends an SQL question\r\n             ▼\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ MySQL / MariaDB server   │\r\n│ ┌──────────┐ ┌─────────┐ │\r\n│ │ students │ │  books  │ │\r\n│ └──────────┘ └─────────┘ │\r\n└────────────┬─────────────┘\r\n             │ sends back rows\r\n             ▼\r\n       the app shows them\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💬 What is SQL?\r\n\r\n**SQL** (said *\"sequel\"* or *\"S-Q-L\"*) stands for **Structured Query Language**. A **query** is a question you ask the database.\r\n\r\nSQL is the shared language of relational databases. MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL and SQLite all use it. Each has small differences, but the core words are the same.\r\n\r\nHere is a preview. You will learn every part of it in Chapters 9–11. For now, just read it like English:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Show the name, grade and city of students in Grade 9 or above\r\nSELECT name, grade, city\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE grade >= 9\r\nORDER BY name;\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn this book's school database, the answer is:\r\n\r\n| name | grade | city |\r\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| Bilal | 9 | Lahore |\r\n| Khadija | 9 | Islamabad |\r\n| Tariq | 9 | Karachi |\r\n\r\nRead it aloud: *\"Select name, grade and city from students where the grade is 9 or more, sorted by name.\"* SQL was designed to read almost like a sentence.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏫 The School Database in This Book\r\n\r\nEvery chapter uses one running example, a database called `school_db`. It has five tables:\r\n\r\n| Table | What one row means |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `classes` | One class, such as Grade 8 Eagles, and its teacher |\r\n| `students` | One student: name, grade, GPA, class and city |\r\n| `books` | One library book: title, category, pages |\r\n| `loans` | One time a student **borrowed a library book** |\r\n| `donations` | One gift to a school charity drive |\r\n\r\nYou will build these tables yourself in Chapters 4–8. The same tables are already loaded in the site's [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), so you can practise any time.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤲 Beneficial Knowledge & Digital Stewardship (*'Ilm Nāfi' & Amānah*)\r\n\r\nStoring people's data is not only a technical skill. It is a moral duty. In Islamic ethics, entrusted data is an **Amānah** (a trust you must look after).\r\n\r\n1. **Truthfulness (*Ṣidq*)**: A database must hold true records. Never change grades or numbers to mislead anyone.\r\n2. **Privacy**: People trust us with their names and contact details. We never leak or sell them, and we never leave a database open without a password.\r\n3. **Store only what you need**: If the library only needs a student's name and class, do not also collect their home address.\r\n4. **Beneficial purpose**: Use databases to help your community: school records, hospital schedules, charity and Zakat trackers.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: \"A spreadsheet file is the same as a database.\"**\r\n\r\nA student keeps the library list in `books.xlsx` and shares it with five helpers. Two helpers edit it at once. One helper's changes disappear, and nobody notices.\r\n\r\n**Better:** keep shared, important records in a database. The server handles many users safely, and column rules stop wrong data from getting in. Use a spreadsheet for small personal lists.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOpen the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/). Find the list of tables on the page. Write down the five table names and guess two columns for each one.\r\n\r\n*Hint: the names match the table in \"The School Database in This Book\" above.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **database** is an organised, permanent store of data, run by server software.\r\n- Databases beat spreadsheets for size, many users, correct data and privacy.\r\n- An **RDBMS** keeps data in linked **tables**. MySQL and MariaDB are popular examples.\r\n- **SQL** is the language we use to ask a database questions.\r\n- Stored data is an *Amānah*: keep it true, private and as small as needed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does **RDBMS** stand for?\r\n2. What language do we use to talk to a MySQL database?\r\n3. Name two reasons a database is better than a spreadsheet for a web app.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. **Relational Database Management System**.\r\n2. **SQL** (Structured Query Language).\r\n3. Any two: it stays fast with very many rows; many users can edit safely at once; column rules stop wrong data; each user can get only the access they need; tables link cleanly.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA school app stores each student's name, class and parent's phone number. Explain two ways the idea of *Amānah* (trust) should guide how the developer designs this database.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nAny two, for example:\r\n\r\n- **Privacy:** protect the database with a password and give each user only the access they need, so phone numbers cannot leak.\r\n- **Store only what is needed:** do not collect extra details such as home addresses if the app does not use them.\r\n- **Truthfulness:** keep records accurate and never change them to mislead.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA community health clinic keeps patient appointments and its medicine stock in one Excel file on the front-desk laptop.\r\n\r\n1. List three risks of this set-up.\r\n2. For each risk, say how a MySQL database would help.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n| Risk with the Excel file | How a database helps |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| The laptop breaks or is stolen, and the data is gone. | The data lives on a server that can be backed up. |\r\n| Two staff edit at once and overwrite each other. | The server handles many users safely. |\r\n| Anyone who opens the file sees every patient. | Each user gets only the access they need. |\r\n| Someone types \"ten\" in the stock column. | Column types reject wrong data. |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch02-tables-rows-and-columns.md": "# Chapter 2: Tables, Rows, and Columns\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: A database keeps data in tables. A table is about one kind of thing (like students). Each column is one fact about it (like name). Each row is one real thing (like one student).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nThink of the class register your teacher fills in each morning. Across the top are headings: roll number, name, present or absent. Down the page, each line is one student. Nobody writes two students on one line, and nobody adds a new heading for each new child. A database table works exactly like that register.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Name the parts of a table: table, column, row and value.\r\n- Read a real table from the school database.\r\n- Decide whether new data needs a new row or a new column.\r\n- Apply three golden rules of good table design.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧩 The Parts of a Table\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/table-anatomy-records.svg\" alt=\"Table Anatomy: Fields, Records & Types\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nHere is the `students` table from this book's `school_db` database:\r\n\r\n| id | name | grade | gpa | class_id | city |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Zayd | 8 | 3.90 | 2 | Lahore |\r\n| 2 | Maryam | 7 | 3.75 | 1 | Karachi |\r\n| 3 | Bilal | 9 | 3.95 | 3 | Lahore |\r\n| 4 | Fatima | 8 | 3.85 | 2 | Islamabad |\r\n| 5 | Tariq | 9 | 3.60 | 3 | Karachi |\r\n| 6 | Aisha | 7 | 3.40 | 1 | Lahore |\r\n| 7 | Hamza | 8 | 3.20 | 2 | Peshawar |\r\n| 8 | Khadija | 9 | 3.70 | 3 | Islamabad |\r\n\r\n(`gpa` means *grade point average*, a score from 0 to 4. `class_id` says which class the student is in.)\r\n\r\n```\r\n          columns (fields)\r\n     ┌──────┬────────┬───────┐\r\n     │ id   │ name   │ grade │\r\n     ├──────┼────────┼───────┤\r\nrow →│ 1    │ Zayd   │ 8     │\r\nrow →│ 2    │ Maryam │ 7     │\r\n     └──────┴────────┴───────┘\r\n                ▲\r\n         one value (cell)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Key words\r\n\r\n1. **Table**: a grid of data about one kind of thing, such as `students` or `books`. Some books call it an **entity**.\r\n2. **Column** (or **field**): one fact that every row has, such as `name` or `city`. Every value in a column has the same type. The `grade` column holds only whole numbers.\r\n3. **Row** (or **record**): one real thing. Row 3 holds everything about Bilal.\r\n4. **Value** (or **cell**): one piece of data where a row meets a column. Bilal's city is `'Lahore'`.\r\n\r\nThe table above has **6 columns** and **8 rows**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📚 A Second Example: The Library Books\r\n\r\nThe `books` table describes the school library:\r\n\r\n| id | title | category | pages | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Clean Code | Programming | 464 | Yes |\r\n| 2 | Algorithm Design | Computer Science | 320 | Yes |\r\n| 3 | Ethical Technology | Ethics | 210 | No |\r\n| 4 | HTML & CSS Architecture | Web | 288 | Yes |\r\n| 5 | Python Data Science | Data | 352 | No |\r\n| 6 | Digital Stewardship | Ethics | 180 | Yes |\r\n| 7 | Introduction to Networks | Computer Science | 240 | Yes |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics | Art | 120 | Yes |\r\n\r\nNotice the pattern:\r\n\r\n- **Columns** say *what we know* about every book: title, category, pages, available.\r\n- **Rows** are *the books themselves*, one per shelf copy.\r\n- If the library buys 1,000 more books, we add 1,000 new **rows**. We do **not** add new columns.\r\n\r\nNew columns are only for a new *kind* of fact, for example if the library starts recording each book's language.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📏 The Three Golden Rules of Table Design\r\n\r\n### Rule 1: One table = one kind of thing\r\nDo not mix unrelated facts in one table.\r\n\r\n- ❌ One giant table with students, their borrowed books and their teachers' phone numbers.\r\n- ✅ Separate tables: `students`, `books`, `classes`, and a `loans` table that records each time a student borrows a book.\r\n\r\n### Rule 2: One value per cell\r\nEach cell holds one single piece of information. This is called an **atomic** value (it cannot be split further).\r\n\r\n- ❌ A `borrowed_books` cell holding `'Clean Code, Algorithm Design'`.\r\n- ✅ One row per borrowing in the `loans` table:\r\n\r\n| id | student_id | book_id |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| 1 | 1 | 1 |\r\n| 2 | 1 | 2 |\r\n\r\nBoth rows belong to student 1 (Zayd), one for each book.\r\n\r\n### Rule 3: Every row can be told apart\r\nTwo students can share the same name. So every table gets a column with a unique number, usually called `id`. This is the **primary key**, like a student ID card. You will set one up in Chapter 7.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: adding a column for every new item.**\r\n\r\n```\r\nbooks table (wrong)\r\n┌────┬──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐\r\n│ id │ book1    │ book2    │ book3    │\r\n└────┴──────────┴──────────┴──────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: the library buys a fourth book and the table has no room. You must change the table's design each time, and searching becomes very hard.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** one column per *kind of fact* (`title`, `category`, `pages`) and one **row** per book. The table never needs to grow sideways.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOpen the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/) and look at the `donations` table. Answer:\r\n\r\n1. How many columns does it have? Name them.\r\n2. How many rows?\r\n3. What is the value in the `cause` column of row 2?\r\n\r\n*Hint: in the sandbox, type `SELECT * FROM donations;` and press Run. You will learn exactly what this means in Chapter 9.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **table** stores data about one kind of thing.\r\n- A **column** is one fact every row has; all its values share one type.\r\n- A **row** is one real thing, such as one student or one book.\r\n- More things = more rows, not more columns.\r\n- Good tables follow three rules: one kind of thing, one value per cell, a unique `id` for every row.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is the difference between a **column** and a **row**?\r\n2. A school enrols 250 new students. Does `students` gain 250 new columns or 250 new rows?\r\n3. Why do we need an `id` column when students already have names?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A column is one fact that every row has (like `city`). A row is one complete record (like everything about Bilal).\r\n2. **250 new rows.**\r\n3. Two students can have the same name. The `id` is unique, so the computer never mixes them up.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA community honey farm wants an `inventory` table. It must track the product name, jar size in grams, selling price, and jars in stock. Write the column names you would use and give one example row.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n| id | product_name | jar_grams | price | jars_in_stock |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| 1 | Wildflower Honey | 500 | 950.00 | 40 |\r\n\r\nAny clear names are fine, as long as there is an `id` and one column per fact.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA junior coder made a table called `library_users` with this column:\r\n\r\n`borrowed_books: \"Quran Stories, Math Class 9, Web Design\"`\r\n\r\n1. Which golden rule does this break?\r\n2. Describe a better design using separate tables.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It breaks **Rule 2: one value per cell**. Three books are squeezed into one cell. It also mixes two kinds of thing (users and books), breaking Rule 1.\r\n2. Better design:\r\n   - `students` (id, name, ...) — one row per student.\r\n   - `books` (id, title, ...) — one row per book.\r\n   - `loans` (id, student_id, book_id, loan_date) — one row each time a student borrows a book.\r\n\r\nNow a student who borrows three books simply has three rows in `loans`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch03-setting-up-mysql.md": "# Chapter 3: Setting Up MySQL & phpMyAdmin\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: MySQL runs quietly in the background as a server. You talk to it in two ways: with a web page called phpMyAdmin, or by typing commands in a terminal.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA school library has a librarian who sits behind the desk all day. Students do not walk into the store room themselves. They come to the desk and ask: \"Do you have *Clean Code*?\" The librarian fetches the answer. The MySQL server is that librarian. It waits all day for questions, and phpMyAdmin or the terminal is the desk where you ask them.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Start the MySQL server from the XAMPP Control Panel.\r\n- Open phpMyAdmin and find its SQL tab.\r\n- Log in to MySQL from the command line.\r\n- Run your first three SQL commands and read their results.\r\n- Fix the most common start-up problem: a busy port.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🟢 Starting MySQL in XAMPP\r\n\r\n**XAMPP** is a free package that installs a web server (Apache), PHP and a database server on your computer. The database server in XAMPP is **MariaDB**, MySQL's close cousin. It understands the same SQL, so this book calls it \"MySQL\".\r\n\r\n```\r\nXAMPP Control Panel\r\n┌─────────┬─────────┬────────┬───────┐\r\n│ Module  │ Status  │ Action │ Port  │\r\n├─────────┼─────────┼────────┼───────┤\r\n│ Apache  │ Running │ Stop   │ 80    │\r\n│ MySQL   │ Running │ Stop   │ 3306  │\r\n└─────────┴─────────┴────────┴───────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nSteps:\r\n\r\n1. Open the **XAMPP Control Panel** from the Start menu.\r\n2. Click **Start** next to **Apache** (phpMyAdmin needs it).\r\n3. Click **Start** next to **MySQL**.\r\n4. The name turns green and shows port **3306**.\r\n\r\nA **port** is a numbered door on your computer. Each server program listens at its own door. MySQL's usual door is 3306.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🖥️ Using phpMyAdmin\r\n\r\n**phpMyAdmin** is a web page for managing MySQL with buttons and forms.\r\n\r\n1. Open your browser and go to `http://localhost/phpmyadmin`\r\n2. On the **left** you see a tree of databases.\r\n3. Along the **top** are tabs: **Databases**, **SQL**, **Status**, **Export**, **Import** and more.\r\n4. The **SQL** tab has a big text box. Type a command and press **Go**.\r\n\r\n```\r\nphpMyAdmin\r\n┌──────────────────────────────────────┐\r\n│ Databases  SQL  Export  Import       │\r\n├────────────┬─────────────────────────┤\r\n│ school_db  │ SELECT NOW();           │\r\n│  ├ books   │                         │\r\n│  └ students│                  [ Go ] │\r\n└────────────┴─────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nThroughout this book, **\"run this in phpMyAdmin\"** means: open the SQL tab, paste the command, press Go.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⌨️ Using the Command Line\r\n\r\nThe **command line** (or **CLI**, command-line interface) is a text window where you type commands. Many developers prefer it because it is fast.\r\n\r\n1. In the XAMPP Control Panel, click **Shell** on the right.\r\n2. Type this and press **Enter**:\r\n\r\n   ```bash\r\n   mysql -u root -p\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n   `-u root` means \"log in as the user called root\". `-p` means \"ask me for a password\".\r\n3. At `Enter password:` just press **Enter**. A fresh XAMPP has no password for `root`.\r\n4. You now see the MySQL prompt:\r\n\r\n   ```\r\n   MariaDB [(none)]>\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n   `(none)` means you have not picked a database yet. You will do that in Chapter 4.\r\n\r\nTo leave, type `exit` and press Enter.\r\n\r\n> 🔐 **Amānah note:** \"root with no password\" is only safe on your own practice computer. A real server must always have a strong password.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧪 Your First SQL Commands\r\n\r\nType each command and press Enter. Every SQL **statement** (one complete command) ends with a semicolon `;`.\r\n\r\n### ➗ Do a sum\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT 7 * 6;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| 7 * 6 |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 42 |\r\n\r\n`SELECT` asks the server to give something back. Here it is just a calculation.\r\n\r\n### 👤 Who am I?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT CURRENT_USER();\r\n```\r\n\r\n| CURRENT_USER() |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| root@localhost |\r\n\r\nYou are the `root` user, connected from this same computer (`localhost`).\r\n\r\n### 📚 Which databases exist?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSHOW DATABASES;\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn a fresh XAMPP you will see something like:\r\n\r\n| Database |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| information_schema |\r\n| mysql |\r\n| performance_schema |\r\n| phpmyadmin |\r\n| test |\r\n\r\nThese are **system databases** the server uses for itself. Do not delete them. Your own `school_db` will join this list in the next chapter.\r\n\r\nYou can also try `SELECT NOW();`. It shows the server's current date and time, so your answer will be different from anyone else's.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** The site's SQL Sandbox uses SQLite, not MySQL. `SELECT 7 * 6;` works there, but `SHOW DATABASES` and `CURRENT_USER()` do not.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: forgetting the semicolon.**\r\n\r\n```\r\nMariaDB [(none)]> SELECT 7 * 6\r\n    ->\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: MySQL shows `->` and waits. It thinks your command continues on the next line.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** type `;` and press Enter. The command runs.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: \"Port 3306 in use by another program.\"**\r\n\r\nWhat happens: MySQL turns red and stops. Another program is already using door 3306. Often it is a second copy of MySQL installed earlier.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** close the other database program (or stop its Windows service). Then press Start again.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\n1. Start Apache and MySQL in XAMPP.\r\n2. Open phpMyAdmin, click the **SQL** tab, and run `SELECT 12 * 12;`\r\n3. Then run `SHOW DATABASES;` and count the databases.\r\n\r\n*Hint: if the page does not load, check that Apache is green, not only MySQL.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- XAMPP's database server is MariaDB, which speaks the same SQL as MySQL.\r\n- Start it in the XAMPP Control Panel; it listens on port 3306.\r\n- phpMyAdmin (`http://localhost/phpmyadmin`) lets you run SQL in a browser.\r\n- `mysql -u root -p` opens the command line.\r\n- Every SQL statement ends with `;`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What port does MySQL use by default?\r\n2. What address opens phpMyAdmin in XAMPP?\r\n3. What must end every SQL statement?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. **3306**\r\n2. `http://localhost/phpmyadmin`\r\n3. A semicolon `;`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nLog in with the command line and run `SHOW DATABASES;`. Name three system databases you see. What does the prompt `MariaDB [(none)]>` tell you?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nAny three of: `information_schema`, `mysql`, `performance_schema`, `phpmyadmin`, `test`.\r\n\r\n`(none)` means no database is selected yet. The prompt shows the current database inside the square brackets.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA student clicks **Start** next to MySQL. It turns red and the log says *\"Port 3306 in use by another program\"*.\r\n\r\n1. Explain in one sentence what this means.\r\n2. List two things the student could check.\r\n3. Why should a real school server never keep \"root with no password\"?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Another program is already listening on port 3306, so MySQL cannot use it.\r\n2. Check whether another MySQL or MariaDB is installed and running (for example, as a Windows service), and stop it. Or check Task Manager for a running `mysqld.exe` and close it.\r\n3. Anyone who can reach the server could read or delete every record. Student data is an *Amānah*, so it must be protected with a strong password.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-creating-databases-and-tables/ch04-create-database.md": "# Chapter 4: CREATE DATABASE and USE\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Before you make tables, you make a database to hold them. You create it with `CREATE DATABASE`, choose it with `USE`, and give it the `utf8mb4` character set so every language fits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA school has one big building but many rooms. The science lab keeps its equipment in its own room. The library keeps its books in another. Nobody mixes test tubes with storybooks. A MySQL server is the building, and each **database** is a separate room for one app's tables.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a database \"container\" is.\r\n- Create a database safely with `CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS`.\r\n- Choose the `utf8mb4` character set for Urdu, Arabic and emoji.\r\n- Switch into a database with `USE` and check it with `SELECT DATABASE()`.\r\n- Delete a database with `DROP DATABASE`, and know why this is dangerous.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📁 What is a Database Container?\r\n\r\nOne MySQL server can hold many separate apps. Each app keeps its tables inside its own **database**. MySQL also calls a database a **schema**.\r\n\r\n```\r\nMySQL server (localhost)\r\n├── school_db\r\n│    └ classes, students, books,\r\n│      loans, donations\r\n├── clinic_db\r\n│    └ patients, appointments\r\n└── charity_db\r\n     └ donors, campaigns\r\n```\r\n\r\nTables in `school_db` cannot be confused with tables in `clinic_db`, even if both have a table called `patients` or `students`.\r\n\r\nIn this book we build one database: **`school_db`**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ Creating a Database\r\n\r\nThe command is short:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL answers:\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 1 row affected\r\n```\r\n\r\n### What if it already exists?\r\n\r\nRun the same command a second time and you get an error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1007 (HY000): Can't create database 'school_db'; database exists\r\n```\r\n\r\nTo avoid this, add `IF NOT EXISTS`. MySQL then skips the work quietly if the database is already there:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 1 row affected, 1 warning\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe warning only says \"it already existed\". Nothing was changed. This makes a set-up script safe to run many times.\r\n\r\n### Choose a character set: `utf8mb4`\r\n\r\nA **character set** is the list of letters and symbols a database can store. A **collation** is the rule for comparing and sorting them (for example, is \"a\" equal to \"A\"?).\r\n\r\nOlder set-ups default to `latin1`, which cannot store Urdu, Arabic or emoji. Always choose `utf8mb4`. It covers every language and emoji (🌟):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS school_db\r\n  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4\r\n  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n```\r\n\r\n`_ci` at the end means **case-insensitive**: `'zayd'` and `'Zayd'` are treated as equal when you search.\r\n\r\nYou can check what the server stored:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSHOW CREATE DATABASE school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Database | Create Database |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| school_db | CREATE DATABASE \\`school_db\\` /\\*!40100 DEFAULT CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci \\*/ |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👉 Choosing a Database with `USE`\r\n\r\nWhen you log in, no database is chosen yet. Ask MySQL which one is active:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DATABASE();\r\n```\r\n\r\n| DATABASE() |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| NULL |\r\n\r\n`NULL` means \"nothing\", so no database is selected. Now choose one:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUSE school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nDatabase changed\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck again:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DATABASE();\r\n```\r\n\r\n| DATABASE() |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| school_db |\r\n\r\nFrom now on, every `CREATE TABLE` or `SELECT` you type works inside `school_db`. The command-line prompt also changes from `MariaDB [(none)]>` to `MariaDB [school_db]>`.\r\n\r\nIn phpMyAdmin you can simply click `school_db` in the left tree. That does the same job as `USE`.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** The SQL Sandbox (SQLite) has one database already open, so `CREATE DATABASE` and `USE` do not exist there. Run them in phpMyAdmin.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗑️ Deleting a Database with `DROP DATABASE`\r\n\r\n`DROP` means \"throw away\". This deletes the database **and every table and row inside it**:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDROP DATABASE IF EXISTS practice_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is no Recycle Bin. Once dropped, the data is gone unless you made a **backup** (a saved copy, for example with phpMyAdmin's **Export** tab).\r\n\r\n`IF EXISTS` stops the error you would get if the database is not there.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📋 Command Summary\r\n\r\n| Command | What it does |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `SHOW DATABASES;` | Lists all databases on the server. |\r\n| `CREATE DATABASE name;` | Makes a new, empty database. |\r\n| `USE name;` | Makes that database the active one. |\r\n| `SELECT DATABASE();` | Shows which database is active. |\r\n| `DROP DATABASE name;` | Deletes the database and everything in it. |\r\n\r\nThese commands belong to **DDL** (Data Definition Language): SQL that builds or removes structures, not data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: making tables before choosing a database.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE classes (id INT, name VARCHAR(50));\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens:\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1046 (3D000): No database selected\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL does not know which \"room\" to put the table in.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUSE school_db;\r\nCREATE TABLE classes (id INT, name VARCHAR(50));\r\n```\r\n\r\n*(You will learn `CREATE TABLE` properly in Chapter 5.)*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn phpMyAdmin's **SQL** tab (with no database selected), run:\r\n\r\n1. `CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS school_db CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;`\r\n2. `SHOW DATABASES;` — is `school_db` in the list?\r\n3. Run step 1 again. What message appears this time?\r\n\r\n*Hint: keep `school_db`. Chapter 5 builds its tables.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A database is a separate container for one app's tables.\r\n- `CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS` makes one without failing if it is already there.\r\n- Use `utf8mb4` so every language and emoji can be stored.\r\n- `USE name;` chooses the active database; `SELECT DATABASE();` shows it.\r\n- `DROP DATABASE` deletes everything inside, with no undo.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which command tells MySQL which database to work in?\r\n2. What happens if you run `CREATE DATABASE school_db;` when it already exists?\r\n3. Why is `utf8mb4` better than `latin1`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `USE database_name;`\r\n2. You get `ERROR 1007 ... database exists`. With `IF NOT EXISTS` you get only a warning.\r\n3. `utf8mb4` stores every language, including Urdu and Arabic, plus emoji. `latin1` stores only Western European letters.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite the SQL to:\r\n1. Create a database called `community_library_db` with `utf8mb4` if it does not exist.\r\n2. Make it the active database.\r\n3. Check that it is active.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS community_library_db\r\n  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4\r\n  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n\r\nUSE community_library_db;\r\n\r\nSELECT DATABASE();\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last command returns `community_library_db`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA school's IT team does not let the website or junior staff run `DROP DATABASE`.\r\n\r\n1. Explain why, using the word *Amānah*.\r\n2. Write a safe two-line script that removes an old `practice_db` only if it exists, then creates a fresh one.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `DROP DATABASE` deletes every record at once, with no undo. Student and library records are a trust (*Amānah*). One mistake or one attack through the website could destroy them. So only a few trusted people get that power, and they make a backup first.\r\n2.\r\n```sql\r\nDROP DATABASE IF EXISTS practice_db;\r\nCREATE DATABASE practice_db CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-creating-databases-and-tables/ch05-create-table.md": "# Chapter 5: CREATE TABLE and Schema Design\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `CREATE TABLE` draws the plan for a table: its column names, the kind of data each holds, and a few rules that keep the data clean.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nBefore builders lay a single brick, an architect draws a plan. The plan says where each room goes and how big it is. Nobody starts pouring concrete and \"sees what happens\". A database table needs a plan too. In SQL, that plan is written with `CREATE TABLE`.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a schema is.\r\n- Write a `CREATE TABLE` statement with columns, types and rules.\r\n- Build the five tables of `school_db`.\r\n- Check your work with `SHOW TABLES` and `DESCRIBE`.\r\n- Rename or delete a table.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📐 What is a Schema?\r\n\r\nA **schema** is the plan of a database: which tables exist, which columns each has, and what rules the data must follow.\r\n\r\nWhen you write `CREATE TABLE`, you tell MySQL three things:\r\n\r\n1. **The table's name**, for example `students`.\r\n2. **Its columns**, for example `name`, `grade`, `city`.\r\n3. **The rules**, for example \"a name can never be empty\".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✏️ The Shape of `CREATE TABLE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE table_name (\r\n    column_1  TYPE  rules,\r\n    column_2  TYPE  rules,\r\n    column_3  TYPE  rules\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Columns go inside round brackets `( )`.\r\n- A comma separates each column from the next.\r\n- **No comma** after the last column.\r\n- A semicolon ends the statement.\r\n\r\nA **data type** (or **type**) says what kind of value a column holds. You will meet the full list in Chapter 6. For now, four are enough:\r\n\r\n| Type | Holds | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `INT` | whole numbers | `8` |\r\n| `VARCHAR(50)` | text up to 50 characters | `'Lahore'` |\r\n| `DECIMAL(3,2)` | numbers with 2 decimal places | `3.95` |\r\n| `DATE` | a calendar date | `'2026-09-02'` |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏫 Building `school_db`, Step by Step\r\n\r\nFirst choose the database from Chapter 4:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUSE school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Table 1: `classes`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE classes (\r\n    id      INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    name    VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    teacher VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nRead each line:\r\n\r\n- `id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY` — a whole number. `AUTO_INCREMENT` means MySQL fills it in for you: 1, 2, 3... `PRIMARY KEY` means it is unique for every row, like an ID card. Chapter 7 explains both in depth.\r\n- `name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL` — text up to 50 characters. `NOT NULL` means it can never be left empty.\r\n- `teacher VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL` — the class teacher's name, also required.\r\n\r\n### Table 2: `students`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE students (\r\n    id       INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    name     VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    grade    INT,\r\n    gpa      DECIMAL(3,2),\r\n    class_id INT,\r\n    city     VARCHAR(50)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n`grade`, `gpa`, `class_id` and `city` have no `NOT NULL`, so they may be left empty. `class_id` will hold the `id` of the student's class. Chapter 15 turns it into a proper link.\r\n\r\n### Tables 3–5: `books`, `loans`, `donations`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE books (\r\n    id        INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    title     VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    category  VARCHAR(50),\r\n    pages     INT,\r\n    available VARCHAR(3)\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE loans (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    student_id INT,\r\n    book_id    INT,\r\n    loan_date  DATE,\r\n    returned   VARCHAR(3)\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE donations (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    donor      VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    amount     INT NOT NULL,\r\n    cause      VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    donated_on DATE\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nA row in `loans` means **a student borrowed a library book**. `available` and `returned` hold the text `'Yes'` or `'No'`.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** The SQL Sandbox (SQLite) already has these five tables. SQLite is relaxed about types and writes `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` instead of `INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Checking Your Work: `SHOW TABLES` and `DESCRIBE`\r\n\r\nList the tables in the active database:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSHOW TABLES;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Tables_in_school_db |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| books |\r\n| classes |\r\n| donations |\r\n| loans |\r\n| students |\r\n\r\nLook inside one table's plan:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDESCRIBE students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Type | Null | Key | Default | Extra |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| id | int(11) | NO | PRI | NULL | auto_increment |\r\n| name | varchar(50) | NO | | NULL | |\r\n| grade | int(11) | YES | | NULL | |\r\n| gpa | decimal(3,2) | YES | | NULL | |\r\n| class_id | int(11) | YES | | NULL | |\r\n| city | varchar(50) | YES | | NULL | |\r\n\r\nHow to read it:\r\n\r\n- **Null = NO** means the column is required (`NOT NULL`).\r\n- **Key = PRI** marks the primary key.\r\n- **Extra = auto_increment** shows MySQL fills in the number.\r\n- `int(11)` is just how MariaDB displays `INT`. The 11 is a display width, not a limit.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Renaming or Deleting a Table\r\n\r\nRename a table:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nRENAME TABLE classes TO school_classes;\r\n```\r\n\r\nDelete a table and all its rows (no undo!):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS old_practice_table;\r\n```\r\n\r\n`IF EXISTS` avoids an error if the table is not there. You can also write `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ...` so a script does not fail when the table already exists.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: a missing comma between columns.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE books (\r\n    id    INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    title VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL\r\n    category VARCHAR(50)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens:\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1064 (42000): You have an error in your SQL syntax; ...\r\nnear 'VARCHAR(50))'\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL reads `NOT NULL category` as one broken phrase. **Fix:** put a comma after `NOT NULL`.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: creating a table twice.**\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1050 (42S01): Table 'classes' already exists\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS classes (...)`, or drop the old one first if you really want to start over.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn phpMyAdmin, select `school_db` and open the **SQL** tab. Type (do not paste) the five `CREATE TABLE` statements above. Then run `SHOW TABLES;` and `DESCRIBE books;`.\r\n\r\n*Hint: if you get error 1064, look just before the place it quotes. A comma is usually missing there.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **schema** is the plan of the database's tables and rules.\r\n- `CREATE TABLE name ( column TYPE rules, ... );` builds a table.\r\n- Columns are separated by commas, with none after the last one.\r\n- `NOT NULL` makes a column required; `PRIMARY KEY` makes each row unique; `AUTO_INCREMENT` numbers rows for you.\r\n- `SHOW TABLES` lists tables; `DESCRIBE` shows one table's columns.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which command shows the columns and types of a table?\r\n2. What does `NOT NULL` do?\r\n3. What does `AUTO_INCREMENT` do when a new row is added?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `DESCRIBE table_name;`\r\n2. It makes the column required: the value can never be empty (`NULL`).\r\n3. MySQL gives the new row the next number automatically (1, 2, 3...).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite a `CREATE TABLE` for a `science_fair_projects` table:\r\n- `id`: whole number, auto-numbered, primary key\r\n- `title`: text up to 150 characters, required\r\n- `student_name`: text up to 50 characters, required\r\n- `category`: text up to 30 characters\r\n- `fair_date`: a date\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE science_fair_projects (\r\n    id           INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    title        VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL,\r\n    student_name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    category     VARCHAR(30),\r\n    fair_date    DATE\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe school starts a reading club. Design and create a `club_members` table.\r\n\r\n1. Decide on at least four columns (one must be the primary key).\r\n2. Choose a type for each.\r\n3. Decide which columns must be `NOT NULL`.\r\n4. Write the `CREATE TABLE`, run it, and check it with `DESCRIBE`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE club_members (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    student_id INT NOT NULL,\r\n    joined_on  DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    role       VARCHAR(30)\r\n);\r\n\r\nDESCRIBE club_members;\r\n```\r\n\r\n`student_id` stores the member's id from `students`, so we do not copy their name again. `role` (for example \"Book reviewer\") is optional. Notice we did not store phone numbers: we only keep what the club needs (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-creating-databases-and-tables/ch06-data-types.md": "# Chapter 6: SQL Data Types\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Every column has a data type. The right type saves space, keeps wrong data out, and makes sums and dates work correctly.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA school office has different boxes for different things. Exam papers go in the paper tray. Coins for the charity drive go in a locked coin box. Library cards go in a card file. If someone dropped coins into the paper tray, counting the money would be a mess. Data types are the database's labelled boxes: numbers go with numbers, dates with dates, text with text.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain why each column needs a data type.\r\n- Choose between `INT`, `DECIMAL`, `FLOAT`, `VARCHAR`, `CHAR`, `TEXT`, `DATE` and `BOOLEAN`.\r\n- Explain why money must use `DECIMAL`, not `FLOAT`.\r\n- Understand why each `school_db` column has the type it has.\r\n- Turn on strict mode so MySQL rejects wrong data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ❓ Why Data Types Matter\r\n\r\nA computer treats `15` (a number) and `'15'` (text) differently.\r\n\r\n- Store prices as text and you cannot add them up properly.\r\n- Store dates as words like `'next Tuesday'` and you cannot sort them by time.\r\n\r\nWith a data type, MySQL checks every value that goes into the column:\r\n\r\n```\r\nColumn: age INT\r\n15          → accepted\r\n'Fifteen'   → rejected (or turned into 0!)\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔢 Family 1: Number Types\r\n\r\n| Type | What it holds | Range or size | Good for |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `INT` | whole numbers | about −2.1 billion to +2.1 billion | ids, page counts, grades |\r\n| `BIGINT` | very large whole numbers | about ±9 × 10¹⁸ | huge counters |\r\n| `DECIMAL(M,D)` | **exact** decimals | M digits in total, D after the point | money, GPA, marks |\r\n| `FLOAT` / `DOUBLE` | **approximate** decimals | very large or tiny | science readings |\r\n\r\n`DECIMAL(3,2)` means 3 digits in total, 2 of them after the point. So the largest value is `9.99`. That is perfect for a GPA from 0 to 4.\r\n\r\n### Why money needs `DECIMAL`\r\n\r\n`FLOAT` and `DOUBLE` store numbers in binary (base 2). Many decimals, such as 0.1, cannot be stored exactly in binary. Tiny errors creep in. Here we store 0.1 in a `DOUBLE` column, then add 0.2:\r\n\r\n| dbl |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 0.30000000000000004 |\r\n\r\nAnd `1234567.89` stored in a `FLOAT` column comes back as:\r\n\r\n| f (FLOAT) | d (DECIMAL(10,2)) |\r\n| ---: | ---: |\r\n| 1234570 | 1234567.89 |\r\n\r\nThe `FLOAT` lost the paise! For charity donations, fees or Zakat, fairness means being exact. Always use `DECIMAL(10,2)` for money (up to 99,999,999.99).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔤 Family 2: Text Types\r\n\r\n| Type | What it holds | Good for |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `VARCHAR(N)` | text up to N characters; uses only the space needed | names, titles, cities |\r\n| `CHAR(N)` | text of exactly N characters | codes like `'PK'` or `'IN'` |\r\n| `TEXT` | long text, up to about 64 KB | a book summary |\r\n| `LONGTEXT` | very long text, up to 4 GB | a whole e-book |\r\n\r\n`VARCHAR(50)` storing `'Aisha'` uses only 5 characters of space plus a tiny length marker. `CHAR(50)` would always reserve the full 50.\r\n\r\n> 💡 **Phone numbers are text, not numbers.** You never add phone numbers together, and they can start with `0` or `+`. Use `VARCHAR(20)`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📅 Family 3: Date and Time Types\r\n\r\n| Type | Format | Good for |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `DATE` | `YYYY-MM-DD` | borrowing date, birthday |\r\n| `TIME` | `HH:MM:SS` | class start time |\r\n| `DATETIME` | `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` | exact moment of an event |\r\n| `TIMESTAMP` | same look, stored in UTC | when a row was created or changed |\r\n\r\nDates are written year first: `'2026-09-02'` means 2 September 2026. This order sorts correctly from oldest to newest.\r\n\r\nA common pattern lets MySQL fill in the time for you:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\ncreated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✅ Family 4: True or False (`BOOLEAN`)\r\n\r\n`BOOLEAN` (or `BOOL`) holds true or false. MySQL really stores it as a tiny number: `1` for TRUE and `0` for FALSE. If you insert `TRUE` and read it back, you see `1`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏫 The Types in `school_db`\r\n\r\nHere is why Chapter 5 chose each type:\r\n\r\n| Column | Type | Why |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `students.name` | `VARCHAR(50)` | names vary in length |\r\n| `students.grade` | `INT` | a whole number like 8 |\r\n| `students.gpa` | `DECIMAL(3,2)` | exact, like 3.95; max 9.99 |\r\n| `books.pages` | `INT` | whole number |\r\n| `books.available` | `VARCHAR(3)` | holds `'Yes'` or `'No'` |\r\n| `loans.loan_date` | `DATE` | can be sorted by time |\r\n| `donations.amount` | `INT` | whole rupees only |\r\n\r\n`available` could also be a `BOOLEAN`. We kept `'Yes'`/`'No'` text because it is easy to read for beginners. Both are fine; just choose one and stay consistent.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite (the sandbox) is relaxed about types. It will store `'Fifteen'` in a number column without complaint. MySQL checks types; SQLite mostly does not.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: trusting MySQL to always reject bad data.**\r\n\r\nA standard XAMPP install runs MariaDB in a relaxed mode. Watch what happens:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO t (age) VALUES ('Fifteen');\r\nINSERT INTO t (gpa) VALUES (12.5);      -- gpa is DECIMAL(3,2)\r\nINSERT INTO t (dt)  VALUES ('next Tuesday');\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo error appears! Only a warning. The stored values are:\r\n\r\n| age | gpa | dt |\r\n| ---: | ---: | :--- |\r\n| 0 | 9.99 | 0000-00-00 |\r\n\r\n`'Fifteen'` became `0`, `12.5` was cut down to `9.99`, and the date became zeros. That is wrong data, silently saved.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** turn on **strict mode** for your session:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSET SESSION sql_mode = 'STRICT_TRANS_TABLES';\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow the same insert stops with a clear error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1366 (22007): Incorrect integer value: 'Fifteen' for column ... `age`\r\n```\r\n\r\nMost hosting servers already use strict mode. Turn it on while you practise so you see your mistakes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn phpMyAdmin (database `school_db`), run `DESCRIBE donations;`. Then answer:\r\n\r\n1. Which column would you change to `DECIMAL(10,2)` if donors could give 1500.50 rupees?\r\n2. Which type is `donated_on`, and why is that better than `VARCHAR`?\r\n\r\n*Hint: think about which column holds money and which column you would sort by time.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A data type says what kind of value a column can hold.\r\n- Use `INT` for whole numbers and `DECIMAL(M,D)` for exact decimals and money.\r\n- `FLOAT`/`DOUBLE` are approximate; never use them for money.\r\n- Use `VARCHAR` for most text, including phone numbers; `DATE` for dates written `YYYY-MM-DD`.\r\n- XAMPP is relaxed by default; strict mode makes MySQL reject wrong data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Why is `DECIMAL(10,2)` better than `FLOAT` for donation amounts?\r\n2. In what format does MySQL expect a `DATE`?\r\n3. What is the difference between `VARCHAR(50)` and `CHAR(50)`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `DECIMAL` is exact. `FLOAT` is approximate, so amounts can come back slightly wrong (for example 1234567.89 became 1234570).\r\n2. `YYYY-MM-DD`, for example `'2026-09-02'`.\r\n3. `VARCHAR(50)` uses only the space the text needs, up to 50 characters. `CHAR(50)` always takes the full 50.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nChoose the best type for each value:\r\n1. A student's birth date: `2012-04-15`\r\n2. A phone number: `+92-300-1234567`\r\n3. Whether a book is on the shelf: true/false\r\n4. A textbook price in rupees: `1450.75`\r\n5. The full text of a long encyclopedia article\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `DATE`\r\n2. `VARCHAR(20)` (it has `+` and dashes, and is never used in sums)\r\n3. `BOOLEAN` (or `VARCHAR(3)` holding `'Yes'`/`'No'`, as in `school_db`)\r\n4. `DECIMAL(10,2)`\r\n5. `TEXT` (or `LONGTEXT` if it is extremely long)\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA developer stores phone numbers in an `INT` column.\r\n\r\n1. Predict what happens to `'03001234567'` in relaxed mode and in strict mode.\r\n2. Explain two problems with this design.\r\n3. Write a corrected column definition.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. The number 3001234567 is larger than the biggest `INT` (2,147,483,647). In relaxed mode MariaDB stores `2147483647` with a warning, so every long number turns into the same wrong value. In strict mode the insert fails with `ERROR 1264 ... Out of range value`.\r\n2. Problems: the leading `0` is lost; `+` and dashes cannot be stored; long numbers do not fit.\r\n3. `phone VARCHAR(20)`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-creating-databases-and-tables/ch07-primary-keys.md": "# Chapter 7: Primary Keys, Constraints, and Integrity\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Constraints are the safety rails of a table. A primary key gives every row a unique ID. Other constraints stop empty, repeated or impossible values from getting in.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nEvery student at school gets an ID card with a number. Two students may both be called Fatima, but their card numbers are always different. The office also has rules: every form must have a name, no two students share a card number, and nobody may borrow more than five books. Constraints are those office rules, written into the table so the database checks them every time.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a primary key is and its two rules.\r\n- Use `AUTO_INCREMENT` to number rows for you.\r\n- Add `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, `DEFAULT` and `CHECK` constraints.\r\n- Read the error MySQL gives when a rule is broken.\r\n- Explain why numeric ids are better keys than names or emails.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🪪 What is a Primary Key?\r\n\r\nA **primary key** is the column that identifies each row. It has two rules:\r\n\r\n1. **Unique**: no two rows may have the same value.\r\n2. **Never empty**: it can never be `NULL`.\r\n\r\nA table has **one** primary key.\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents\r\n┌────┬────────┬───────────┐\r\n│ id │ name   │ city      │\r\n├────┼────────┼───────────┤\r\n│ 4  │ Fatima │ Islamabad │ ← PK 4\r\n│ 9  │ Fatima │ Lahore    │ ← PK 9\r\n└────┴────────┴───────────┘\r\n  same name, different id\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔢 `AUTO_INCREMENT`: Numbers For Free\r\n\r\nMost tables use a plain number called `id` as the key, with `AUTO_INCREMENT`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nid INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen you add a row without an id, MySQL picks the next number: 1, then 2, then 3. You never have to remember the last number used. All five `school_db` tables use this pattern.\r\n\r\n> 💡 Ids may have **gaps**. If an insert fails or a row is deleted, MySQL does not reuse that number. That is normal. An id is a label, not a count.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite writes this as `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` (optionally `AUTOINCREMENT`, spelled without the underscore).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛡️ The Constraint Family\r\n\r\nA **constraint** is a rule attached to a column. MySQL checks it on every new or changed row.\r\n\r\n| Constraint | Rule | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `NOT NULL` | the value is required | `name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL` |\r\n| `UNIQUE` | no two rows may repeat it | `card_code VARCHAR(10) UNIQUE` |\r\n| `DEFAULT` | value used when none is given | `status VARCHAR(10) DEFAULT 'active'` |\r\n| `CHECK` | value must pass a test | `CHECK (max_books BETWEEN 1 AND 5)` |\r\n| `PRIMARY KEY` | unique **and** not null | `id INT PRIMARY KEY` |\r\n\r\n**`PRIMARY KEY` vs `UNIQUE`:** a table has one primary key, but it can have many `UNIQUE` columns. A student's id is the primary key; their library card code can also be unique.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏫 Example: Library Cards\r\n\r\nThe library wants to issue cards. Each card needs a unique code, a student's name, a status, and a borrowing limit of 1 to 5 books.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE library_cards (\r\n    id           INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    card_code    VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    student_name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    status       VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',\r\n    max_books    INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 2\r\n                 CHECK (max_books BETWEEN 1 AND 5),\r\n    issued_on    DATE DEFAULT (CURRENT_DATE)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n`BETWEEN 1 AND 5` means \"from 1 up to 5, including both\". `CURRENT_DATE` is today's date.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDESCRIBE library_cards;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Type | Null | Key | Default | Extra |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| id | int(11) | NO | PRI | NULL | auto_increment |\r\n| card_code | varchar(10) | NO | UNI | NULL | |\r\n| student_name | varchar(50) | NO | | NULL | |\r\n| status | varchar(10) | NO | | active | |\r\n| max_books | int(11) | NO | | 2 | |\r\n| issued_on | date | YES | | curdate() | |\r\n\r\n`UNI` marks the unique column. The `Default` column shows the values MySQL will fill in.\r\n\r\n### Watching the rules work\r\n\r\nTo test the rules we need to add rows. The command is `INSERT INTO`, which Chapter 8 teaches fully. Here, just watch what MySQL says.\r\n\r\nTwo good cards (Zayd gives no limit, so he gets the default 2):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO library_cards (card_code, student_name) VALUES ('LC-001', 'Zayd');\r\nINSERT INTO library_cards (card_code, student_name, max_books) VALUES ('LC-002', 'Maryam', 3);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | card_code | student_name | status | max_books |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 1 | LC-001 | Zayd | active | 2 |\r\n| 2 | LC-002 | Maryam | active | 3 |\r\n\r\nNow four rows that break a rule:\r\n\r\n| Attempt | MySQL's answer | Rule broken |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| card `'LC-001'` again for Bilal | `ERROR 1062: Duplicate entry 'LC-001' for key 'card_code'` | `UNIQUE` |\r\n| card `'LC-003'` with no name | `ERROR 1364: Field 'student_name' doesn't have a default value` | `NOT NULL` |\r\n| Aisha with `max_books` 9 | ``ERROR 4025: CONSTRAINT `library_cards.max_books` failed`` | `CHECK` |\r\n| a row with `id` 1 again | `ERROR 1062: Duplicate entry '1' for key 'PRIMARY'` | `PRIMARY KEY` |\r\n\r\nNone of these rows were saved. The table stays clean.\r\n\r\n> 💡 `CHECK` is enforced by MariaDB 10.2+ and MySQL 8.0.16+. Very old MySQL versions read it but ignored it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤔 Why Not Use Names or Emails as Keys?\r\n\r\n- **Names repeat.** Two students can be called Fatima.\r\n- **Emails change.** A student may get a new address, and every link to the old one breaks.\r\n- **Numbers are small and fast** to compare and store.\r\n- **Privacy (*Amānah*).** An id like `4` shows nothing personal when it appears in links or other tables.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: thinking `NOT NULL` always blocks a missing value.**\r\n\r\nOn a standard XAMPP (relaxed mode), leaving out `student_name` does **not** stop the insert. MariaDB saves an empty text `''` and only gives a warning:\r\n\r\n| id | card_code | student_name | status |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 4 | LC-009 | | active |\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** turn on strict mode (Chapter 6) so the rule becomes a real error:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSET SESSION sql_mode = 'STRICT_TRANS_TABLES';\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice also that the id is 4, not 3. The failed attempts used up number 3. Gaps are normal.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAdd constraints to your `school_db.donations` idea. Write (on paper or in phpMyAdmin with a test table) a column rule that stops a donation `amount` of zero or less.\r\n\r\n*Hint: use `CHECK (amount > 0)`.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **primary key** is unique and never empty; each table has one.\r\n- `AUTO_INCREMENT` numbers new rows; gaps are normal.\r\n- `NOT NULL` = required, `UNIQUE` = no repeats, `DEFAULT` = automatic value, `CHECK` = must pass a test.\r\n- When a rule is broken, MySQL refuses the row and says which rule.\r\n- Use strict mode so relaxed XAMPP does not quietly save bad data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What are the two rules of a primary key?\r\n2. What does `AUTO_INCREMENT` do?\r\n3. How is `PRIMARY KEY` different from `UNIQUE`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Every value is unique, and it is never `NULL`.\r\n2. It gives each new row the next number automatically.\r\n3. A table has only one primary key, which is also never null. A table can have many `UNIQUE` columns.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite a `CREATE TABLE` for `teachers`:\r\n- `id`: whole number, auto-numbered, primary key\r\n- `staff_code`: 8 characters, required and unique\r\n- `full_name`: up to 100 characters, required\r\n- `subject`: up to 40 characters, default `'General'`\r\n- `joining_date`: date, required\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE teachers (\r\n    id           INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    staff_code   CHAR(8) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    full_name    VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    subject      VARCHAR(40) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'General',\r\n    joining_date DATE NOT NULL\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n`VARCHAR(8)` for `staff_code` is also fine.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe charity club wants a `pledges` table for promises to donate to a cause.\r\n\r\n1. Give it an auto-numbered primary key.\r\n2. Add a required `donor` name and a required `cause`.\r\n3. Add an `amount` that must be more than 0.\r\n4. Add a `status` that defaults to `'promised'`.\r\n5. Explain why the key should be `id` and not `donor`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE pledges (\r\n    id     INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    donor  VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    cause  VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\r\n    amount INT NOT NULL CHECK (amount > 0),\r\n    status VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'promised'\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe same donor can make many pledges, so `donor` is not unique and cannot be the key. An `id` is unique for every pledge.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-insert-and-select/ch08-insert-into.md": "# Chapter 8: INSERT INTO — Adding Records\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `INSERT INTO` adds new rows to a table. You name the table, list the columns you are filling, and give the values in the same order.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nOn the first day of term, the school secretary fills in a new line in the register for each new student: name, grade, class, city. She does not redraw the register. She just adds lines. `INSERT INTO` is that pen: it writes new lines into a table that already exists.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Write an `INSERT INTO ... VALUES` statement.\r\n- Know when to use quotes and when not to.\r\n- Skip `AUTO_INCREMENT` and `DEFAULT` columns.\r\n- Add many rows with one statement.\r\n- Fill all five `school_db` tables with the data used in the rest of this book.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧩 The Shape of `INSERT INTO`\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/crud-lifecycle.svg\" alt=\"The 4 CRUD Operations in SQL\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n**CRUD** stands for **C**reate, **R**ead, **U**pdate, **D**elete: the four jobs every app does with data. `INSERT` is the **C**.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2, column3)\r\nVALUES (value1, value2, value3);\r\n```\r\n\r\nFour rules:\r\n\r\n1. The values must be in the **same order** as the columns.\r\n2. Text and dates go inside **single quotes**: `'Lahore'`, `'2026-09-02'`.\r\n3. Numbers have **no quotes**: `8`, `3.90`.\r\n4. You may leave out a column that has `AUTO_INCREMENT` or a `DEFAULT`. MySQL fills it in.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ➕ Adding One Row\r\n\r\nMake sure `school_db` is active (`USE school_db;`), then add the first class:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO classes (name, teacher)\r\nVALUES ('Grade 7 Falcons', 'Ms. Hafsa');\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 1 row affected\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe did not give an `id`. MySQL chose one. To see which number it used:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT LAST_INSERT_ID();\r\n```\r\n\r\n| LAST_INSERT_ID() |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 1 |\r\n\r\nThis is handy in apps: right after adding a student, you can learn their new id.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📦 Adding Many Rows at Once\r\n\r\nPut several sets of brackets after `VALUES`, separated by commas:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO classes (name, teacher) VALUES\r\n  ('Grade 8 Eagles', 'Mr. Imran'),\r\n  ('Grade 9 Hawks',  'Ms. Noor');\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 2 rows affected\r\nRecords: 2  Duplicates: 0  Warnings: 0\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne statement with many rows is faster than many separate statements. The server does the work in one trip.\r\n\r\nLet us look at the table. (`SELECT * FROM` means \"show every column of\"; Chapter 9 explains it.)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM classes;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | teacher |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| 2 | Grade 8 Eagles | Mr. Imran |\r\n| 3 | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏫 Filling `school_db`\r\n\r\nHere is the full data for the other four tables. The rest of this book uses exactly these rows, so type them carefully (or copy them once you have typed a few yourself).\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO students (name, grade, gpa, class_id, city) VALUES\r\n  ('Zayd',    8, 3.90, 2, 'Lahore'),\r\n  ('Maryam',  7, 3.75, 1, 'Karachi'),\r\n  ('Bilal',   9, 3.95, 3, 'Lahore'),\r\n  ('Fatima',  8, 3.85, 2, 'Islamabad'),\r\n  ('Tariq',   9, 3.60, 3, 'Karachi'),\r\n  ('Aisha',   7, 3.40, 1, 'Lahore'),\r\n  ('Hamza',   8, 3.20, 2, 'Peshawar'),\r\n  ('Khadija', 9, 3.70, 3, 'Islamabad');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO books (title, category, pages, available) VALUES\r\n  ('Clean Code',                'Programming',      464, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Algorithm Design',          'Computer Science', 320, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Ethical Technology',        'Ethics',           210, 'No'),\r\n  ('HTML & CSS Architecture',   'Web',              288, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Python Data Science',       'Data',             352, 'No'),\r\n  ('Digital Stewardship',       'Ethics',           180, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Introduction to Networks',  'Computer Science', 240, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Arabic Calligraphy Basics', 'Art',              120, 'Yes');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned) VALUES\r\n  (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'Yes'), (1, 2, '2026-09-20', 'No'),\r\n  (2, 4, '2026-09-05', 'Yes'), (3, 2, '2026-09-08', 'Yes'),\r\n  (3, 7, '2026-09-25', 'No'),  (4, 6, '2026-09-10', 'Yes'),\r\n  (5, 3, '2026-09-12', 'No'),  (6, 1, '2026-09-28', 'No'),\r\n  (8, 5, '2026-09-15', 'Yes'), (8, 6, '2026-10-01', 'No');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO donations (donor, amount, cause, donated_on) VALUES\r\n  ('Ahmad',   1500, 'Books',       '2026-09-01'),\r\n  ('Sara',    5000, 'Clean Water', '2026-09-03'),\r\n  ('Ibrahim', 2500, 'Food Pantry', '2026-09-07'),\r\n  ('Hafsa',   3000, 'Books',       '2026-09-12'),\r\n  ('Yusuf',   8000, 'Clean Water', '2026-09-18'),\r\n  ('Zainab',  4500, 'Food Pantry', '2026-09-21'),\r\n  ('Omar',    1000, 'Books',       '2026-09-26'),\r\n  ('Layla',   2000, 'Clean Water', '2026-10-02');\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach statement reports how many rows it added, for example `Query OK, 8 rows affected`.\r\n\r\nA `loans` row like `(1, 2, '2026-09-20', 'No')` reads: *student 1 (Zayd) borrowed book 2 (Algorithm Design) on 20 September 2026 and has not returned it yet.*\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** The SQL Sandbox already contains these exact rows. Its **Reset** button puts them back if you change anything.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕳️ Leaving Columns Out\r\n\r\nYou do not have to fill every column:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO students (name, grade) VALUES ('Yusuf', 6);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | grade | gpa | class_id | city |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\r\n| 9 | Yusuf | 6 | NULL | NULL | NULL |\r\n\r\nThe columns we skipped became `NULL`, which means \"no value yet\". A `NOT NULL` column without a default could not be skipped like this. \r\n\r\n> 💡 Later chapters show results for the **original** rows only (8 students, 8 books). Try the examples in this section in the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/) and press **Reset** afterwards, so your data matches the book.\r\n\r\n### Text that contains an apostrophe\r\n\r\nA title like *Children's Science Atlas* has a `'` inside it. Write it twice:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO books (title, category, pages, available)\r\nVALUES ('Children''s Science Atlas', 'Science', 96, 'Yes');\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL stores `Children's Science Atlas`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: the counts do not match.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO students (name, grade, city) VALUES ('Zayd', 8);\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1136 (21S01): Column count doesn't match value count at row 1\r\n```\r\n\r\nThree columns but two values. **Fix:** give a value for every column you listed.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: forgetting quotes.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO students (name, grade, city) VALUES (Bilal, 9, 'Lahore');\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1054 (42S22): Unknown column 'Bilal' in 'field list'\r\n```\r\n\r\nWithout quotes, MySQL thinks `Bilal` is a column name. **Fix:** `'Bilal'`.\r\n\r\nDates without quotes are sneakier. `2026-09-02` is read as a sum: 2026 − 9 − 2 = 2015. Strict mode gives `ERROR 1292: Incorrect date value: '2015'`. Relaxed XAMPP saves `0000-00-00` with only a warning. **Fix:** `'2026-09-02'`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOpen the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/). Add yourself as a donor to the *Books* cause:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO donations (donor, amount, cause, donated_on)\r\nVALUES ('Your Name', 500, 'Books', '2026-10-13');\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen run `SELECT * FROM donations;` and find your row at the bottom. Which id did it get?\r\n\r\n*Hint: there were 8 donations, so the next id is...*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `INSERT INTO table (columns) VALUES (values);` adds a row.\r\n- Values must match the columns in number and order.\r\n- Text and dates need single quotes; numbers do not.\r\n- Skip `AUTO_INCREMENT` and `DEFAULT` columns; skipped nullable columns become `NULL`.\r\n- Many rows can go in one `INSERT`, separated by commas.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which statement adds new rows to a table?\r\n2. Must you give a value for an `AUTO_INCREMENT` column?\r\n3. Which quotes go around text values?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `INSERT INTO`\r\n2. No. MySQL fills it in automatically.\r\n3. Single quotes, like `'Lahore'`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nAdd two new books to `books` in **one** statement:\r\n- *Islamic Ethics for Coders*, category Ethics, 150 pages, available\r\n- *Web Engineering Fundamentals*, category Web, 300 pages, available\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO books (title, category, pages, available) VALUES\r\n  ('Islamic Ethics for Coders',    'Ethics', 150, 'Yes'),\r\n  ('Web Engineering Fundamentals', 'Web',    300, 'Yes');\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL replies `Query OK, 2 rows affected`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThis statement fails. Find **three** problems, explain each, and write the fixed version.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned)\r\nVALUES (7, 8, 2026-10-05);\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Four columns but only three values (`returned` is missing) → error 1136.\r\n2. The date has no quotes, so it becomes the sum 2015.\r\n3. Even if it ran, `returned` would have no value. A new loan should say `'No'`.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned)\r\nVALUES (7, 8, '2026-10-05', 'No');\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis records Hamza (student 7) borrowing *Arabic Calligraphy Basics* (book 8).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-insert-and-select/ch09-select-basics.md": "# Chapter 9: SELECT Basics & Aliases\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `SELECT` reads data from a table and shows it to you. It never changes the table. You choose which columns to see, can rename them, and can even calculate new ones.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA visitor asks the librarian, \"Could you show me just the titles and page counts of your books?\" The librarian does not hand over the whole catalogue with every detail. She copies out only the two columns asked for, and the catalogue stays exactly as it was. `SELECT` works the same way: it makes a fresh list for you and leaves the table untouched.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Read all columns with `SELECT *` and chosen columns by name.\r\n- Rename result columns with `AS` (an alias).\r\n- Calculate new columns with `+ - * /`.\r\n- Join text together with `CONCAT`.\r\n- Remove repeated values with `DISTINCT`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 The Shape of a `SELECT`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT column1, column2\r\nFROM table_name;\r\n```\r\n\r\nRead it as: *\"Give me these columns from this table.\"*\r\n\r\nWhen MySQL runs a `SELECT`:\r\n\r\n1. It reads the rows of the table.\r\n2. It keeps only the columns you asked for.\r\n3. It sends back a temporary table called a **result set**.\r\n4. **Nothing in the stored table changes.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⭐ All Columns vs. Chosen Columns\r\n\r\n### Everything with `*`\r\n\r\nThe star `*` means \"every column\":\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM classes;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | teacher |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| 2 | Grade 8 Eagles | Mr. Imran |\r\n| 3 | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n\r\n### Only the columns you need\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | Lahore |\r\n| Maryam | Karachi |\r\n| Bilal | Lahore |\r\n| Fatima | Islamabad |\r\n| Tariq | Karachi |\r\n| Aisha | Lahore |\r\n| Hamza | Peshawar |\r\n| Khadija | Islamabad |\r\n\r\n`SELECT *` is fine for a quick look in phpMyAdmin. In a real app, list the columns you need:\r\n\r\n- Less data travels over the network, so pages load faster.\r\n- You do not accidentally show private columns (*Amānah*).\r\n- If someone adds a column later, your app is not surprised.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏷️ Renaming Columns with `AS`\r\n\r\nAn **alias** is a temporary nickname for a column in the result. It does not rename the real column.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name AS student, gpa AS score\r\nFROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student | score |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 3.90 |\r\n| Maryam | 3.75 |\r\n| Bilal | 3.95 |\r\n| Fatima | 3.85 |\r\n| Tariq | 3.60 |\r\n| Aisha | 3.40 |\r\n| Hamza | 3.20 |\r\n| Khadija | 3.70 |\r\n\r\nIf the nickname has a space, put it in quotes: `title AS 'Book Title'`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧮 Calculated Columns\r\n\r\nYou can do maths inside `SELECT`. Suppose a reader needs about 2 minutes per page:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages, pages * 2 AS minutes_to_read\r\nFROM books;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages | minutes_to_read |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Clean Code | 464 | 928 |\r\n| Algorithm Design | 320 | 640 |\r\n| Ethical Technology | 210 | 420 |\r\n| HTML & CSS Architecture | 288 | 576 |\r\n| Python Data Science | 352 | 704 |\r\n| Digital Stewardship | 180 | 360 |\r\n| Introduction to Networks | 240 | 480 |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics | 120 | 240 |\r\n\r\nThe `books` table still has no `minutes_to_read` column. The value is worked out fresh each time.\r\n\r\nAnother example: a kind sponsor promises to **match** every donation (give the same amount again). What will each cause receive?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, amount, amount * 2 AS with_match\r\nFROM donations;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | amount | with_match |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Ahmad | 1500 | 3000 |\r\n| Sara | 5000 | 10000 |\r\n| Ibrahim | 2500 | 5000 |\r\n| Hafsa | 3000 | 6000 |\r\n| Yusuf | 8000 | 16000 |\r\n| Zainab | 4500 | 9000 |\r\n| Omar | 1000 | 2000 |\r\n| Layla | 2000 | 4000 |\r\n\r\nThe maths signs are `+` (add), `-` (subtract), `*` (multiply) and `/` (divide).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗 Joining Text with `CONCAT`\r\n\r\n`CONCAT` glues pieces of text together into one value:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT CONCAT(name, ' from ', city) AS label\r\nFROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| label |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Zayd from Lahore |\r\n| Maryam from Karachi |\r\n| Bilal from Lahore |\r\n| Fatima from Islamabad |\r\n| Tariq from Karachi |\r\n| Aisha from Lahore |\r\n| Hamza from Peshawar |\r\n| Khadija from Islamabad |\r\n\r\nNotice the spaces inside `' from '`. Without them you would get `ZaydfromLahore`.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** Newer SQLite versions accept `CONCAT` too. If the sandbox ever rejects it, use SQLite's join sign: `name || ' from ' || city`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧹 Removing Repeats with `DISTINCT`\r\n\r\nWhich cities do our students come from? Without help, Lahore appears three times. `DISTINCT` keeps each value once:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DISTINCT city FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| city |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Lahore |\r\n| Karachi |\r\n| Islamabad |\r\n| Peshawar |\r\n\r\nFour different cities, even though there are eight students.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: a comma after the last column.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city,\r\nFROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1064 (42000): You have an error in your SQL syntax; ... near 'FROM students'\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL expects another column after the comma. **Fix:** remove the last comma: `SELECT name, city FROM students;`\r\n\r\n**Mistake: thinking `AS` renames the real column.** After `SELECT name AS student ...`, the table's column is still called `name`. The alias lives only in that one result.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), write one query that shows each book's `title` and its category in this form: `Clean Code (Programming)`. Name the column `shelf_label`.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `CONCAT(title, ' (', category, ')')`.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `SELECT columns FROM table;` reads data and never changes it.\r\n- `*` means all columns; listing columns is better in real apps.\r\n- `AS` gives a column a nickname in the result only.\r\n- You can calculate new columns with `+ - * /` and glue text with `CONCAT`.\r\n- `DISTINCT` shows each different value once.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Does `SELECT` change the data in a table?\r\n2. What does `*` mean in `SELECT * FROM books;`?\r\n3. Which keyword gives a result column a new name?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. No. It only reads.\r\n2. \"All columns\".\r\n3. `AS`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite a query on `books` that shows:\r\n- the title as `book`\r\n- the page count as `pages`\r\n- the page count divided by 20 as `reading_days` (a reader doing 20 pages a day)\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title AS book, pages, pages / 20 AS reading_days\r\nFROM books;\r\n```\r\n\r\nFirst rows of the result:\r\n\r\n| book | pages | reading_days |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Clean Code | 464 | 23.2000 |\r\n| Algorithm Design | 320 | 16.0000 |\r\n\r\nMySQL shows 4 decimal places after `/`. The sandbox (SQLite) shows `23` because it divides whole numbers as whole numbers; write `pages / 20.0` there to see `23.2`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe school newsletter wants a list of book categories and a list of student labels.\r\n\r\n1. Write a query that lists each book category **once**.\r\n2. Write a query that shows `Grade 8: Zayd` style labels for every student, in a column called `badge`.\r\n3. Explain why a real app should not use `SELECT *` on the `students` table.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DISTINCT category FROM books;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| category |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Programming |\r\n| Computer Science |\r\n| Ethics |\r\n| Web |\r\n| Data |\r\n| Art |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT CONCAT('Grade ', grade, ': ', name) AS badge\r\nFROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first row is `Grade 8: Zayd`.\r\n\r\n3. `SELECT *` sends every column, including ones the page does not need. That wastes time and may expose private data (*Amānah*). List only the columns you will show.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-insert-and-select/ch10-where-clause.md": "# Chapter 10: The WHERE Clause & Logical Filters\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `WHERE` is a filter. Only the rows that pass your test appear in the result. You can join tests with `AND`, `OR` and `NOT`, and use `BETWEEN`, `IN` and `IS NULL` for common checks.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt the end of the day, the librarian wants to phone only the students who still have a book at home. She does not call all eight students. She runs her finger down the borrowing list and stops only at rows marked \"not returned\". That finger is the `WHERE` clause: it lets through only the rows that match.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Filter rows with `WHERE` and comparison signs like `=`, `>`, `!=`.\r\n- Combine tests with `AND`, `OR` and `NOT`.\r\n- Use brackets to control which test is checked first.\r\n- Write ranges with `BETWEEN` and lists with `IN`.\r\n- Find missing values with `IS NULL`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧹 The Shape of `WHERE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT columns\r\nFROM table_name\r\nWHERE condition;\r\n```\r\n\r\nA **condition** is a test that is either true or false for each row. MySQL keeps the rows where it is true.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, grade, city\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE city = 'Lahore';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | grade | city |\r\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | 8 | Lahore |\r\n| Bilal | 9 | Lahore |\r\n| Aisha | 7 | Lahore |\r\n\r\nThree of the eight students passed the test.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ Comparison Signs\r\n\r\n| Sign | Meaning | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `=` | equal to | `WHERE grade = 9` |\r\n| `!=` or `<>` | not equal to | `WHERE available != 'Yes'` |\r\n| `>` / `<` | more than / less than | `WHERE pages > 300` |\r\n| `>=` / `<=` | at least / at most | `WHERE gpa >= 3.8` |\r\n\r\nBooks with more than 300 pages:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books WHERE pages > 300;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Code | 464 |\r\n| Algorithm Design | 320 |\r\n| Python Data Science | 352 |\r\n\r\nBooks that are not on the shelf:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, available FROM books WHERE available != 'Yes';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | available |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Ethical Technology | No |\r\n| Python Data Science | No |\r\n\r\nText values need quotes (`'Yes'`). Numbers do not (`300`).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔀 `AND`, `OR`, `NOT`\r\n\r\n### `AND`: both tests must be true\r\n\r\nGrade 8 students with a GPA of at least 3.8:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, grade, gpa\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE grade = 8 AND gpa >= 3.8;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | grade | gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 8 | 3.90 |\r\n| Fatima | 8 | 3.85 |\r\n\r\nHamza is in Grade 8 too, but his GPA (3.20) fails the second test.\r\n\r\n### `OR`: at least one test must be true\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE city = 'Karachi' OR city = 'Peshawar';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Maryam | Karachi |\r\n| Tariq | Karachi |\r\n| Hamza | Peshawar |\r\n\r\n### `NOT`: flip the test\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, grade FROM students WHERE NOT grade = 9;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | grade |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 8 |\r\n| Maryam | 7 |\r\n| Fatima | 8 |\r\n| Aisha | 7 |\r\n| Hamza | 8 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📏 `BETWEEN` and `IN`\r\n\r\n### `BETWEEN`: a range, ends included\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, amount\r\nFROM donations\r\nWHERE amount BETWEEN 2000 AND 4500;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | amount |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Ibrahim | 2500 |\r\n| Hafsa | 3000 |\r\n| Zainab | 4500 |\r\n| Layla | 2000 |\r\n\r\nLayla (2000) and Zainab (4500) are included, because `BETWEEN` includes both ends. It means the same as `amount >= 2000 AND amount <= 4500`.\r\n\r\n`BETWEEN` works for dates too:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, donated_on\r\nFROM donations\r\nWHERE donated_on BETWEEN '2026-09-10' AND '2026-09-30';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | donated_on |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Hafsa | 2026-09-12 |\r\n| Yusuf | 2026-09-18 |\r\n| Zainab | 2026-09-21 |\r\n| Omar | 2026-09-26 |\r\n\r\n### `IN`: matches any value in a list\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, category\r\nFROM books\r\nWHERE category IN ('Ethics', 'Art');\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | category |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Ethical Technology | Ethics |\r\n| Digital Stewardship | Ethics |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics | Art |\r\n\r\n`IN ('Ethics', 'Art')` is a shorter way to write `category = 'Ethics' OR category = 'Art'`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕳️ Missing Values: `IS NULL`\r\n\r\n`NULL` means \"no value\" or \"unknown\". It is not zero and not empty text.\r\n\r\nSuppose we add a new student, Yusuf, without a city (as in Chapter 8). Now try:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students WHERE city = NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nEmpty set\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo rows! Nothing is ever \"equal\" to unknown, not even another unknown. Use `IS NULL` instead:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students WHERE city IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Yusuf | NULL |\r\n\r\nAnd `IS NOT NULL` finds rows that do have a value.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: mixing `AND` and `OR` without brackets.**\r\n\r\nWe want Grade 7 **or** Grade 9 students who live in Lahore:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, grade, city FROM students\r\nWHERE grade = 7 OR grade = 9 AND city = 'Lahore';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | grade | city |\r\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| Maryam | 7 | Karachi |\r\n| Bilal | 9 | Lahore |\r\n| Aisha | 7 | Lahore |\r\n\r\nMaryam lives in Karachi, so why is she here? `AND` is checked **before** `OR`, like × before + in maths. MySQL read it as: *grade 7* OR *(grade 9 AND Lahore)*.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** put brackets around the `OR` part.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, grade, city FROM students\r\nWHERE (grade = 7 OR grade = 9) AND city = 'Lahore';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | grade | city |\r\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| Bilal | 9 | Lahore |\r\n| Aisha | 7 | Lahore |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), find every loan where the book has **not** been returned. Show `student_id`, `book_id` and `loan_date`.\r\n\r\n*Hint: the `returned` column holds `'Yes'` or `'No'`. You should get 5 rows.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `WHERE condition` keeps only the rows where the condition is true.\r\n- Compare with `=`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`; quote text, not numbers.\r\n- `AND` needs both tests; `OR` needs at least one; `NOT` flips a test.\r\n- `AND` runs before `OR`, so use brackets when you mix them.\r\n- `BETWEEN` includes both ends; `IN` checks a list; use `IS NULL`, never `= NULL`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which clause filters rows in a `SELECT`?\r\n2. How do you find rows where a column has no value?\r\n3. What is the difference between `AND` and `OR`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `WHERE`\r\n2. `WHERE column IS NULL`\r\n3. `AND` keeps a row only if both tests are true. `OR` keeps it if at least one is true.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nUsing `books`, write a query for each:\r\n1. Books with fewer than 250 pages.\r\n2. Books in the *Computer Science* category that are available.\r\n3. Books with 200 to 300 pages (ends included).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books WHERE pages < 250;\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE category = 'Computer Science' AND available = 'Yes';\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books WHERE pages BETWEEN 200 AND 300;\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Ethical Technology (210), Digital Stewardship (180), Introduction to Networks (240), Arabic Calligraphy Basics (120).\r\n2. Algorithm Design, Introduction to Networks.\r\n3. Ethical Technology (210), HTML & CSS Architecture (288), Introduction to Networks (240).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe charity club wants a list of gifts for the *Books* or *Food Pantry* causes that were **more than 2000** rupees.\r\n\r\n1. Write the query with `OR` and brackets.\r\n2. Rewrite it with `IN`.\r\n3. Predict the result rows.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, cause, amount FROM donations\r\nWHERE (cause = 'Books' OR cause = 'Food Pantry') AND amount > 2000;\r\n\r\nSELECT donor, cause, amount FROM donations\r\nWHERE cause IN ('Books', 'Food Pantry') AND amount > 2000;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | cause | amount |\r\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\r\n| Ibrahim | Food Pantry | 2500 |\r\n| Hafsa | Books | 3000 |\r\n| Zainab | Food Pantry | 4500 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-insert-and-select/ch11-order-by-and-limit.md": "# Chapter 11: Sorting & Pagination — ORDER BY & LIMIT\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `ORDER BY` sorts the result: A to Z, smallest to largest, or oldest to newest (or the other way). `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` cut out one slice of rows, which is how websites show results page by page.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nOn prize day, the head teacher reads out the top three readers. First she sorts the reading list from most books to fewest. Then she reads only the first three names and stops. Sorting is `ORDER BY`. Stopping after three is `LIMIT 3`. Together they answer \"who are the top three?\" in one short query.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Sort results with `ORDER BY`, ascending (`ASC`) or descending (`DESC`).\r\n- Sort by two columns to break ties.\r\n- Show only the first few rows with `LIMIT`.\r\n- Skip rows with `OFFSET` to build page 1, page 2, page 3.\r\n- Calculate the offset for any page number.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ↕️ Why Sort?\r\n\r\nWithout `ORDER BY`, MySQL returns rows in whatever order is easiest for it. Often that looks like id order, but **it is not promised**. If the order matters to you, ask for it.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT columns\r\nFROM table_name\r\nORDER BY column [ASC | DESC];\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `ASC` (**ascending**): A → Z, small → large, old → new. This is the default.\r\n- `DESC` (**descending**): Z → A, large → small, new → old.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔤 Sorting Examples\r\n\r\n### A to Z by name\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students ORDER BY name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Aisha | Lahore |\r\n| Bilal | Lahore |\r\n| Fatima | Islamabad |\r\n| Hamza | Peshawar |\r\n| Khadija | Islamabad |\r\n| Maryam | Karachi |\r\n| Tariq | Karachi |\r\n| Zayd | Lahore |\r\n\r\n### Highest GPA first\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, gpa FROM students ORDER BY gpa DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Bilal | 3.95 |\r\n| Zayd | 3.90 |\r\n| Fatima | 3.85 |\r\n| Maryam | 3.75 |\r\n| Khadija | 3.70 |\r\n| Tariq | 3.60 |\r\n| Aisha | 3.40 |\r\n| Hamza | 3.20 |\r\n\r\nRemember: **DESC = largest first.**\r\n\r\n### Two columns: break the ties\r\n\r\nSeveral students share a grade. Sort by grade first, then by name inside each grade:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT grade, name FROM students ORDER BY grade, name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| grade | name |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Aisha |\r\n| 7 | Maryam |\r\n| 8 | Fatima |\r\n| 8 | Hamza |\r\n| 8 | Zayd |\r\n| 9 | Bilal |\r\n| 9 | Khadija |\r\n| 9 | Tariq |\r\n\r\nEach column can have its own direction. Highest grade first, and the best GPA first inside each grade:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT grade, name, gpa FROM students ORDER BY grade DESC, gpa DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| grade | name | gpa |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 9 | Bilal | 3.95 |\r\n| 9 | Khadija | 3.70 |\r\n| 9 | Tariq | 3.60 |\r\n| 8 | Zayd | 3.90 |\r\n| 8 | Fatima | 3.85 |\r\n| 8 | Hamza | 3.20 |\r\n| 7 | Maryam | 3.75 |\r\n| 7 | Aisha | 3.40 |\r\n\r\n### With `WHERE`\r\n\r\n`WHERE` comes **before** `ORDER BY`. Clean Water gifts, largest first:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, amount FROM donations\r\nWHERE cause = 'Clean Water'\r\nORDER BY amount DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | amount |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Yusuf | 8000 |\r\n| Sara | 5000 |\r\n| Layla | 2000 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✂️ Only the First Few: `LIMIT`\r\n\r\n`LIMIT n` keeps the first *n* rows of the result. The three longest books:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books\r\nORDER BY pages DESC\r\nLIMIT 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Code | 464 |\r\n| Python Data Science | 352 |\r\n| Algorithm Design | 320 |\r\n\r\nThe two newest donations:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, donated_on FROM donations\r\nORDER BY donated_on DESC\r\nLIMIT 2;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | donated_on |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Layla | 2026-10-02 |\r\n| Omar | 2026-09-26 |\r\n\r\nDates stored as `DATE` sort correctly by time, which is one reason Chapter 6 chose that type.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📄 Pages of Results: `LIMIT` with `OFFSET`\r\n\r\nA website showing 100 books does not dump them all at once. It shows page 1, page 2, and so on. This is called **pagination**.\r\n\r\n- `LIMIT` = how many rows per page.\r\n- `OFFSET` = how many rows to skip first.\r\n\r\nWith 3 books per page:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Page 1: skip 0\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books ORDER BY id LIMIT 3 OFFSET 0;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Clean Code |\r\n| 2 | Algorithm Design |\r\n| 3 | Ethical Technology |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Page 2: skip 3\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books ORDER BY id LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 4 | HTML & CSS Architecture |\r\n| 5 | Python Data Science |\r\n| 6 | Digital Stewardship |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Page 3: skip 6\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books ORDER BY id LIMIT 3 OFFSET 6;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Introduction to Networks |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics |\r\n\r\nPage 3 has only two rows, because there are only eight books.\r\n\r\n### The page formula\r\n\r\n**OFFSET = (page number − 1) × rows per page**\r\n\r\nPage 3 with 3 per page: (3 − 1) × 3 = 6. ✔\r\n\r\nMySQL also has a short form: `LIMIT 3, 3` means \"skip 3, take 3\". The first number is the offset. Many people find `LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3` easier to read.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite accepts both `LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3` and `LIMIT 3, 3`, so every query in this chapter runs in the sandbox.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: `LIMIT` without `ORDER BY`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt may *look* right today. But without `ORDER BY` the row order is not promised. After rows are changed, page 2 might repeat a book from page 1 or skip one completely. **Fix:** always sort by something unique, such as `id`, when you paginate.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: putting `ORDER BY` before `WHERE`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor FROM donations ORDER BY amount WHERE cause = 'Books';\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis gives `ERROR 1064`. The order of parts is fixed: `SELECT … FROM … WHERE … ORDER BY … LIMIT …`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), find the **single shortest book** in the library. Show its title and pages.\r\n\r\n*Hint: sort by `pages` from small to large and keep one row. The answer has 120 pages.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Row order is not promised unless you use `ORDER BY`.\r\n- `ASC` (default) = small to large; `DESC` = large to small.\r\n- Sort by two columns to break ties, each with its own direction.\r\n- `LIMIT n` keeps the first *n* rows; `OFFSET k` skips *k* rows first.\r\n- Offset for a page = (page − 1) × rows per page; always paginate with `ORDER BY`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which direction does `ORDER BY` use if you write neither `ASC` nor `DESC`?\r\n2. What does `LIMIT 5` do?\r\n3. How do you show the newest loans first?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `ASC` (smallest or A first).\r\n2. It keeps only the first 5 rows of the result.\r\n3. `ORDER BY loan_date DESC`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. List all books from fewest pages to most.\r\n2. Show the one book with the most pages.\r\n3. Write the query for **page 4** of the book list with **12** books per page.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books ORDER BY pages;\r\n\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books ORDER BY pages DESC LIMIT 1;\r\n\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books ORDER BY id LIMIT 12 OFFSET 36;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe second query returns *Clean Code* (464). For page 4: (4 − 1) × 12 = 36.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe school wants a \"Students by City\" page.\r\n\r\n1. Sort students by city A to Z, and by name A to Z inside each city.\r\n2. Show only the first 4 rows.\r\n3. Write the query for the next 4 rows.\r\n4. Explain what could go wrong if you left out `ORDER BY`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students ORDER BY city, name LIMIT 4 OFFSET 0;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Fatima | Islamabad |\r\n| Khadija | Islamabad |\r\n| Maryam | Karachi |\r\n| Tariq | Karachi |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students ORDER BY city, name LIMIT 4 OFFSET 4;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Aisha | Lahore |\r\n| Bilal | Lahore |\r\n| Zayd | Lahore |\r\n| Hamza | Peshawar |\r\n\r\n4. Without `ORDER BY`, the database may return rows in a different order on each run. A student could appear on both pages or on neither.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-update-and-delete/ch12-update-statement.md": "# Chapter 12: The UPDATE Statement — Modifying Records\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `UPDATE` changes values in rows that already exist. Always add a `WHERE` clause. Without it, MySQL changes **every** row in the table.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nHamza's family moves from Peshawar to Islamabad. The school secretary opens the register, finds Hamza's line, rubs out \"Peshawar\" and writes \"Islamabad\". She does not touch anyone else's line. And before rubbing anything out, she checks twice that it really is Hamza's line. That careful edit is exactly what `UPDATE ... WHERE` does.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Write an `UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE` statement.\r\n- Change one column or several columns at once.\r\n- Update a value based on its old value (for example `pages + 16`).\r\n- Read MySQL's \"Rows matched / Changed\" message.\r\n- Use the \"SELECT first, then UPDATE\" safety habit.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✏️ The Shape of `UPDATE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE table_name\r\nSET column1 = new_value1, column2 = new_value2\r\nWHERE condition;\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `UPDATE` names the table.\r\n- `SET` says which columns get which new values.\r\n- `WHERE` says **which rows** to change. It works exactly like the `WHERE` you learned in Chapter 10.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Changing One Row\r\n\r\n### Step 1: look before you change\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, name, city FROM students WHERE id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | city |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Hamza | Peshawar |\r\n\r\nOne row, and it is the right student.\r\n\r\n### Step 2: update with the same `WHERE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE students\r\nSET city = 'Islamabad'\r\nWHERE id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 1 row affected\r\nRows matched: 1  Changed: 1  Warnings: 0\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Rows matched**: how many rows passed the `WHERE` test.\r\n- **Changed**: how many actually got a different value.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: check the result\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, name, city FROM students WHERE id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | city |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Hamza | Islamabad |\r\n\r\nIf you run the same `UPDATE` again, MySQL says `Rows matched: 1  Changed: 0`. The row matched, but it already held `'Islamabad'`, so nothing changed.\r\n\r\n> 💡 Why `WHERE id = 7` and not `WHERE name = 'Hamza'`? The id is unique. A second Hamza might join the school next year.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📚 More Real Updates\r\n\r\n### A book comes back to the shelf\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'Yes' WHERE id = 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 3 | Ethical Technology | Yes |\r\n\r\n### Marking a loan as returned\r\n\r\nKhadija (student 8) returns *Digital Stewardship* (book 6). Two tests pick out the exact loan:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE loans\r\nSET returned = 'Yes'\r\nWHERE student_id = 8 AND book_id = 6;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM loans WHERE student_id = 8;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | student_id | book_id | loan_date | returned |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 9 | 8 | 5 | 2026-09-15 | Yes |\r\n| 10 | 8 | 6 | 2026-10-01 | Yes |\r\n\r\n### Several rows at once\r\n\r\nAt the start of a new year, every Grade 9 student moves up to Grade 10:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE students SET grade = 10 WHERE grade = 9;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 3 rows affected\r\nRows matched: 3  Changed: 3  Warnings: 0\r\n```\r\n\r\nBilal, Tariq and Khadija are now in Grade 10. Here the `WHERE` matches three rows on purpose.\r\n\r\n### Several columns at once\r\n\r\nSeparate each `column = value` pair with a comma:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books\r\nSET category = 'Computer Science', pages = 330\r\nWHERE id = 2;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ➕ Updating From the Old Value\r\n\r\nThe new value can use the old one. A new edition of *Arabic Calligraphy Basics* has 16 extra practice pages:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books SET pages = pages + 16 WHERE id = 8;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics | 136 |\r\n\r\nMySQL reads the old value (120), adds 16, and stores 136. You do not need to know the old value yourself.\r\n\r\n> ⚠️ Rules from Chapter 7 still apply. A `gpa + 0.1` for Bilal gives 4.05. `DECIMAL(3,2)` allows up to 9.99, so MySQL accepts it, even though a GPA should stop at 4. A `CHECK (gpa <= 4)` constraint would have caught this.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🚨 The Golden Rule: Never Forget `WHERE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE students SET city = 'Quetta';\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 8 rows affected\r\nRows matched: 8  Changed: 8  Warnings: 0\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | Quetta |\r\n| Maryam | Quetta |\r\n| Bilal | Quetta |\r\n| Fatima | Quetta |\r\n| Tariq | Quetta |\r\n| Aisha | Quetta |\r\n| Hamza | Quetta |\r\n| Khadija | Quetta |\r\n\r\nEvery student now \"lives\" in Quetta. MySQL did not ask \"are you sure?\" There is no undo button. The only way back is a backup, or a transaction (Chapter 14).\r\n\r\n**The safety habit:**\r\n\r\n1. Write the `SELECT` with your `WHERE` first.\r\n2. Check the rows it shows.\r\n3. Change `SELECT ... FROM` into `UPDATE ... SET`, keeping the **same** `WHERE`.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** If you try this in the SQL Sandbox, press **Reset** to bring the original data back. A real MySQL server has no Reset button.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: using `AND` instead of a comma in `SET`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books SET category = 'Web' AND pages = 300 WHERE id = 4;\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: MySQL reads `'Web' AND pages = 300` as one true/false test. On a relaxed XAMPP it stores the answer, `0`, in `category` (with 2 warnings), and the pages do not change at all:\r\n\r\n| id | title | category | pages |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 4 | HTML & CSS Architecture | 0 | 288 |\r\n\r\nIn strict mode you get `ERROR 1292: Truncated incorrect DOUBLE value: 'Web'` instead.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books SET category = 'Web', pages = 300 WHERE id = 4;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), Zayd (student 1) returns *Algorithm Design* (book 2).\r\n\r\n1. `SELECT` the loan first.\r\n2. Update its `returned` column to `'Yes'`.\r\n3. `SELECT` again to check. Then press **Reset**.\r\n\r\n*Hint: the loan has `student_id = 1 AND book_id = 2`.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `UPDATE table SET column = value WHERE condition;` changes existing rows.\r\n- Use a unique column, like `id`, in `WHERE` to change exactly one row.\r\n- Separate several changes with commas in `SET`.\r\n- A new value can use the old one: `pages = pages + 16`.\r\n- Without `WHERE`, every row changes. Always `SELECT` first with the same `WHERE`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which keyword lists the columns and their new values?\r\n2. What happens with `UPDATE books SET available = 'No';`?\r\n3. How do you add 1 to a column called `visits`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `SET`\r\n2. There is no `WHERE`, so **every** book is marked `'No'`.\r\n3. `SET visits = visits + 1` (with a `WHERE` for the right row).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. Change Aisha's (id 6) city to Karachi.\r\n2. Mark every book in the *Ethics* category as available.\r\n3. Hafsa (donation id 4) gave 500 rupees more. Increase her `amount` by 500.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE students SET city = 'Karachi' WHERE id = 6;\r\n\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'Yes' WHERE category = 'Ethics';\r\n\r\nUPDATE donations SET amount = amount + 500 WHERE id = 4;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe second one reports `Rows matched: 2  Changed: 1`. Both Ethics books matched, but *Digital Stewardship* was already `'Yes'`. Hafsa's amount becomes 3500.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe librarian moves every *Data* book into the *Computer Science* category and marks them available.\r\n\r\n1. Write the `SELECT` you would run first.\r\n2. Write the `UPDATE`.\r\n3. Predict the \"Rows matched / Changed\" line.\r\n4. Explain why the `SELECT` step is part of treating data as an *Amānah*.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, title, category, available FROM books WHERE category = 'Data';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title | category | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 5 | Python Data Science | Data | No |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books\r\nSET category = 'Computer Science', available = 'Yes'\r\nWHERE category = 'Data';\r\n```\r\n\r\n`Rows matched: 1  Changed: 1  Warnings: 0`\r\n\r\n4. The records belong to the whole school. Checking first proves you will change only the rows you mean to. A careless update could wreck everyone's data.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-update-and-delete/ch13-delete-statement.md": "# Chapter 13: The DELETE Statement & TRUNCATE\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `DELETE` removes chosen rows from a table. `TRUNCATE` empties a whole table quickly. `DROP` throws away the table itself. Many real apps prefer a \"soft delete\", which hides a row instead of destroying it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt the end of term, the librarian tidies her borrowing notebook. She crosses out only the old lines for books that came back long ago. She does not tear out every page, and she certainly does not throw the notebook in the bin. Crossing out chosen lines is `DELETE ... WHERE`. Tearing out every page is `TRUNCATE`. Binning the notebook is `DROP`.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Remove chosen rows with `DELETE FROM ... WHERE`.\r\n- Use \"SELECT first\" before deleting.\r\n- Explain the difference between `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE` and `DROP`.\r\n- See how `AUTO_INCREMENT` numbers behave after deleting.\r\n- Use the soft delete pattern to keep records safe (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗑️ The Shape of `DELETE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM table_name\r\nWHERE condition;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is no column list. `DELETE` always removes **whole rows**. To clear just one value, use `UPDATE ... SET column = NULL` instead.\r\n\r\nLike `UPDATE`, **`DELETE` without `WHERE` removes every row.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Deleting One Row\r\n\r\nOmar's donation (id 7) was entered by mistake. First, look:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, donor, amount FROM donations WHERE id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | donor | amount |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 7 | Omar | 1000 |\r\n\r\nThen delete with the **same** `WHERE`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM donations WHERE id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 1 row affected\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, donor FROM donations;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | donor |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Ahmad |\r\n| 2 | Sara |\r\n| 3 | Ibrahim |\r\n| 4 | Hafsa |\r\n| 5 | Yusuf |\r\n| 6 | Zainab |\r\n| 8 | Layla |\r\n\r\nNumber 7 is gone, and there is now a gap. That is normal. The next new donation does **not** fill the gap; it gets id 9.\r\n\r\nA `WHERE` that matches nothing is harmless: `DELETE FROM students WHERE id = 99;` says `Query OK, 0 rows affected`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📚 Deleting Several Rows\r\n\r\nThe librarian removes old, finished loans: returned **and** borrowed before 10 September.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM loans\r\nWHERE returned = 'Yes' AND loan_date < '2026-09-10';\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 3 rows affected\r\n```\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM loans;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | student_id | book_id | loan_date | returned |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 2 | 1 | 2 | 2026-09-20 | No |\r\n| 5 | 3 | 7 | 2026-09-25 | No |\r\n| 6 | 4 | 6 | 2026-09-10 | Yes |\r\n| 7 | 5 | 3 | 2026-09-12 | No |\r\n| 8 | 6 | 1 | 2026-09-28 | No |\r\n| 9 | 8 | 5 | 2026-09-15 | Yes |\r\n| 10 | 8 | 6 | 2026-10-01 | No |\r\n\r\nLoans 1, 3 and 4 were removed. Loan 6 (10 September) stayed, because `<` means \"strictly before\".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ `DELETE` vs `TRUNCATE` vs `DROP`\r\n\r\n| Command | What it does | Ids start again at 1? | Can be undone in a transaction? |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `DELETE FROM t WHERE ...` | removes matching rows | No | Yes (Chapter 14) |\r\n| `TRUNCATE TABLE t;` | removes **all** rows, keeps the empty table | Yes | No |\r\n| `DROP TABLE t;` | removes rows **and** the table's plan | table is gone | No |\r\n\r\n```\r\nDELETE   → cross out some lines\r\nTRUNCATE → tear out every page,\r\n           keep the empty notebook\r\nDROP     → bin the whole notebook\r\n```\r\n\r\nSee the difference in numbering. After `TRUNCATE TABLE donations;` and one new insert:\r\n\r\n| id | donor |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Sara |\r\n\r\nThe counter restarted at 1. After a `DELETE`, it would have carried on (9, 10, 11...).\r\n\r\n`TRUNCATE` is very fast on huge tables, because it does not remove rows one by one. Use it only for scratch tables you truly want empty.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite has no `TRUNCATE`. Use `DELETE FROM t;` there (it empties the table). Press **Reset** afterwards.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤲 The Soft Delete Pattern (*Amānah*)\r\n\r\nSchool records are a trust. A student who leaves still has a history: books borrowed, marks earned. Destroying those rows can break other tables and loses the truth.\r\n\r\nSo many apps use a **soft delete**: they mark the row as deleted instead of removing it.\r\n\r\n1. Add a flag column. (`ALTER TABLE` changes an existing table's plan; here it adds one column.)\r\n\r\n   ```sql\r\n   ALTER TABLE students\r\n   ADD COLUMN is_deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE;\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n2. When Tariq (id 5) leaves, \"soft delete\" him:\r\n\r\n   ```sql\r\n   UPDATE students SET is_deleted = TRUE WHERE id = 5;\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n3. Normal pages only show rows that are not deleted:\r\n\r\n   ```sql\r\n   SELECT id, name FROM students WHERE is_deleted = FALSE;\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n| id | name |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Zayd |\r\n| 2 | Maryam |\r\n| 3 | Bilal |\r\n| 4 | Fatima |\r\n| 6 | Aisha |\r\n| 7 | Hamza |\r\n| 8 | Khadija |\r\n\r\nWhy this is the ethical choice:\r\n\r\n- A mistaken click can be undone: set `is_deleted` back to `FALSE`.\r\n- The history stays true and can be checked later (*Ṣidq*, truthfulness).\r\n- Rows in other tables, like Tariq's loans, still point to a real student.\r\n\r\nBut soft delete is not always right. If a family asks the school to **erase** personal data it no longer needs, a real `DELETE` (after checking the rules) respects their privacy. Keep only what you need.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: forgetting `WHERE`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM classes;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nQuery OK, 3 rows affected\r\n```\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM classes;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nEmpty set\r\n```\r\n\r\nAll three classes are gone. MySQL never asked \"are you sure?\"\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** `SELECT` first, then delete with the same `WHERE`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM classes WHERE id = 3;\r\nDELETE FROM classes WHERE id = 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\nChapter 14 shows two extra safety nets: transactions and safe-updates mode.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/):\r\n\r\n1. Show all donations under 2000 rupees.\r\n2. Delete them with the same `WHERE`.\r\n3. Show all donations again. How many rows are left? Then press **Reset**.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `WHERE amount < 2000`. Two rows match (Ahmad 1500 and Omar 1000), so 6 should be left.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `DELETE FROM table WHERE condition;` removes whole rows that match.\r\n- Without `WHERE`, every row is deleted, with no warning.\r\n- Deleted ids leave gaps; `AUTO_INCREMENT` keeps counting forward.\r\n- `TRUNCATE` empties a table and restarts ids at 1; `DROP` removes the table itself.\r\n- A soft delete (`is_deleted = TRUE`) hides a row but keeps history safe.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which statement removes chosen rows from a table?\r\n2. What happens with `DELETE FROM students;`?\r\n3. After `TRUNCATE`, what id does the next new row get?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `DELETE FROM ... WHERE ...`\r\n2. Every student row is deleted. The empty table stays.\r\n3. 1. `TRUNCATE` resets the `AUTO_INCREMENT` counter. (After `DELETE`, numbering would carry on.)\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. Delete every loan that has been returned.\r\n2. Empty a scratch table called `temp_imports` so that its ids start again at 1.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM loans WHERE returned = 'Yes';\r\n\r\nTRUNCATE TABLE temp_imports;\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn the original data, the first statement removes 5 rows (loans 1, 3, 4, 6 and 9).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe library wants to stop showing *Ethical Technology* (book 3) because it is being repaired, but keep its borrowing history.\r\n\r\n1. Explain why `DELETE FROM books WHERE id = 3;` is a bad idea here.\r\n2. Design a soft delete for `books`: the column, the \"hide\" statement and the query for the public catalogue.\r\n3. Write the statement that brings the book back after repair.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Loan 7 says student 5 borrowed book 3. If book 3 is deleted, that loan points to a book that no longer exists, and the history is broken.\r\n2.\r\n```sql\r\nALTER TABLE books ADD COLUMN is_hidden BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE;\r\n\r\nUPDATE books SET is_hidden = TRUE WHERE id = 3;\r\n\r\nSELECT id, title FROM books WHERE is_hidden = FALSE;\r\n```\r\n\r\n3.\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE books SET is_hidden = FALSE WHERE id = 3;\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-update-and-delete/ch14-safe-updates.md": "# Chapter 14: Safe Updates, Transactions & Data Protection\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Data is a trust (*Amānah*). A transaction groups several changes so they all succeed together or all get cancelled together. Safe-updates mode blocks risky `UPDATE` and `DELETE` statements.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nWhen Hamza borrows a book, the librarian does two things. She writes a new line in the borrowing notebook, and she moves the book's card from \"on shelf\" to \"out\". If the bell rings after the first step, the notebook says Hamza has the book, but the card still says it is on the shelf. The records now disagree. A transaction is like a rule: \"finish both steps, or undo the first one\".\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a transaction is and why it is \"all or nothing\".\r\n- Use `START TRANSACTION`, `COMMIT` and `ROLLBACK`.\r\n- Describe the four ACID promises in plain words.\r\n- Turn on safe-updates mode to block `UPDATE`/`DELETE` without a key.\r\n- Know where transactions work (InnoDB, the command line, one phpMyAdmin run).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗 What is a Transaction?\r\n\r\nBorrowing a library book needs **two** changes:\r\n\r\n1. **Add** a row to `loans`.\r\n2. **Update** the book: `available = 'No'`.\r\n\r\nIf the computer crashes between steps 1 and 2, the data is half-done. The loan exists, but the catalogue still shows the book as available. Another student might be told it is on the shelf.\r\n\r\nA **transaction** wraps several statements into one unit:\r\n\r\n- If everything works: **`COMMIT`** saves all the changes for good.\r\n- If anything goes wrong: **`ROLLBACK`** cancels all of them, as if nothing happened.\r\n\r\n```\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n ├ 1. INSERT INTO loans ...\r\n └ 2. UPDATE books ...\r\n        │\r\n   ┌────┴─────┐\r\n   ▼          ▼\r\nCOMMIT;    ROLLBACK;\r\n(save all) (undo all)\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧪 A Transaction You Can Watch\r\n\r\nBefore we start, *Arabic Calligraphy Basics* is on the shelf:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, title, available FROM books WHERE id = 8;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics | Yes |\r\n\r\n### Try it, then cancel\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned)\r\nVALUES (7, 8, '2026-10-13', 'No');\r\n\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'No' WHERE id = 8;\r\n```\r\n\r\nInside the transaction, the change is visible to you:\r\n\r\n| id | title | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics | No |\r\n\r\nNow Hamza changes his mind:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nROLLBACK;\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck again:\r\n\r\n| id | title | available |\r\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics | Yes |\r\n\r\nAnd `SELECT * FROM loans WHERE student_id = 7;` gives `Empty set`. **Both** changes were undone.\r\n\r\n### Do it for real\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned)\r\nVALUES (7, 8, '2026-10-13', 'No');\r\n\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'No' WHERE id = 8;\r\n\r\nCOMMIT;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM loans WHERE student_id = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | student_id | book_id | loan_date | returned |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 12 | 7 | 8 | 2026-10-13 | No |\r\n\r\nWhy id 12 and not 11? The cancelled insert had already used number 11. A rollback undoes the row, not the counter. Gaps again, and that is fine.\r\n\r\n### Where transactions work\r\n\r\n- Tables must use the **InnoDB** storage engine. It is the default in MySQL and MariaDB, so your `school_db` tables already use it.\r\n- Normally MySQL is in **autocommit** mode: each statement is saved the moment it runs. `START TRANSACTION` pauses that until you `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`.\r\n- In **phpMyAdmin**, put the whole transaction in **one** SQL box and press Go once. Each Go is a fresh connection, so a transaction cannot stretch across two clicks. The command line has no such limit.\r\n- `TRUNCATE`, `DROP` and `CREATE TABLE` save immediately and cannot be rolled back.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite writes `BEGIN TRANSACTION;` (or just `BEGIN;`) instead of `START TRANSACTION;`. `COMMIT` and `ROLLBACK` are the same. MySQL also accepts `BEGIN;`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧱 The ACID Promises\r\n\r\nA trustworthy database keeps four promises, known by the letters **ACID**:\r\n\r\n| Letter | Name | Plain words |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **A** | Atomicity | All steps happen, or none do. (*Atomic* means \"cannot be split\".) |\r\n| **C** | Consistency | The rules (`NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK`...) are never broken. |\r\n| **I** | Isolation | Two people working at once do not see each other's half-finished work. |\r\n| **D** | Durability | After `COMMIT`, the data survives a crash or power cut. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🦺 Safe-Updates Mode\r\n\r\nChapters 12 and 13 showed how one missing `WHERE` can change every row. MySQL has a guard for this:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSET sql_safe_updates = 1;\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow try a careless update:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nUPDATE students SET grade = 10;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1175 (HY000): You are using safe update mode and you tried\r\nto update a table without a WHERE that uses a KEY column\r\n```\r\n\r\nBlocked! A **key column** is an indexed column such as the primary key `id`. This still works:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM loans WHERE id = 1;\r\n```\r\n\r\nSafe mode is strict. Even `DELETE FROM loans WHERE returned = 'Yes';` is blocked, because `returned` is not a key. If you really mean it, turn the guard off for one statement with `SET sql_safe_updates = 0;`, run it carefully, and turn it back on.\r\n\r\nThe setting lasts until you log out. MySQL Workbench, another MySQL tool, switches it on by default.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite has no safe-updates mode. The sandbox's **Reset** button is your safety net there.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤲 Protecting Data Is an *Amānah*\r\n\r\n- **Wrap linked changes in a transaction**, so records never disagree.\r\n- **Back up** before big changes (phpMyAdmin → Export).\r\n- **Keep a history** of who changed what and when. Many apps add `updated_at` and `updated_by` columns, so mistakes or misuse can be traced honestly.\r\n- **Give the website's database user only the rights it needs.** A web page should never be able to `DROP` a table.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: forgetting to finish the transaction.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'No' WHERE id = 2;\r\n-- ... then the student closes the window\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: there was no `COMMIT`. When the connection closes, MySQL rolls the change back. The student thinks it was saved, but it was not.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** always end with `COMMIT;` (to save) or `ROLLBACK;` (to cancel), then check with a `SELECT`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/):\r\n\r\n1. Run `BEGIN;`\r\n2. Delete every donation with `DELETE FROM donations;`\r\n3. Run `SELECT * FROM donations;` — empty!\r\n4. Run `ROLLBACK;` and select again.\r\n\r\n*Hint: after the rollback all 8 donations are back, without pressing Reset.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A transaction groups changes: `START TRANSACTION; ... COMMIT;` or `ROLLBACK;`.\r\n- `COMMIT` saves all the changes; `ROLLBACK` cancels all of them.\r\n- ACID = all-or-nothing, rules kept, no interference, safe after commit.\r\n- Use one phpMyAdmin run (or the command line) for a whole transaction.\r\n- `SET sql_safe_updates = 1;` blocks `UPDATE`/`DELETE` that do not use a key column.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does `COMMIT` do?\r\n2. What does `ROLLBACK` do?\r\n3. What do the letters of **ACID** stand for?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It saves every change made since `START TRANSACTION`, permanently.\r\n2. It cancels every change made since `START TRANSACTION`.\r\n3. **A**tomicity, **C**onsistency, **I**solation, **D**urability.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nFatima (student 4) returns *Digital Stewardship* (book 6) from loan 6. Write a transaction that marks the loan as returned **and** marks the book as available.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nUPDATE loans SET returned = 'Yes' WHERE id = 6;\r\nUPDATE books SET available = 'Yes' WHERE id = 6;\r\n\r\nCOMMIT;\r\n```\r\n\r\n(In the original data, loan 6 is already marked `'Yes'` and book 6 is already available, so MySQL reports `Changed: 0` for both. The pattern is what matters.)\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe charity club moves Layla's gift (donation 8) from *Clean Water* to *Books*, and records a note in a `donation_changes` table at the same time.\r\n\r\n1. Write a transaction with both steps.\r\n2. Say what should happen if the second step fails.\r\n3. Explain why keeping such a change log is part of *Amānah*.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nUPDATE donations SET cause = 'Books' WHERE id = 8;\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO donation_changes (donation_id, old_cause, new_cause, changed_on)\r\nVALUES (8, 'Clean Water', 'Books', '2026-10-13');\r\n\r\nCOMMIT;\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. Run `ROLLBACK;` instead of `COMMIT;`. Then the cause is not changed without a record of the change.\r\n3. Donors trusted the club with their money for a cause. A log shows honestly who moved a gift, when and why, so it can be checked later.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-joins-and-relationships/ch15-foreign-keys.md": "# Chapter 15: Table Relationships & Foreign Keys\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Instead of repeating the same facts in one huge table, we split data into smaller tables and link them. A foreign key is a column that points to a row in another table, and MySQL checks that the row really exists.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nOn a library slip, the librarian does not write out Zayd's full name, class, teacher and city every time he borrows a book. She writes his **student number** and the **book number**. Anyone can look those numbers up in the student register and the book catalogue. But imagine a slip with student number 99, when no student 99 exists. That slip points nowhere. A foreign key is the rule that stops such slips from ever being written.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Spot the problems caused by repeating data in one table.\r\n- Explain parent tables, child tables and foreign keys.\r\n- Add foreign keys to `school_db` with `FOREIGN KEY ... REFERENCES`.\r\n- Read the errors MySQL gives when a link would break.\r\n- Choose an `ON DELETE` action: `RESTRICT`, `CASCADE` or `SET NULL`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔁 The Problem with Repeating Data\r\n\r\nSuppose we stored the class details inside every student row:\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents (bad design)\r\n┌────────┬────────────────┬───────────┐\r\n│ name   │ class_name     │ teacher   │\r\n├────────┼────────────────┼───────────┤\r\n│ Zayd   │ Grade 8 Eagles │ Mr. Imran │\r\n│ Fatima │ Grade 8 Eagles │ Mr. Imran │\r\n│ Hamza  │ Grade 8 Eagles │ Mr. Imran │\r\n└────────┴────────────────┴───────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nThree problems appear:\r\n\r\n1. **Waste**: \"Grade 8 Eagles, Mr. Imran\" is typed again for every student.\r\n2. **Update problem**: if a new teacher takes over, we must change many rows. Miss one, and the data disagrees with itself.\r\n3. **Delete problem**: if the last Grade 8 student leaves and we delete their row, we lose the fact that the class exists at all.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧩 The Fix: Split and Link\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/primary-foreign-key-rel.svg\" alt=\"Primary Key to Foreign Key Relational Mapping\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n`school_db` already does this. Class details live **once** in `classes`. Each student stores only the class's `id` in `class_id`.\r\n\r\n```\r\nclasses (parent)\r\n┌────┬─────────────────┬───────────┐\r\n│ id │ name            │ teacher   │\r\n├────┼─────────────────┼───────────┤\r\n│ 2  │ Grade 8 Eagles  │ Mr. Imran │\r\n└────┴─────────────────┴───────────┘\r\n  ▲\r\n  │ class_id = 2\r\n  │\r\nstudents (child)\r\n┌────┬────────┬──────────┐\r\n│ id │ name   │ class_id │\r\n├────┼────────┼──────────┤\r\n│ 1  │ Zayd   │ 2        │\r\n│ 4  │ Fatima │ 2        │\r\n│ 7  │ Hamza  │ 2        │\r\n└────┴────────┴──────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nKey words:\r\n\r\n- **Parent table**: the table being pointed **to** (`classes`).\r\n- **Child table**: the table that does the pointing (`students`).\r\n- **Foreign key**: the child's pointing column (`students.class_id`). It holds a primary key value from the parent.\r\n\r\nSplitting data like this, so each fact is stored once, is called **normalization**.\r\n\r\n`school_db` has three links:\r\n\r\n| Child column | Points to |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `students.class_id` | `classes.id` |\r\n| `loans.student_id` | `students.id` |\r\n| `loans.book_id` | `books.id` |\r\n\r\nThe `loans` table is a child of **two** parents. Each row links one student to one book.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔒 Declaring Foreign Keys\r\n\r\nSo far, `class_id` is just a number. MySQL does not know it is a link, so it would accept `class_id = 50`. Let us make the links official.\r\n\r\n### On tables that already exist: `ALTER TABLE`\r\n\r\n`ALTER TABLE` changes the plan of an existing table:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nALTER TABLE students\r\n  ADD CONSTRAINT fk_students_class\r\n  FOREIGN KEY (class_id) REFERENCES classes(id);\r\n\r\nALTER TABLE loans\r\n  ADD CONSTRAINT fk_loans_student\r\n    FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id),\r\n  ADD CONSTRAINT fk_loans_book\r\n    FOREIGN KEY (book_id) REFERENCES books(id);\r\n```\r\n\r\nRead it as: *\"`class_id` must match an `id` in `classes`.\"* The name after `CONSTRAINT` (like `fk_students_class`) is your label for the rule. It appears in error messages.\r\n\r\n### In a new table: inside `CREATE TABLE`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE loans (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    student_id INT,\r\n    book_id    INT,\r\n    loan_date  DATE,\r\n    returned   VARCHAR(3),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_loans_student\r\n      FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_loans_book\r\n      FOREIGN KEY (book_id) REFERENCES books(id)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nCreate the **parent tables first**. If `books` does not exist yet, MySQL refuses:\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1005 (HY000): Can't create table ... (errno: 150\r\n\"Foreign key constraint is incorrectly formed\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🚫 Watching the Rules Work\r\n\r\n### A loan for a student who does not exist\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO loans (student_id, book_id, loan_date, returned)\r\nVALUES (99, 1, '2026-10-13', 'No');\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1452 (23000): Cannot add or update a child row: a foreign key\r\nconstraint fails (`school_db`.`loans`, CONSTRAINT `fk_loans_student`\r\nFOREIGN KEY (`student_id`) REFERENCES `students` (`id`))\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is no student 99, so the slip is refused.\r\n\r\n### Deleting a parent that still has children\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nDELETE FROM students WHERE id = 1;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1451 (23000): Cannot delete or update a parent row: a foreign\r\nkey constraint fails (`school_db`.`loans`, CONSTRAINT `fk_loans_student` ...)\r\n```\r\n\r\nZayd still has loans. Deleting him would leave loans pointing nowhere.\r\n\r\nBut `DELETE FROM students WHERE id = 7;` **works**, because Hamza has no loans. Nothing would be left pointing at him.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite only checks foreign keys after `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;`, and the sandbox's tables have no foreign keys declared. So these errors only appear in MySQL.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚙️ What Happens on Delete? `ON DELETE`\r\n\r\nYou can choose what MySQL does when a parent row is deleted:\r\n\r\n| Action | What happens to the children |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `RESTRICT` / `NO ACTION` | **Default.** The delete is refused (error 1451). |\r\n| `CASCADE` | The children are deleted too. |\r\n| `SET NULL` | The children stay, but their link becomes `NULL`. |\r\n\r\nExample: if a class is closed, keep the students but clear their class:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCONSTRAINT fk_students_class\r\n  FOREIGN KEY (class_id) REFERENCES classes(id)\r\n  ON DELETE SET NULL\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter deleting class 1 (Grade 7 Falcons), Maryam and Aisha remain:\r\n\r\n| id | name | class_id |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 2 | Maryam | NULL |\r\n| 6 | Aisha | NULL |\r\n\r\nChoose carefully. `CASCADE` on `loans.student_id` would silently erase a student's whole borrowing history. For school records, the safe default `RESTRICT` (plus soft delete from Chapter 13) is usually the right choice.\r\n\r\nThere is also `ON UPDATE CASCADE`: if a parent's id changes, the children's links change with it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: making the child table first.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE loans (... FOREIGN KEY (book_id) REFERENCES books(id));\r\n-- books does not exist yet\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: `ERROR 1005 ... errno: 150 \"Foreign key constraint is incorrectly formed\"`.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** create `classes`, `students` and `books` first, then `loans`. When removing tables, go the other way: drop `loans` before `books`. (Dropping `books` while `loans` points to it gives `ERROR 1451`.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn phpMyAdmin, on your own `school_db`:\r\n\r\n1. Run the two `ALTER TABLE` statements above.\r\n2. Try to add a loan for book 50. Read the error.\r\n3. Try to delete student 7 (Hamza). Why does it work? Then add him back with `INSERT`.\r\n\r\n*Hint: look for \"child row\" vs \"parent row\" in the error text.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Repeating data causes waste and update and delete problems.\r\n- Split data into tables and link them with ids (normalization).\r\n- A **foreign key** in a child table must match a primary key in the parent.\r\n- MySQL refuses links to missing rows (error 1452) and deletes that would orphan children (error 1451).\r\n- `ON DELETE` can be `RESTRICT` (default), `CASCADE` or `SET NULL`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is a foreign key?\r\n2. Which must be created first: the parent or the child table?\r\n3. Name one benefit of splitting data into linked tables.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A column in a child table that holds a primary key value from a parent table, and must match a real row there.\r\n2. The parent table.\r\n3. Any one: each fact is stored once; updates happen in one place; deleting one row does not lose unrelated facts.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA `book_reviews` table records short reviews students write for the library. It has `id`, `book_id`, `student_id`, `review` (up to 300 characters) and `written_on`. Write its `CREATE TABLE` with **both** foreign keys.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE book_reviews (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    book_id    INT NOT NULL,\r\n    student_id INT NOT NULL,\r\n    review     VARCHAR(300) NOT NULL,\r\n    written_on DATE,\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_reviews_book\r\n      FOREIGN KEY (book_id) REFERENCES books(id),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_reviews_student\r\n      FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nWith the foreign keys from this chapter in place (default `RESTRICT`):\r\n\r\n1. Predict what happens with `DELETE FROM classes WHERE id = 3;` and explain why.\r\n2. The school really is closing Grade 9 Hawks. Describe two different safe ways to handle it.\r\n3. Which `ON DELETE` action would be dangerous on `loans.student_id`, and why?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It fails with `ERROR 1451 ... Cannot delete or update a parent row`. Bilal, Tariq and Khadija still have `class_id = 3`.\r\n2. Either move the students first (`UPDATE students SET class_id = 2 WHERE class_id = 3;`) and then delete the class; or define the key with `ON DELETE SET NULL` so the students stay with no class. A soft delete on `classes` (an `is_closed` flag) is a third option.\r\n3. `CASCADE`. Deleting a student would silently delete all their loans, wiping the library's true history (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-joins-and-relationships/ch16-what-is-a-join.md": "# Chapter 16: What is a JOIN? — Combining Tables\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: Chapter 15 split our data into linked tables. A `JOIN` puts the pieces back together inside one query result, using the links. The stored tables do not change.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA loan slip says only \"student 1, book 2\". The librarian wants to print a friendly reminder: *\"Zayd, please return Algorithm Design.\"* So she puts the slip next to the student register and finds number 1, then next to the catalogue and finds number 2. Matching numbers across lists like this is exactly what a `JOIN` does, only much faster.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain why we need `JOIN` after splitting data into tables.\r\n- Write a `JOIN ... ON` that matches a foreign key to a primary key.\r\n- Name columns clearly with `table.column` and short table aliases.\r\n- Join three tables in one query.\r\n- Explain what goes wrong if you forget the `ON` condition.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧩 Putting the Pieces Back Together\r\n\r\n`school_db` stores each fact once:\r\n\r\n- `students` holds names and a `class_id`.\r\n- `classes` holds class names and teachers.\r\n- `loans` holds only numbers: `student_id`, `book_id`.\r\n\r\nThat is tidy, but a loan row like `(2, 1, 2, '2026-09-20', 'No')` is hard for a person to read. A **JOIN** builds a result that combines columns from two or more tables, row by matching row.\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/sql-joins-visual.svg\" alt=\"SQL JOINs Visualized\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nThe picture shows three kinds of join. This chapter uses the plain `JOIN`, which keeps only rows that match on both sides. Chapter 17 looks at it closely (its full name is `INNER JOIN`), and Chapter 18 covers `LEFT` and `RIGHT` joins.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗 The Shape of a JOIN\r\n\r\nEvery join needs two things:\r\n\r\n1. **Which table to join**: `JOIN second_table`\r\n2. **How rows match**: `ON first.foreign_key = second.id`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT students.name, classes.name, classes.teacher\r\nFROM students\r\nJOIN classes ON students.class_id = classes.id\r\nORDER BY students.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | name | teacher |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | Grade 8 Eagles | Mr. Imran |\r\n| Maryam | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| Bilal | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Fatima | Grade 8 Eagles | Mr. Imran |\r\n| Tariq | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Aisha | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| Hamza | Grade 8 Eagles | Mr. Imran |\r\n| Khadija | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n\r\nHow MySQL thinks about it, for Zayd:\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents row: Zayd, class_id = 2\r\n                       │\r\n                       ▼ ON class_id = id\r\nclasses row:  id = 2, Grade 8 Eagles,\r\n              Mr. Imran\r\n                       │\r\n                       ▼\r\nresult row:   Zayd | Grade 8 Eagles |\r\n              Mr. Imran\r\n```\r\n\r\nWriting `students.name` (table name, dot, column name) tells MySQL exactly which `name` you mean. Both tables have a `name` column!\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✂️ Short Names: Table Aliases\r\n\r\nTyping `students.` and `classes.` again and again is tiring. Give each table a short **alias** right after its name:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name AS student, c.name AS class, c.teacher\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN classes c ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nWHERE c.teacher = 'Ms. Noor';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student | class | teacher |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Bilal | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Tariq | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Khadija | Grade 9 Hawks | Ms. Noor |\r\n\r\n- `students s` means \"call `students` **s** in this query\".\r\n- Column aliases with `AS` (Chapter 9) fix the confusing double `name` heading.\r\n- `WHERE` and `ORDER BY` work after a join exactly as before.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗🔗 Three Tables: Who Borrowed What?\r\n\r\nA loan links a student **and** a book, so we join three tables. Start from `loans` and join each parent:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name AS student, b.title AS book, l.loan_date\r\nFROM loans l\r\nJOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nJOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nORDER BY l.loan_date;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student | book | loan_date |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | Clean Code | 2026-09-02 |\r\n| Maryam | HTML & CSS Architecture | 2026-09-05 |\r\n| Bilal | Algorithm Design | 2026-09-08 |\r\n| Fatima | Digital Stewardship | 2026-09-10 |\r\n| Tariq | Ethical Technology | 2026-09-12 |\r\n| Khadija | Python Data Science | 2026-09-15 |\r\n| Zayd | Algorithm Design | 2026-09-20 |\r\n| Bilal | Introduction to Networks | 2026-09-25 |\r\n| Aisha | Clean Code | 2026-09-28 |\r\n| Khadija | Digital Stewardship | 2026-10-01 |\r\n\r\nNow the loan list reads like sentences. The `loans` table itself still holds only numbers.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💥 Forgetting `ON`: Every Row Times Every Row\r\n\r\nWhat if you leave out the `ON` part?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, c.name\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN classes c;\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL pairs **every** student with **every** class. This is called a **cross join** (or *Cartesian product*). The first rows look like this:\r\n\r\n| name | name |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Zayd | Grade 7 Falcons |\r\n| Zayd | Grade 8 Eagles |\r\n| Zayd | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n| Maryam | Grade 7 Falcons |\r\n| Maryam | Grade 8 Eagles |\r\n| Maryam | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n\r\n8 students × 3 classes = **24 rows**, and most of them are false: Zayd is not in three classes! With 1,000 students and 50 classes you would get 50,000 nonsense rows.\r\n\r\nThere are rare real uses for a cross join (for example, listing every student with every club to plan a timetable). Then write `CROSS JOIN` on purpose so readers know you meant it.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** Every query in this chapter runs the same way in the SQL Sandbox (SQLite).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: an unclear column name.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, name\r\nFROM students\r\nJOIN classes ON students.class_id = classes.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1052 (23000): Column 'id' in field list is ambiguous\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Ambiguous** means \"could mean two things\". Both tables have `id` (and `name`).\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** say which table each column comes from.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT students.id, students.name\r\nFROM students\r\nJOIN classes ON students.class_id = classes.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), show each book that is **not yet returned**, with the borrower's name. Columns: `student`, `book`.\r\n\r\n*Hint: start from the three-table query above and add `WHERE l.returned = 'No'`. You should get 5 rows.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A `JOIN` combines rows from linked tables in a result; stored tables do not change.\r\n- Write `JOIN other_table ON child.foreign_key = parent.id`.\r\n- Use `table.column` or short aliases (`students s`) to avoid ambiguous names.\r\n- Chain joins to combine three or more tables.\r\n- Without `ON`, every row pairs with every row (a cross join), giving wrong, huge results.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Why do we need `JOIN` in a database that uses foreign keys?\r\n2. Which keyword gives the matching condition?\r\n3. What is a table alias, and why is it useful?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. The data is split into tables to avoid repeats. A join brings the related pieces back together in one result.\r\n2. `ON`\r\n3. A short nickname for a table in one query, like `s` for `students`. It makes long queries shorter and easier to read.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nUsing aliases `s` and `c`, list every Grade 7 student with their class name and teacher.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name AS student, c.name AS class, c.teacher\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN classes c ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nWHERE s.grade = 7;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student | class | teacher |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Maryam | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| Aisha | Grade 7 Falcons | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA student wrote:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT students.name, books.title\r\nFROM students\r\nJOIN books;\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Explain what is wrong and predict how many rows it returns.\r\n2. Fix it so that it lists who borrowed which book. (Which table must sit in the middle?)\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. There is no `ON`, so it is a cross join: 8 students × 8 books = 64 rows, mostly false. Also, `students` and `books` are not linked directly. The link goes through `loans`.\r\n2.\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT students.name, books.title\r\nFROM loans\r\nJOIN students ON loans.student_id = students.id\r\nJOIN books    ON loans.book_id = books.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis returns the 10 real loans.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-joins-and-relationships/ch17-inner-join.md": "# Chapter 17: The INNER JOIN in Depth\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: An `INNER JOIN` keeps only rows that have a match in **both** tables. A row with no partner on the other side is left out of the result.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nThe librarian wants to send a thank-you card to every student who has borrowed at least one book. She lays the student register beside the borrowing notebook and only writes cards where a name appears in both. Hamza has never borrowed a book, so he gets no card this time. The new calligraphy book has never been borrowed, so it is not mentioned. Only the matches count. That is an inner join.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain which rows an `INNER JOIN` keeps and which it drops.\r\n- See why one row can appear several times after a join.\r\n- Join three or four tables in a chain.\r\n- Filter and sort joined results with `WHERE` and `ORDER BY`.\r\n- Use `DISTINCT` to remove repeats in a joined result.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Only the Matches\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/sql-joins-visual.svg\" alt=\"SQL JOINs Visualized\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nIn the picture, an `INNER JOIN` is the shaded middle: the part where both circles overlap.\r\n\r\n`JOIN` and `INNER JOIN` mean exactly the same thing in MySQL. Writing `INNER` makes your meaning clearer to readers.\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents             loans\r\n┌───────────┐        ┌──────────────┐\r\n│ 1 Zayd    │──match─│ student 1 ×2 │\r\n│ 2 Maryam  │──match─│ student 2    │\r\n│ ...       │        │ ...          │\r\n│ 7 Hamza   │   ✗    │ (none)       │\r\n└───────────┘        └──────────────┘\r\nHamza has no partner → left out\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👀 Watching Rows Appear and Disappear\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.id, s.name, l.book_id\r\nFROM students s\r\nINNER JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nORDER BY s.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | book_id |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\r\n| 1 | Zayd | 1 |\r\n| 1 | Zayd | 2 |\r\n| 2 | Maryam | 4 |\r\n| 3 | Bilal | 2 |\r\n| 3 | Bilal | 7 |\r\n| 4 | Fatima | 6 |\r\n| 5 | Tariq | 3 |\r\n| 6 | Aisha | 1 |\r\n| 8 | Khadija | 5 |\r\n| 8 | Khadija | 6 |\r\n\r\nTwo things to notice:\r\n\r\n1. **Hamza (id 7) is missing.** He has no loans, so there is nothing to match.\r\n2. **Zayd, Bilal and Khadija appear twice.** Each has two loans, and every match makes its own row.\r\n\r\nThe same is true on the books side. *Arabic Calligraphy Basics* (book 8) has never been borrowed, so an inner join of `books` with `loans` will never show it. (Chapter 18 shows how to find such \"missing\" rows.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Join, Then Filter\r\n\r\n`WHERE` can test columns from **any** joined table. Which books are still out, and who has them?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name AS student, b.title AS book, l.loan_date\r\nFROM loans l\r\nINNER JOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nINNER JOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE l.returned = 'No'\r\nORDER BY l.loan_date;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student | book | loan_date |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Tariq | Ethical Technology | 2026-09-12 |\r\n| Zayd | Algorithm Design | 2026-09-20 |\r\n| Bilal | Introduction to Networks | 2026-09-25 |\r\n| Aisha | Clean Code | 2026-09-28 |\r\n| Khadija | Digital Stewardship | 2026-10-01 |\r\n\r\nThis is the librarian's reminder list, oldest loan first.\r\n\r\nFilter on the book's category instead: who borrowed *Computer Science* books?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT b.title, s.name\r\nFROM books b\r\nINNER JOIN loans l    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nINNER JOIN students s ON s.id = l.student_id\r\nWHERE b.category = 'Computer Science'\r\nORDER BY b.title, s.name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | name |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Algorithm Design | Bilal |\r\n| Algorithm Design | Zayd |\r\n| Introduction to Networks | Bilal |\r\n\r\nThe order of the `ON` sides does not matter: `s.id = l.student_id` means the same as `l.student_id = s.id`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗 A Chain of Four Tables\r\n\r\nEach extra `INNER JOIN` adds one more table to the chain. To connect four tables you need three joins. Here we go from a loan to the student, the book **and** the student's class:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, b.title, c.name AS class\r\nFROM loans l\r\nINNER JOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nINNER JOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nINNER JOIN classes c  ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nWHERE c.name = 'Grade 9 Hawks'\r\nORDER BY s.name, b.title;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | title | class |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Bilal | Algorithm Design | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n| Bilal | Introduction to Networks | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n| Khadija | Digital Stewardship | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n| Khadija | Python Data Science | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n| Tariq | Ethical Technology | Grade 9 Hawks |\r\n\r\nFollow the links like stepping stones:\r\n\r\n```\r\nloans ──student_id──► students\r\n  │                      │\r\nbook_id               class_id\r\n  ▼                      ▼\r\nbooks                 classes\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧹 Removing Repeats After a Join\r\n\r\nWhich students have read an *Ethics* book, and who is their teacher? Khadija borrowed *Digital Stewardship* once, but if she had borrowed two Ethics books she would appear twice. `DISTINCT` (Chapter 9) keeps each pair once:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DISTINCT s.name, c.teacher\r\nFROM loans l\r\nJOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nJOIN classes c  ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nJOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE b.category = 'Ethics';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | teacher |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Fatima | Mr. Imran |\r\n| Tariq | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Khadija | Ms. Noor |\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** All queries in this chapter run unchanged in the SQL Sandbox. The sandbox tables have no declared foreign keys, but joins only need matching values, not declared keys.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: expecting everyone to appear.**\r\n\r\nA teacher asks for \"a list of all students and the books they borrowed\". A student writes an inner join and sends it. Hamza is missing, and nobody notices that he has borrowed nothing, which was exactly what the teacher wanted to know.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** when the question says \"**all** students\", an inner join is the wrong tool. Use a `LEFT JOIN` (Chapter 18), which keeps every student.\r\n\r\n**Mistake: joining on the wrong columns.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n... JOIN books b ON l.student_id = b.id\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL does not complain: both are numbers. But it pairs student numbers with book numbers, and the results are nonsense. **Fix:** always match a foreign key with the primary key it points to: `l.book_id = b.id`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), list every loan made by a student from **Lahore**: student name, book title and loan date, newest first.\r\n\r\n*Hint: join `loans`, `students` and `books`, then `WHERE s.city = 'Lahore'`. You should get 5 rows.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `INNER JOIN` (or just `JOIN`) keeps only rows that match on both sides.\r\n- Rows with no partner (Hamza, book 8) disappear from the result.\r\n- A row matching several partners appears several times.\r\n- Chain joins one table at a time; four tables need three joins.\r\n- `WHERE`, `ORDER BY` and `DISTINCT` work on joined results as usual.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. A student has no loans. Will they appear in `students INNER JOIN loans`?\r\n2. A book has never been borrowed. Will it appear in `books INNER JOIN loans`?\r\n3. How many joins do you need to connect four tables?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. No. There is nothing to match.\r\n2. No, for the same reason.\r\n3. Three.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nList every loan with the student's name, the book title and the class teacher, only for books that **have been returned**. Sort by student name.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, b.title, c.teacher\r\nFROM loans l\r\nINNER JOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nINNER JOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nINNER JOIN classes c  ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nWHERE l.returned = 'Yes'\r\nORDER BY s.name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | title | teacher |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Bilal | Algorithm Design | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Fatima | Digital Stewardship | Mr. Imran |\r\n| Khadija | Python Data Science | Ms. Noor |\r\n| Maryam | HTML & CSS Architecture | Ms. Hafsa |\r\n| Zayd | Clean Code | Mr. Imran |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nMs. Noor wants to know which **books** her Grade 9 Hawks have borrowed, each title listed once, A to Z.\r\n\r\n1. Decide which tables you need and how they link.\r\n2. Write the query.\r\n3. Explain why `DISTINCT` matters here.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `classes` → `students` (by `class_id`) → `loans` (by `student_id`) → `books` (by `book_id`).\r\n2.\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT DISTINCT b.title\r\nFROM classes c\r\nINNER JOIN students s ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nINNER JOIN loans l    ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nINNER JOIN books b    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE c.teacher = 'Ms. Noor'\r\nORDER BY b.title;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Algorithm Design |\r\n| Digital Stewardship |\r\n| Ethical Technology |\r\n| Introduction to Networks |\r\n| Python Data Science |\r\n\r\n3. If two Hawks borrowed the same book, it would appear twice. `DISTINCT` keeps each title once.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-joins-and-relationships/ch18-left-and-right-joins.md": "# Chapter 18: LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN & Finding Missing Records\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: A `LEFT JOIN` keeps **every** row from the left table, even when the right table has no match. The missing values show as `NULL`. This also lets you find things that never happened, such as books nobody has borrowed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt the end of term, the head teacher asks for a reading report on **every** student, not just the ones who borrowed books. The librarian copies the whole register first. Then, next to each name, she writes the books borrowed. Next to Hamza's name she writes a dash, because he borrowed nothing. Nobody is left off the report. That dash is `NULL`, and the report is a `LEFT JOIN`.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain how `LEFT JOIN` differs from `INNER JOIN`.\r\n- Read `NULL` values in a joined result.\r\n- Replace `NULL` with a friendly label using `COALESCE`.\r\n- Find \"missing\" rows with `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE ... IS NULL`.\r\n- Know what `RIGHT JOIN` does and why most people flip it into a `LEFT JOIN`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤔 When INNER JOIN Is Not Enough\r\n\r\nIn Chapter 17, the inner join of `students` and `loans` dropped Hamza, because he has no loans. For a report on \"all students\" that is wrong.\r\n\r\nA **`LEFT JOIN`** (full name `LEFT OUTER JOIN`) keeps every row of the table written **before** the word `JOIN` (the *left* table). If there is no match on the right, the right side's columns are filled with `NULL`.\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents (left)       loans (right)\r\n┌───────────┐         ┌──────────────┐\r\n│ 1 Zayd    │──match──│ book 1, 2    │\r\n│ 7 Hamza   │──none───│ NULL         │\r\n│ 8 Khadija │──match──│ book 5, 6    │\r\n└───────────┘         └──────────────┘\r\nevery student is kept\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📋 A LEFT JOIN Step by Step\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.id, s.name, l.book_id, l.loan_date\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nORDER BY s.id, l.loan_date;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name | book_id | loan_date |\r\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Zayd | 1 | 2026-09-02 |\r\n| 1 | Zayd | 2 | 2026-09-20 |\r\n| 2 | Maryam | 4 | 2026-09-05 |\r\n| 3 | Bilal | 2 | 2026-09-08 |\r\n| 3 | Bilal | 7 | 2026-09-25 |\r\n| 4 | Fatima | 6 | 2026-09-10 |\r\n| 5 | Tariq | 3 | 2026-09-12 |\r\n| 6 | Aisha | 1 | 2026-09-28 |\r\n| 7 | Hamza | NULL | NULL |\r\n| 8 | Khadija | 5 | 2026-09-15 |\r\n| 8 | Khadija | 6 | 2026-10-01 |\r\n\r\nAll eight students appear. Hamza is there, with `NULL` where his loan details would be. Students with two loans still appear twice, just like in an inner join.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏷️ Friendly Labels with `COALESCE`\r\n\r\n`NULL` looks unfriendly on a report. `COALESCE(a, b)` gives back `a` if it has a value, otherwise `b`. (It returns the first value in its list that is not `NULL`.)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, COALESCE(l.loan_date, 'No loans yet') AS loan_date\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE s.id IN (6, 7)\r\nORDER BY s.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | loan_date |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Aisha | 2026-09-28 |\r\n| Hamza | No loans yet |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Finding What Is Missing\r\n\r\nThis is the most useful trick in the chapter. After a `LEFT JOIN`, the rows with **no match** are exactly the rows where the right side is `NULL`. So filter for them.\r\n\r\n### Students who have never borrowed a book\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.id, s.name\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE l.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Hamza |\r\n\r\nThe librarian could invite Hamza to choose his first book.\r\n\r\n### Books that have never been borrowed\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT b.id, b.title\r\nFROM books b\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE l.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | title |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 8 | Arabic Calligraphy Basics |\r\n\r\nThis pattern is often called an **anti-join**: \"rows in A with **no** partner in B\". We test `l.id`, the right table's primary key, because it is never `NULL` in a real loan row. If it is `NULL`, there was no match.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ↔️ What About `RIGHT JOIN`?\r\n\r\nA `RIGHT JOIN` keeps every row of the **right** table instead. These two queries give the same rows:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- right join: keep every book\r\nSELECT b.title, l.id\r\nFROM loans l\r\nRIGHT JOIN books b ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE l.id IS NULL;\r\n\r\n-- the same idea as a left join: just swap the tables\r\nSELECT b.title, l.id\r\nFROM books b\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE l.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | id |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics | NULL |\r\n\r\nMost developers use `LEFT JOIN` only. Reading \"keep everything from the first table I name\" is easier than switching sides in your head.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite supports `RIGHT JOIN` only from version 3.39. If your tool rejects it, swap the two tables and use `LEFT JOIN`, as shown above. `COALESCE` and `LEFT JOIN` work everywhere.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: a `WHERE` on the right table turns a LEFT JOIN back into an INNER JOIN.**\r\n\r\nWe want every student, plus the book they still have at home (if any):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, l.book_id\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE l.returned = 'No'\r\nORDER BY s.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | book_id |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 2 |\r\n| Bilal | 7 |\r\n| Tariq | 3 |\r\n| Aisha | 1 |\r\n| Khadija | 6 |\r\n\r\nMaryam, Fatima and Hamza vanished. For them `l.returned` is `NULL` (or `'Yes'`), and `NULL = 'No'` is not true, so `WHERE` threw them out.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** move the test into the `ON` part. Then it decides which loans *match*, without removing students:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, l.book_id\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id AND l.returned = 'No'\r\nORDER BY s.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | book_id |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 2 |\r\n| Maryam | NULL |\r\n| Bilal | 7 |\r\n| Fatima | NULL |\r\n| Tariq | 3 |\r\n| Aisha | 1 |\r\n| Hamza | NULL |\r\n| Khadija | 6 |\r\n\r\nThe one exception is `WHERE right_table.id IS NULL`, which you add on purpose to find missing rows.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), list **every book** with the name of each student who borrowed it. Books nobody borrowed should still appear, with `'Not borrowed yet'` instead of a name.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `books b LEFT JOIN loans l ... LEFT JOIN students s ...`, and `COALESCE(s.name, 'Not borrowed yet')`. You should get 11 rows.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `LEFT JOIN` keeps every row of the left table; unmatched right columns become `NULL`.\r\n- `COALESCE(value, fallback)` swaps a `NULL` for a friendly label.\r\n- `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL` finds rows with no partner (an anti-join).\r\n- `RIGHT JOIN` keeps every row of the right table; swapping tables into a `LEFT JOIN` is usually clearer.\r\n- Put tests on the right table in `ON`, not `WHERE`, or the extra rows disappear.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does a `LEFT JOIN` do with a left-table row that has no match?\r\n2. What value fills the right-table columns in that case?\r\n3. What does `COALESCE` do?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It keeps the row anyway.\r\n2. `NULL`.\r\n3. It returns the first value in its list that is not `NULL`, for example `COALESCE(name, 'Unknown')`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite a query that finds every **class** that has no students. Then predict the result on our data.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT c.id, c.name\r\nFROM classes c\r\nLEFT JOIN students s ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nWHERE s.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn our data every class has students, so the result is `Empty set`. If a new class \"Grade 6 Doves\" were added with no students, it would appear.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe library wants an \"every book\" report.\r\n\r\n1. Show every book title and the borrower's name, with `'Not borrowed yet'` for books with no loans. Sort by book id.\r\n2. Explain why an `INNER JOIN` would give the wrong report.\r\n3. Explain why this report is useful for the library (think about which books to promote).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT b.title, COALESCE(s.name, 'Not borrowed yet') AS borrower\r\nFROM books b\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l    ON l.book_id = b.id\r\nLEFT JOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nORDER BY b.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe result has 11 rows. The last one is `Arabic Calligraphy Basics | Not borrowed yet`. (Books borrowed twice, like *Clean Code*, appear twice.)\r\n\r\n2. An `INNER JOIN` drops *Arabic Calligraphy Basics*, the very book the librarian needs to notice.\r\n3. Books nobody borrows may need a display, a mention in assembly, or a reading club, so the library's money is well used.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-advanced-queries/ch19-aggregate-functions.md": "# Chapter 19: Aggregate Functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX)\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: An aggregate function looks at many rows and gives back **one** summary number: how many, the total, the average, the smallest or the largest.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAfter the charity week, the club leader does not read out all eight donation slips at assembly. She says: \"We received 8 gifts, 27,500 rupees in total. The biggest gift was 8,000 rupees.\" Each of those sentences squeezes a whole pile of slips into one number. In SQL, the tools that do this squeezing are called aggregate functions.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Count rows with `COUNT(*)`, `COUNT(column)` and `COUNT(DISTINCT column)`.\r\n- Add up a column with `SUM`.\r\n- Work out an average with `AVG` and tidy it with `ROUND`.\r\n- Find the smallest and largest values with `MIN` and `MAX`.\r\n- Combine several aggregates and a `WHERE` in one summary query.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧮 The Five Aggregate Functions\r\n\r\n| Function | Gives back | Example question |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `COUNT()` | how many rows (or values) | How many students are there? |\r\n| `SUM()` | the total of a number column | How much was donated? |\r\n| `AVG()` | the average (mean) | What is the average GPA? |\r\n| `MIN()` | the smallest or earliest value | Which book is shortest? |\r\n| `MAX()` | the largest or latest value | When was the last gift? |\r\n\r\n```\r\npages of 8 books\r\n464, 320, 210, 288,\r\n352, 180, 240, 120\r\n        │\r\n        ▼  MAX(pages)\r\n       464   (one row, one value)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe **average** (or *mean*) is the total divided by how many values there are.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔢 Counting with `COUNT`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS students FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| students |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 8 |\r\n\r\n`COUNT(*)` counts rows. Combine it with `WHERE` to count only some of them. How many books are still out?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS not_returned\r\nFROM loans\r\nWHERE returned = 'No';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| not_returned |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 5 |\r\n\r\nHow many **different** cities do students come from?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT city) AS cities FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cities |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 4 |\r\n\r\n### `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(column)`\r\n\r\n`COUNT(column)` skips `NULL` values. Suppose Yusuf joins with no city (as in Chapter 8):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_, COUNT(city) AS with_city FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| rows_ | with_city |\r\n| ---: | ---: |\r\n| 9 | 8 |\r\n\r\nNine students, but only eight have a city on record.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ➕ Totals with `SUM`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT SUM(amount) AS total FROM donations;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| total |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 27500 |\r\n\r\nOnly the *Clean Water* cause:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT SUM(amount) AS clean_water FROM donations\r\nWHERE cause = 'Clean Water';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| clean_water |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 15000 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ Averages with `AVG` and `ROUND`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT AVG(gpa) AS average_gpa FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| average_gpa |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 3.668750 |\r\n\r\nThat is a lot of digits. `ROUND(value, 2)` keeps 2 decimal places:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS average_gpa FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| average_gpa |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 3.67 |\r\n\r\n`AVG` ignores `NULL` values. A student with no GPA does not pull the average down to zero.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** MySQL prints `AVG(amount)` on donations as `3437.5000` (4 decimals). SQLite prints `3437.5`, and shows `AVG(gpa)` as `3.66875`. The value is the same; only the display differs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📏 Smallest and Largest: `MIN` and `MAX`\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT MIN(pages) AS shortest, MAX(pages) AS longest FROM books;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| shortest | longest |\r\n| ---: | ---: |\r\n| 120 | 464 |\r\n\r\nThey work on dates too (earliest and latest):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT MIN(donated_on) AS first_gift, MAX(donated_on) AS last_gift\r\nFROM donations;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| first_gift | last_gift |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| 2026-09-01 | 2026-10-02 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📊 A Summary Card in One Query\r\n\r\nYou can put several aggregates in one `SELECT`. Here is a library summary card:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*)              AS books,\r\n       SUM(pages)            AS total_pages,\r\n       MIN(pages)            AS shortest,\r\n       MAX(pages)            AS longest,\r\n       ROUND(AVG(pages), 1)  AS average\r\nFROM books;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| books | total_pages | shortest | longest | average |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| 8 | 2174 | 120 | 464 | 271.8 |\r\n\r\nAggregates also work after a join. How many loans were made by students from Lahore?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS lahore_loans\r\nFROM loans l\r\nJOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE s.city = 'Lahore';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| lahore_loans |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 5 |\r\n\r\n### When nothing matches\r\n\r\nIf `WHERE` keeps no rows, `COUNT` gives `0`, but `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` give `NULL` (there is nothing to add up):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS gifts, SUM(amount) AS total\r\nFROM donations WHERE cause = 'Space Camp';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| gifts | total |\r\n| ---: | ---: |\r\n| 0 | NULL |\r\n\r\nUse `COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)` (Chapter 18) if you want `0` instead.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: mixing a plain column with an aggregate.** \"Who has the highest GPA?\"\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, MAX(gpa) FROM students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | MAX(gpa) |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 3.95 |\r\n\r\nWrong! Bilal has 3.95, not Zayd. `MAX` squeezes all rows into one, and MySQL just picks a name from some row. (Strict servers refuse this query instead.)\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** sort and take one row (Chapter 11):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, gpa FROM students ORDER BY gpa DESC LIMIT 1;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Bilal | 3.95 |\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: an aggregate inside `WHERE`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE gpa > AVG(gpa);\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1111 (HY000): Invalid use of group function\r\n```\r\n\r\n`WHERE` checks one row at a time, but `AVG` needs all rows. Chapter 22 shows the fix: a subquery.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), make a summary for the *Books* cause: number of gifts, total, and average rounded to a whole number.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `COUNT(*)`, `SUM(amount)`, `ROUND(AVG(amount))` with `WHERE cause = 'Books'`. The total should be 5500.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Aggregates turn many rows into one value.\r\n- `COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(column)` skips `NULL`; `COUNT(DISTINCT column)` counts different values.\r\n- `SUM` totals, `AVG` averages (use `ROUND` to tidy), `MIN`/`MAX` find extremes, also for dates.\r\n- `WHERE` filters rows **before** they are summarised.\r\n- Do not mix plain columns with aggregates (that needs `GROUP BY`, Chapter 20), and do not put aggregates in `WHERE`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Name the five aggregate functions.\r\n2. What is the difference between `COUNT(*)` and `COUNT(city)`?\r\n3. What does `ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2)` do?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`.\r\n2. `COUNT(*)` counts every row. `COUNT(city)` counts only rows where `city` is not `NULL`.\r\n3. It works out the average GPA and rounds it to 2 decimal places (3.67).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. Find the total amount given to the *Food Pantry* cause.\r\n2. Find how many loans there are, the first loan date and the latest loan date.\r\n3. Find the average number of pages of *Ethics* books.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT SUM(amount) FROM donations WHERE cause = 'Food Pantry';\r\n-- 7000\r\n\r\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS loans, MIN(loan_date) AS first, MAX(loan_date) AS latest\r\nFROM loans;\r\n-- 10 | 2026-09-02 | 2026-10-01\r\n\r\nSELECT AVG(pages) FROM books WHERE category = 'Ethics';\r\n-- 195.0000  (210 + 180 = 390, ÷ 2 = 195)\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a \"charity dashboard\" query for the club noticeboard.\r\n\r\n1. In one query, show the number of gifts, the total, the largest gift, the smallest gift, and the average rounded to whole rupees.\r\n2. Explain why `SELECT donor, MAX(amount) FROM donations;` is **not** a safe way to find the biggest donor, and write a correct query.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT COUNT(*)            AS gifts,\r\n       SUM(amount)         AS total,\r\n       MAX(amount)         AS largest,\r\n       MIN(amount)         AS smallest,\r\n       ROUND(AVG(amount))  AS average\r\nFROM donations;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| gifts | total | largest | smallest | average |\r\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| 8 | 27500 | 8000 | 1000 | 3438 |\r\n\r\n2. `MAX(amount)` gives 8000, but `donor` comes from some row MySQL picks, not necessarily Yusuf's. Correct:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, amount FROM donations ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 1;\r\n-- Yusuf | 8000\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-advanced-queries/ch20-group-by-and-having.md": "# Chapter 20: GROUP BY & HAVING Clauses\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `GROUP BY` sorts rows into groups that share a value, then runs an aggregate (like `COUNT` or `SUM`) once for each group. `HAVING` filters those groups. `WHERE` filters single rows before the grouping.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAfter charity week, the club pours all the donation slips onto a table. Then they make three piles: one for *Books*, one for *Clean Water*, one for *Food Pantry*. They count and add up each pile separately. Finally, they decide to announce only the causes that raised more than 6,000 rupees. Making piles is `GROUP BY`. Adding up each pile is the aggregate. Choosing which piles to announce is `HAVING`.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Group rows with `GROUP BY` and summarise each group.\r\n- Group by more than one column.\r\n- Filter groups with `HAVING`.\r\n- Explain the difference between `WHERE` and `HAVING`.\r\n- Put the parts of a query in the right order, and know the order MySQL runs them.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧺 The Grouping Idea\r\n\r\n<div align=\"center\" style=\"margin: 2rem 0;\">\r\n  <img src=\"../assets/diagrams/mysql/group-by-aggregation.svg\" alt=\"How GROUP BY & Aggregations Work\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);\">\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nIn Chapter 19, `COUNT(*)` told us there are 8 students in total. But how many are in **each grade**?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT grade, COUNT(*) AS students\r\nFROM students\r\nGROUP BY grade\r\nORDER BY grade;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| grade | students |\r\n| ---: | ---: |\r\n| 7 | 2 |\r\n| 8 | 3 |\r\n| 9 | 3 |\r\n\r\nWhat MySQL does:\r\n\r\n```\r\nall 8 students\r\n   │ GROUP BY grade\r\n   ├─ grade 7: Maryam, Aisha    → 2\r\n   ├─ grade 8: Zayd, Fatima,\r\n   │           Hamza            → 3\r\n   └─ grade 9: Bilal, Tariq,\r\n               Khadija          → 3\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne result row per group. The aggregate runs inside each group.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📊 Grouping in Practice\r\n\r\n### Donations by cause\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT cause,\r\n       COUNT(*)    AS gifts,\r\n       SUM(amount) AS total,\r\n       MAX(amount) AS largest\r\nFROM donations\r\nGROUP BY cause\r\nORDER BY total DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cause | gifts | total | largest |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Clean Water | 3 | 15000 | 8000 |\r\n| Food Pantry | 2 | 7000 | 4500 |\r\n| Books | 3 | 5500 | 3000 |\r\n\r\nYou can sort by an alias such as `total`.\r\n\r\n### Students by city\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT city, COUNT(*) AS students, ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS avg_gpa\r\nFROM students\r\nGROUP BY city\r\nORDER BY students DESC, city;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| city | students | avg_gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Lahore | 3 | 3.75 |\r\n| Islamabad | 2 | 3.78 |\r\n| Karachi | 2 | 3.68 |\r\n| Peshawar | 1 | 3.20 |\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite stores GPA as an approximate number, so it shows Karachi as `3.67` and Peshawar as `3.2`. MySQL's exact `DECIMAL` gives `3.68` and `3.20`.\r\n\r\n### Loans per student (with a join)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, COUNT(*) AS loans\r\nFROM loans l\r\nJOIN students s ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nGROUP BY s.id, s.name\r\nORDER BY loans DESC, s.name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | loans |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Bilal | 2 |\r\n| Khadija | 2 |\r\n| Zayd | 2 |\r\n| Aisha | 1 |\r\n| Fatima | 1 |\r\n| Maryam | 1 |\r\n| Tariq | 1 |\r\n\r\nWe group by `s.id` **and** `s.name`. The id keeps two students with the same name apart.\r\n\r\nHamza is missing, because the inner join drops him. With a `LEFT JOIN` and `COUNT(l.id)` he appears with 0. (`COUNT(l.id)` skips the `NULL`, while `COUNT(*)` would wrongly count his empty row as 1.)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.name, COUNT(l.id) AS loans\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nGROUP BY s.id, s.name\r\nORDER BY s.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | loans |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Zayd | 2 |\r\n| Maryam | 1 |\r\n| Bilal | 2 |\r\n| Fatima | 1 |\r\n| Tariq | 1 |\r\n| Aisha | 1 |\r\n| Hamza | 0 |\r\n| Khadija | 2 |\r\n\r\n### Two columns\r\n\r\n`GROUP BY grade, city` makes one group for each **pair** of values. In our small school each pair has only one student, so every count is 1. In a big school this would show, for example, how many Grade 8 students live in Lahore.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🚦 Filtering Groups with `HAVING`\r\n\r\nWhich causes raised more than 6,000 rupees?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\nFROM donations\r\nGROUP BY cause\r\nHAVING SUM(amount) > 6000;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cause | total |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Water | 15000 |\r\n| Food Pantry | 7000 |\r\n\r\n*Books* (5500) was a group too, but it failed the `HAVING` test.\r\n\r\n### `WHERE` and `HAVING` together\r\n\r\n| Clause | Runs | Filters |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `WHERE` | **before** grouping | single rows |\r\n| `HAVING` | **after** grouping | whole groups |\r\n\r\nOnly gifts from 10 September onwards, grouped by cause, and only causes with at least 2 such gifts:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\nFROM donations\r\nWHERE donated_on >= '2026-09-10'\r\nGROUP BY cause\r\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2\r\nORDER BY total DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cause | total |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Water | 10000 |\r\n| Books | 4000 |\r\n\r\nStep by step: `WHERE` removes the early gifts (Ahmad, Sara, Ibrahim). The remaining five are grouped. *Food Pantry* has only one late gift (Zainab), so `HAVING` drops it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧭 The Order of the Parts\r\n\r\nYou **write** the parts in this order:\r\n\r\n```\r\nSELECT … FROM … JOIN … WHERE …\r\nGROUP BY … HAVING … ORDER BY … LIMIT …\r\n```\r\n\r\nMySQL **runs** them in a different order:\r\n\r\n1. `FROM` and `JOIN`: find the rows.\r\n2. `WHERE`: keep only some rows.\r\n3. `GROUP BY`: make the groups.\r\n4. `HAVING`: keep only some groups.\r\n5. `SELECT`: work out the columns and aliases.\r\n6. `ORDER BY`: sort.\r\n7. `LIMIT`: cut to size.\r\n\r\nThis explains the rules: `WHERE` cannot use `COUNT(*)`, because the groups do not exist yet at step 2.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: an aggregate in `WHERE`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT grade, COUNT(*) FROM students\r\nWHERE COUNT(*) > 2\r\nGROUP BY grade;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1111 (HY000): Invalid use of group function\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** move the test to `HAVING`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT grade, COUNT(*) FROM students\r\nGROUP BY grade\r\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 2;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis returns grades 8 and 9 (3 students each).\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: a plain column that is not grouped.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT city, name, COUNT(*) FROM students GROUP BY city;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| city | name | COUNT(*) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\r\n| Lahore | Zayd | 3 |\r\n\r\nA relaxed XAMPP shows *one* name per city, picked by the server. Lahore has three students, so \"Zayd\" is misleading. A strict server (`ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY` mode) refuses: `ERROR 1055: 'school_db.students.name' isn't in GROUP BY`.\r\n\r\n**Rule:** every column in `SELECT` must either be in `GROUP BY` or be inside an aggregate.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), show each book **category** with its number of titles and total pages, A to Z.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `GROUP BY category`. You should get 6 rows; Computer Science has 2 titles and 560 pages.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `GROUP BY column` makes one group per value and one result row per group.\r\n- Aggregates (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`) run inside each group.\r\n- `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\r\n- Every selected column must be grouped or aggregated.\r\n- Write order: `SELECT … FROM … WHERE … GROUP BY … HAVING … ORDER BY … LIMIT`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does `GROUP BY` do?\r\n2. What is the difference between `WHERE` and `HAVING`?\r\n3. Can you write `WHERE COUNT(*) > 5`? What should you write instead?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It puts rows with the same value into groups, so aggregates are worked out once per group.\r\n2. `WHERE` filters single rows before grouping. `HAVING` filters groups after grouping.\r\n3. No (error 1111). Use `HAVING COUNT(*) > 5` after `GROUP BY`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. Show how many loans are returned and how many are not (`returned` = Yes / No).\r\n2. Show each class name with the number of loans made by its students.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT returned, COUNT(*) AS loans\r\nFROM loans\r\nGROUP BY returned;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| returned | loans |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| No | 5 |\r\n| Yes | 5 |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT c.name AS class, COUNT(l.id) AS loans\r\nFROM classes c\r\nJOIN students s ON s.class_id = c.id\r\nLEFT JOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nGROUP BY c.id, c.name\r\nORDER BY c.id;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| class | loans |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Grade 7 Falcons | 2 |\r\n| Grade 8 Eagles | 3 |\r\n| Grade 9 Hawks | 5 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThis query has an error. Explain it and fix it. Then extend it: show only cities whose students have an average GPA of **at least 3.7**, best first.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT city, AVG(gpa)\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE AVG(gpa) >= 3.7\r\nGROUP BY city;\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`AVG` cannot be used in `WHERE`, because `WHERE` runs before the groups exist (error 1111). Use `HAVING`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT city, ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS avg_gpa\r\nFROM students\r\nGROUP BY city\r\nHAVING AVG(gpa) >= 3.7\r\nORDER BY avg_gpa DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| city | avg_gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Islamabad | 3.78 |\r\n| Lahore | 3.75 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-advanced-queries/ch21-like-and-wildcards.md": "# Chapter 21: Pattern Matching — LIKE & Wildcards\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: `LIKE` searches for text that fits a pattern. In a pattern, `%` stands for \"any number of characters (even none)\" and `_` stands for \"exactly one character\".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA Grade 7 student comes to the library desk. \"I want that book about data… I think *data* was somewhere in the title.\" The librarian does not need the exact title. She runs her finger down the catalogue looking for any title that has \"data\" anywhere in it. `LIKE` lets the database do that same kind of \"I only remember part of it\" search.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain why `=` is not enough for searching.\r\n- Use `%` to match \"starts with\", \"ends with\" and \"contains\".\r\n- Use `_` to match an exact number of characters.\r\n- Know when `LIKE` is case-insensitive.\r\n- Search for a real `%` or `_` with `ESCAPE`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔎 Why `=` Is Not Enough\r\n\r\n`=` needs a perfect match:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name = 'Zay';\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nEmpty set\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo student is called exactly \"Zay\". A search box on a website must also find \"Zayd\" when someone types only part of the name. That is the job of `LIKE`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🃏 Wildcard 1: `%` (Any Number of Characters)\r\n\r\nA **wildcard** is a symbol that stands in for other characters, like a joker in a word puzzle.\r\n\r\n### Starts with\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name LIKE 'Z%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Zayd |\r\n\r\n`'Z%'` means \"Z, then anything (or nothing)\".\r\n\r\n### Ends with\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE '%Design';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Algorithm Design |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, city FROM students WHERE city LIKE '%abad';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | city |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Fatima | Islamabad |\r\n| Khadija | Islamabad |\r\n\r\n### Contains\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE '%data%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Python Data Science |\r\n\r\n`%` on **both** sides means \"data can be anywhere\". It also found \"Data\" with a capital D (see the case section below).\r\n\r\n| Pattern | Meaning | Example match |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `'A%'` | starts with A | Algorithm Design |\r\n| `'%a'` | ends with a | Sara, Hafsa, Layla |\r\n| `'%net%'` | contains \"net\" | Introduction to Networks |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🃏 Wildcard 2: `_` (Exactly One Character)\r\n\r\nEach `_` matches exactly **one** character, no more and no less.\r\n\r\nNames whose **second** letter is \"a\":\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name LIKE '_a%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Zayd |\r\n| Maryam |\r\n| Fatima |\r\n| Tariq |\r\n| Hamza |\r\n\r\nNames with exactly **five** letters (five underscores):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name LIKE '_____';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Bilal |\r\n| Tariq |\r\n| Aisha |\r\n| Hamza |\r\n\r\n`_` is handy for codes with a fixed shape, for example `'CS-___'` matches `CS-101` but not `CS-1001`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ➖ `NOT LIKE`\r\n\r\n`NOT LIKE` keeps rows that do **not** fit the pattern. Titles without the letter \"e\":\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title NOT LIKE '%e%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics |\r\n\r\n`LIKE` also works with `AND`, `OR`, `ORDER BY` and `GROUP BY`, just like `=`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔠 Upper and Lower Case\r\n\r\nWith the usual MySQL settings, `LIKE` **ignores case**. The collation's `_ci` ending (Chapter 4) means *case-insensitive*:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name LIKE 'aisha';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Aisha |\r\n\r\nFor a case-sensitive search, add `BINARY`, which compares the exact bytes:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE BINARY name LIKE 'aisha';\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nEmpty set\r\n```\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite's `LIKE` also ignores case for English letters (A–Z), so these examples behave the same. SQLite has no `BINARY` keyword; it uses `GLOB` for case-sensitive patterns instead.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛡️ Searching for a Real `%` or `_`\r\n\r\nSuppose the library adds a science booklet, *Save 20% Water at Home*, and also *Room 205 Lab Notes*. We want titles containing \"20%\":\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE '%20%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Save 20% Water at Home |\r\n| Room 205 Lab Notes |\r\n\r\nRoom 205 matched too! The `%` after 20 was read as a wildcard. To mean a **real** percent sign, **escape** it. \"Escape\" means \"treat the next character as plain text\". Choose an escape character with `ESCAPE`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE '%20!%%' ESCAPE '!';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Save 20% Water at Home |\r\n\r\nRead `'%20!%%'` as: anything, then `20`, then a real `%` (because of the `!`), then anything.\r\n\r\nMySQL also accepts a backslash without the `ESCAPE` part: `LIKE '%20\\%%'`. The `ESCAPE` form works in SQLite too, so it is the safer habit.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: using `=` with wildcards.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title = '%Design';\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nEmpty set\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith `=`, the `%` is just a percent sign. No title literally starts with \"%\". **Fix:** `WHERE title LIKE '%Design'`.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: forgetting the `%`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE 'Data';\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis returns nothing: without wildcards, `LIKE` acts like `=`. **Fix:** `LIKE '%Data%'`.\r\n\r\n> 💡 **Speed note:** `LIKE 'Clean%'` can use an index (a sorted lookup list, like a book's index) because the start is known. `LIKE '%Clean%'` must check every row, which is slower on very big tables. For our small tables you will not notice.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), find every donor whose name **ends with \"a\"** and show how much they gave, largest first.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `LIKE '%a'` with `ORDER BY amount DESC`. You should see Sara, Hafsa and Layla.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `LIKE` matches text against a pattern; `=` needs an exact match.\r\n- `%` = any number of characters (even zero); `_` = exactly one character.\r\n- `'x%'` starts with, `'%x'` ends with, `'%x%'` contains.\r\n- With the usual `_ci` collation, `LIKE` ignores case; `BINARY` makes it exact.\r\n- Use `ESCAPE` to search for a real `%` or `_`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does `%` match?\r\n2. What does `_` match?\r\n3. Which operator do you use with wildcards instead of `=`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Any number of characters, including none.\r\n2. Exactly one character.\r\n3. `LIKE` (or `NOT LIKE`).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. Find books whose title starts with \"Introduction\".\r\n2. Find students whose name contains the letters \"ar\".\r\n3. Find donations whose cause has exactly 5 characters.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE title LIKE 'Introduction%';\r\n-- Introduction to Networks\r\n\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE name LIKE '%ar%';\r\n-- Maryam, Tariq\r\n\r\nSELECT donor, cause FROM donations WHERE cause LIKE '_____';\r\n-- Ahmad, Hafsa, Omar (cause: Books)\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe library website gets a search box. A visitor types `tech`.\r\n\r\n1. Write the query that finds every book whose title **or** category contains the search word.\r\n2. Extend it: count how many matching books there are in each category.\r\n3. Explain why the website must not paste the visitor's text straight into the SQL (think *Amānah*).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, category FROM books\r\nWHERE title LIKE '%tech%' OR category LIKE '%tech%';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | category |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Ethical Technology | Ethics |\r\n\r\nYou might guess *HTML & CSS Architecture* would match too. It does not: \"Archi**tec**ture\" has \"tec\" but no \"tech\". Run your query to test guesses like this.\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT category, COUNT(*) AS books\r\nFROM books\r\nWHERE title LIKE '%tech%' OR category LIKE '%tech%'\r\nGROUP BY category;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| category | books |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Ethics | 1 |\r\n\r\n3. A visitor could type SQL code instead of a word and change or steal data (this attack is called *SQL injection*). Websites must pass the search word as a separate value, using **prepared statements** in PHP. Protecting the school's data is an *Amānah*.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-advanced-queries/ch22-subqueries.md": "# Chapter 22: Introduction to Subqueries\r\n\r\n> **Key Idea**: A subquery is a `SELECT` written inside another query, in brackets. The inner query runs first, and the outer query uses its answer.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nThe head teacher says: \"Give a certificate to every student whose GPA is above the school average.\" The office helper cannot do this in one step. First she works out the average on a scrap of paper. Then she goes down the list and picks everyone above that number. A subquery puts that scrap-of-paper step **inside** the main query, so SQL does both steps for you.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a subquery is and which part runs first.\r\n- Compare a value with a one-number subquery (like an average).\r\n- Use a list subquery with `IN` and `NOT IN`.\r\n- Build a derived table: a subquery in `FROM`, with a name.\r\n- Decide when a JOIN or a subquery reads more clearly.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ❓ Why Subqueries?\r\n\r\nIn Chapter 19 this failed:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students WHERE gpa > AVG(gpa);\r\n-- ERROR 1111: Invalid use of group function\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe could do it in two steps by hand:\r\n\r\n1. `SELECT ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) FROM students;` → **3.67**\r\n2. `SELECT name, gpa FROM students WHERE gpa > 3.67;`\r\n\r\nBut the number 3.67 is now typed into the query. When a new student joins, the real average changes and our query is out of date.\r\n\r\nA **subquery** (or *inner query*) puts step 1 inside step 2:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name, gpa\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE gpa > (SELECT AVG(gpa) FROM students)\r\nORDER BY gpa DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name | gpa |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Bilal | 3.95 |\r\n| Zayd | 3.90 |\r\n| Fatima | 3.85 |\r\n| Maryam | 3.75 |\r\n| Khadija | 3.70 |\r\n\r\n```\r\n1. inner: SELECT AVG(gpa) ...\r\n          → 3.66875\r\n2. outer: WHERE gpa > 3.66875\r\n          → 5 students\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe brackets are required. The inner query is worked out first, and its answer drops into the outer query like a number you typed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1️⃣ One-Value Subqueries\r\n\r\nA subquery that returns **one row and one column** can be used anywhere a single value fits: with `=`, `>`, `<` and so on.\r\n\r\nThe longest book in the library:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages\r\nFROM books\r\nWHERE pages = (SELECT MAX(pages) FROM books);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Code | 464 |\r\n\r\nGifts larger than the average gift:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT donor, amount\r\nFROM donations\r\nWHERE amount > (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM donations)\r\nORDER BY amount DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| donor | amount |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Yusuf | 8000 |\r\n| Sara | 5000 |\r\n| Zainab | 4500 |\r\n\r\n(The average is 3437.5, so Hafsa's 3000 is just below.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📋 List Subqueries with `IN`\r\n\r\nSometimes the inner query returns **many** rows. Then use `IN` (Chapter 10), which checks \"is the value somewhere in this list?\"\r\n\r\nWhich students still have a book at home? The inner query lists their ids:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT student_id FROM loans WHERE returned = 'No';\r\n```\r\n\r\n| student_id |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 1 |\r\n| 3 |\r\n| 5 |\r\n| 6 |\r\n| 8 |\r\n\r\nPut it inside:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE id IN (SELECT student_id FROM loans WHERE returned = 'No')\r\nORDER BY name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Aisha |\r\n| Bilal |\r\n| Khadija |\r\n| Tariq |\r\n| Zayd |\r\n\r\n### `NOT IN`: who never borrowed?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT id, name\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT student_id FROM loans);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| id | name |\r\n| ---: | :--- |\r\n| 7 | Hamza |\r\n\r\nAnd books nobody has borrowed:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books\r\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT book_id FROM loans);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics |\r\n\r\nThese are the same answers as the `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL` trick in Chapter 18, written in a different way.\r\n\r\n> ⚠️ If the inner list ever contains a `NULL`, `NOT IN` returns **no rows at all**, because SQL cannot be sure the value is \"not in\" an unknown. Our `loans.student_id` has no `NULL`s. If yours might, add `WHERE student_id IS NOT NULL` inside the subquery.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧱 Subqueries in `FROM`: Derived Tables\r\n\r\nSo far the inner query gave us a number or a list. It can also give a whole **table**. You can then read from that table in the outer query, just like a real one.\r\n\r\nThink of it as building a small table on a whiteboard first, then asking questions about the whiteboard.\r\n\r\n### Step 1: build the small table\r\n\r\nTotals per cause (Chapter 20):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\nFROM donations\r\nGROUP BY cause;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cause | total |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Books | 5500 |\r\n| Clean Water | 15000 |\r\n| Food Pantry | 7000 |\r\n\r\n### Step 2: put it in brackets, give it a name, and query it\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT t.cause, t.total\r\nFROM (\r\n    SELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\n    FROM donations\r\n    GROUP BY cause\r\n) AS t\r\nWHERE t.total > 6000\r\nORDER BY t.total DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| cause | total |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Water | 15000 |\r\n| Food Pantry | 7000 |\r\n\r\n```\r\ninner query → whiteboard table \"t\"\r\n┌─────────────┬───────┐\r\n│ cause       │ total │\r\n│ Books       │  5500 │\r\n│ Clean Water │ 15000 │\r\n│ Food Pantry │  7000 │\r\n└─────────────┴───────┘\r\nouter query: FROM t WHERE total > 6000\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis temporary table is called a **derived table** (it is *derived*, or made, from other data). Two rules:\r\n\r\n1. It must be inside brackets.\r\n2. It **must have a name** (`AS t`). The outer query needs a name to talk about it.\r\n\r\n(You could also get this answer with `HAVING`. Derived tables shine when you need a second round of maths on top of a summary.)\r\n\r\n### A second round of maths\r\n\r\n\"On average, how many books does a borrowing student take out?\" First count loans per student, then average those counts:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT ROUND(AVG(t.loans), 2) AS avg_loans\r\nFROM (\r\n    SELECT student_id, COUNT(*) AS loans\r\n    FROM loans\r\n    GROUP BY student_id\r\n) AS t;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| avg_loans |\r\n| ---: |\r\n| 1.43 |\r\n\r\nThe inner table has 7 rows (counts 2, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 2). Their average is 10 ÷ 7 ≈ 1.43. You cannot write `AVG(COUNT(*))` directly, so the derived table does the first round.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite runs all subqueries in this chapter. It even lets you skip the `AS t` name, but MySQL does not, so always include it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ Subqueries vs JOINs\r\n\r\nMany questions can be answered either way. \"Students with a book still out\":\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- subquery\r\nSELECT name FROM students\r\nWHERE id IN (SELECT student_id FROM loans WHERE returned = 'No');\r\n\r\n-- join\r\nSELECT DISTINCT s.name\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN loans l ON l.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE l.returned = 'No';\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth return the same 5 names.\r\n\r\n- A **subquery** reads like steps: \"first find the ids, then the names\".\r\n- A **JOIN** is needed when you want to **show columns from both tables** (for example the book title too).\r\n- Speed is often the same. Modern database engines frequently rewrite one form into the other. Choose the one that is clearest to read.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: a one-value comparison with a many-row subquery.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT name FROM students\r\nWHERE gpa > (SELECT gpa FROM students WHERE city = 'Lahore');\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nERROR 1242 (21000): Subquery returns more than 1 row\r\n```\r\n\r\nThree Lahore students means three GPAs. `>` cannot compare with three numbers at once. **Fix:** make the inner query return one value, for example `(SELECT MAX(gpa) FROM students WHERE city = 'Lahore')`, or use `IN` if you meant \"equal to one of them\".\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: a derived table with no name.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT * FROM (SELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\n               FROM donations GROUP BY cause)\r\nWHERE total > 6000;\r\n```\r\n\r\nMariaDB answers `ERROR 1064 ... near 'WHERE total > 6000'`; MySQL 8 says `ERROR 1248: Every derived table must have its own alias`. **Fix:** add `AS t` after the closing bracket.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn the [SQL Sandbox](../studios/sql-sandbox/), list the books that are **longer than the average book**, longest first.\r\n\r\n*Hint: `WHERE pages > (SELECT AVG(pages) FROM books)`. The average is 271.75, so you should get 4 books.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A subquery is a `SELECT` inside brackets inside another query; the inner one runs first.\r\n- A one-value subquery works with `=`, `>`, `<` (for example, above the average).\r\n- A list subquery works with `IN` and `NOT IN`; watch out for `NULL` in `NOT IN`.\r\n- A subquery in `FROM` is a derived table: it needs brackets and a name (`AS t`).\r\n- Many questions can use a JOIN or a subquery; pick the clearer one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is a subquery?\r\n2. Which runs first: the inner or the outer query?\r\n3. Which operator do you use when the subquery returns many rows?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A `SELECT` query written in brackets inside another query.\r\n2. The inner query.\r\n3. `IN` (or `NOT IN`).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n1. List books with **fewer** pages than the average book.\r\n2. List the titles of books that **Zayd** (student 1) has borrowed, using `IN`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title, pages FROM books\r\nWHERE pages < (SELECT AVG(pages) FROM books)\r\nORDER BY pages;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | pages |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Arabic Calligraphy Basics | 120 |\r\n| Digital Stewardship | 180 |\r\n| Ethical Technology | 210 |\r\n| Introduction to Networks | 240 |\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT title FROM books\r\nWHERE id IN (SELECT book_id FROM loans WHERE student_id = 1);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Clean Code |\r\n| Algorithm Design |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nThe charity club wants to praise every cause whose total is **above the average cause total**.\r\n\r\n1. Write a derived table that gives each cause's total.\r\n2. Use it twice: once to list the causes, and once inside a one-value subquery for the average total.\r\n3. Predict the result.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT t.cause, t.total\r\nFROM (SELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\n      FROM donations GROUP BY cause) AS t\r\nWHERE t.total > (\r\n    SELECT AVG(t2.total)\r\n    FROM (SELECT cause, SUM(amount) AS total\r\n          FROM donations GROUP BY cause) AS t2\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe cause totals are 5500, 15000 and 7000. Their average is 27500 ÷ 3 ≈ 9166.67. Only one cause is above it:\r\n\r\n| cause | total |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Clean Water | 15000 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-07-projects/proj01-student-management-db.md": "# Capstone Project 1: School Student & Grade Management Database\r\n\r\n> **Project Mission**: Design, build and query a complete database for a school's courses and exam marks. You will use everything from this book: tables, keys, constraints, inserts, joins, grouping and subqueries.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt the end of term, the school office prepares report cards. Each student takes several courses, and each course has many students. On paper, this means piles of mark sheets, one per course, and someone copying marks onto every student's card by hand. One copying slip and a student gets the wrong mark. In this project you build the database that prints every report card straight from one trusted set of marks.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Design a \"many-to-many\" link with a junction table.\r\n- Build three tables with primary keys, foreign keys and `CHECK` / `UNIQUE` rules.\r\n- Load sample data with multi-row `INSERT`s.\r\n- Write report queries with `JOIN`, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING` and a subquery.\r\n- Test that the rules really protect the data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗺️ Step 1: Plan the Tables\r\n\r\nA student takes many courses, and a course has many students. This is a **many-to-many** relationship. We cannot store it in `students` or in `courses` alone. Instead we add a third table, `enrollments`, where each row means \"this student takes this course\". A table that links two others like this is called a **junction table**.\r\n\r\n```\r\nstudents          courses\r\n┌───────────┐     ┌──────────────┐\r\n│ PK id     │     │ PK id        │\r\n│ roll_no   │     │ course_code  │\r\n│ full_name │     │ course_title │\r\n│ email     │     │ credit_hours │\r\n│ grade     │     │ teacher_name │\r\n└─────┬─────┘     └──────┬───────┘\r\n      │                  │\r\n      └──────┐   ┌───────┘\r\n             ▼   ▼\r\n        enrollments\r\n        ┌───────────────┐\r\n        │ PK id         │\r\n        │ FK student_id │\r\n        │ FK course_id  │\r\n        │ semester      │\r\n        │ exam_marks    │\r\n        │ grade_letter  │\r\n        └───────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ Step 2: Create the Database and Tables\r\n\r\nRun this in phpMyAdmin's **SQL** tab (or the command line):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS school_portal_db\r\n  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4\r\n  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n\r\nUSE school_portal_db;\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS students (\r\n    id            INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    roll_number   VARCHAR(15) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    full_name     VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    email         VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    grade_level   INT NOT NULL CHECK (grade_level BETWEEN 6 AND 12),\r\n    enrolled_date DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    is_active     BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS courses (\r\n    id            INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    course_code   VARCHAR(15) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    course_title  VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    credit_hours  INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 3 CHECK (credit_hours BETWEEN 1 AND 5),\r\n    teacher_name  VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS enrollments (\r\n    id            INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    student_id    INT NOT NULL,\r\n    course_id     INT NOT NULL,\r\n    semester      VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Fall 2026',\r\n    exam_marks    DECIMAL(5,2) NOT NULL CHECK (exam_marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\r\n    grade_letter  CHAR(2) NOT NULL,\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_enr_student FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_enr_course  FOREIGN KEY (course_id)  REFERENCES courses(id),\r\n    UNIQUE KEY uq_student_course_sem (student_id, course_id, semester)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nDesign choices to notice:\r\n\r\n- `DECIMAL(5,2)` holds marks like `94.50` exactly (Chapter 6).\r\n- The `UNIQUE KEY` on three columns together means a student cannot be enrolled in the same course twice in one semester. Each column alone may repeat; the **combination** may not.\r\n- Both foreign keys use the safe default (`RESTRICT`, Chapter 15). Marks are an official record, so deleting a student must not silently delete their marks. Use the `is_active` flag (a soft delete, Chapter 13) when a student leaves.\r\n- `students` and `courses` are created before `enrollments`, because parents come first.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📥 Step 3: Add Sample Data\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO courses (course_code, course_title, credit_hours, teacher_name) VALUES\r\n  ('CS-101',   'Intro to Computer Science',    3, 'Mr. Imran'),\r\n  ('CS-201',   'Web & Database Engineering',   4, 'Mr. Imran'),\r\n  ('MATH-101', 'Algebra Foundations',          3, 'Dr. Amina'),\r\n  ('ETH-101',  'Ethics & Digital Stewardship', 2, 'Ms. Noor');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO students (roll_number, full_name, email, grade_level, enrolled_date) VALUES\r\n  ('STD-001', 'Aisha Siddiq',  'aisha@school.example.org',  9,  '2026-08-15'),\r\n  ('STD-002', 'Bilal Mansoor', 'bilal@school.example.org',  9,  '2026-08-15'),\r\n  ('STD-003', 'Fatima Zahra',  'fatima@school.example.org', 10, '2026-08-16'),\r\n  ('STD-004', 'Hamza Tariq',   'hamza@school.example.org',  9,  '2026-08-17'),\r\n  ('STD-005', 'Zayd Ali',      'zayd@school.example.org',   10, '2026-08-18'),\r\n  ('STD-006', 'Maryam Yusuf',  'maryam@school.example.org', 10, '2026-08-20');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO enrollments (student_id, course_id, exam_marks, grade_letter) VALUES\r\n  (1, 1, 94.50, 'A+'), (1, 2, 92.00, 'A'),  (1, 4, 98.00, 'A+'),\r\n  (2, 1, 85.00, 'A-'), (2, 3, 76.50, 'B'),\r\n  (3, 2, 88.50, 'A'),  (3, 4, 95.00, 'A+'),\r\n  (4, 1, 68.00, 'C+'),\r\n  (5, 3, 82.00, 'B+');\r\n```\r\n\r\nAll the data is sample data. The emails use `example.org`, a domain reserved for examples, so no real person is involved. We leave out `semester`, so every row gets the default `'Fall 2026'`. Maryam (STD-006) has just joined and has no enrollments yet.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📊 Step 4: Report Queries\r\n\r\n### Report 1: Aisha's report card (three-table join)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.roll_number, s.full_name AS student, c.course_code,\r\n       c.course_title, e.exam_marks, e.grade_letter\r\nFROM enrollments e\r\nJOIN students s ON e.student_id = s.id\r\nJOIN courses c  ON e.course_id = c.id\r\nWHERE s.roll_number = 'STD-001'\r\nORDER BY e.exam_marks DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| roll_number | student | course_code | course_title | exam_marks | grade_letter |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- |\r\n| STD-001 | Aisha Siddiq | ETH-101 | Ethics & Digital Stewardship | 98.00 | A+ |\r\n| STD-001 | Aisha Siddiq | CS-101 | Intro to Computer Science | 94.50 | A+ |\r\n| STD-001 | Aisha Siddiq | CS-201 | Web & Database Engineering | 92.00 | A |\r\n\r\n### Report 2: Course statistics (LEFT JOIN + GROUP BY)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT c.course_code,\r\n       COUNT(e.student_id)          AS enrolled,\r\n       ROUND(AVG(e.exam_marks), 2)  AS average,\r\n       MIN(e.exam_marks)            AS lowest,\r\n       MAX(e.exam_marks)            AS highest\r\nFROM courses c\r\nLEFT JOIN enrollments e ON c.id = e.course_id\r\nGROUP BY c.id, c.course_code\r\nORDER BY average DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| course_code | enrolled | average | lowest | highest |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\r\n| ETH-101 | 2 | 96.50 | 95.00 | 98.00 |\r\n| CS-201 | 2 | 90.25 | 88.50 | 92.00 |\r\n| CS-101 | 3 | 82.50 | 68.00 | 94.50 |\r\n| MATH-101 | 2 | 79.25 | 76.50 | 82.00 |\r\n\r\nThe `LEFT JOIN` means a new course with no students would still be listed, with 0 enrolled.\r\n\r\n### Report 3: Honour roll (HAVING)\r\n\r\nStudents whose average mark is 85 or more:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.full_name,\r\n       COUNT(e.course_id)          AS courses,\r\n       ROUND(AVG(e.exam_marks), 2) AS average\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN enrollments e ON s.id = e.student_id\r\nGROUP BY s.id, s.full_name\r\nHAVING AVG(e.exam_marks) >= 85\r\nORDER BY average DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name | courses | average |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Aisha Siddiq | 3 | 94.83 |\r\n| Fatima Zahra | 2 | 91.75 |\r\n\r\n### Report 4: Students not yet enrolled (anti-join)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.roll_number, s.full_name\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN enrollments e ON e.student_id = s.id\r\nWHERE e.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| roll_number | full_name |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| STD-006 | Maryam Yusuf |\r\n\r\n### Report 5: Above the course average (subquery)\r\n\r\nWho scored above the CS-101 average (82.50)?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.full_name, e.exam_marks\r\nFROM enrollments e\r\nJOIN students s ON s.id = e.student_id\r\nWHERE e.course_id = 1\r\n  AND e.exam_marks > (SELECT AVG(exam_marks)\r\n                      FROM enrollments WHERE course_id = 1);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name | exam_marks |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Aisha Siddiq | 94.50 |\r\n| Bilal Mansoor | 85.00 |\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** These tables are not in the SQL Sandbox. Build this project in phpMyAdmin. (In SQLite you would write `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` instead of `INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY`.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛡️ Step 5: Test the Rules\r\n\r\nA good builder tries to break their own work. Each of these should fail:\r\n\r\n| Test | MySQL's answer |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Enrol Aisha in CS-101 again | `ERROR 1062: Duplicate entry '1-1-Fall 2026' for key 'uq_student_course_sem'` |\r\n| Give Maryam a mark of 105 | ``ERROR 4025: CONSTRAINT `enrollments.exam_marks` failed`` |\r\n| Delete Aisha (id 1) | `ERROR 1451: Cannot delete or update a parent row ...` |\r\n\r\nEvery failure here is good news: the database refused bad or dangerous changes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: storing the course list inside `students`.**\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- wrong idea\r\ncourses_taken VARCHAR(200)  -- 'CS-101, CS-201, ETH-101'\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: you cannot store a mark per course, cannot join to `courses`, and finding \"everyone in CS-101\" needs messy text searching.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** a junction table (`enrollments`) with one row per student-course pair, as in this project.\r\n\r\n**Mistake: `ON DELETE CASCADE` on marks.** It looks tidy, but one careless `DELETE FROM students` would wipe that student's whole academic history. Keep `RESTRICT` and use `is_active = FALSE`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nEnrol Maryam (student 6) in *Algebra Foundations* (course 3) with 91 marks and grade `'A'`. Then re-run Reports 2, 3 and 4.\r\n\r\n*Hint: MATH-101's average becomes 83.17; Maryam joins the honour roll and leaves the \"not enrolled\" list.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A many-to-many relationship needs a junction table with two foreign keys.\r\n- `CHECK`, `UNIQUE` (even on several columns) and foreign keys keep the data honest.\r\n- Report cards and statistics come from joins, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING` and subqueries.\r\n- `LEFT JOIN` and `COUNT(column)` keep empty courses or students visible.\r\n- Official records deserve `RESTRICT` plus soft delete, not `CASCADE` (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is a junction table, and which one does this project use?\r\n2. What does `UNIQUE KEY uq_student_course_sem (student_id, course_id, semester)` prevent?\r\n3. Why are `students` and `courses` created before `enrollments`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A table that links two other tables in a many-to-many relationship. Here it is `enrollments`.\r\n2. The same student being enrolled in the same course twice in the same semester.\r\n3. `enrollments` has foreign keys pointing to them. Parent tables must exist first.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite a query that shows each student's total **credit hours** (from `courses.credit_hours`), most first.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT s.full_name, SUM(c.credit_hours) AS credits\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN enrollments e ON e.student_id = s.id\r\nJOIN courses c     ON c.id = e.course_id\r\nGROUP BY s.id, s.full_name\r\nORDER BY credits DESC, s.full_name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name | credits |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Aisha Siddiq | 9 |\r\n| Bilal Mansoor | 6 |\r\n| Fatima Zahra | 6 |\r\n| Hamza Tariq | 3 |\r\n| Zayd Ali | 3 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nExtend the project with attendance.\r\n\r\n1. Create an `attendance` table: `id`, `student_id` (FK), `course_id` (FK), `class_date`, `present` (`BOOLEAN`), with one row per student, course and date at most.\r\n2. Insert three sample rows.\r\n3. Write a query showing each student's number of days present.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE attendance (\r\n    id         INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    student_id INT NOT NULL,\r\n    course_id  INT NOT NULL,\r\n    class_date DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    present    BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_att_student FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_att_course  FOREIGN KEY (course_id)  REFERENCES courses(id),\r\n    UNIQUE KEY uq_att (student_id, course_id, class_date)\r\n);\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO attendance (student_id, course_id, class_date, present) VALUES\r\n  (1, 1, '2026-09-01', TRUE),\r\n  (1, 1, '2026-09-02', TRUE),\r\n  (2, 1, '2026-09-01', FALSE);\r\n\r\nSELECT s.full_name, SUM(a.present) AS days_present\r\nFROM students s\r\nJOIN attendance a ON a.student_id = s.id\r\nGROUP BY s.id, s.full_name;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name | days_present |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Aisha Siddiq | 2 |\r\n| Bilal Mansoor | 0 |\r\n\r\n`SUM(present)` works because `TRUE` is stored as 1 and `FALSE` as 0.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-07-projects/proj02-library-lending-db.md": "# Capstone Project 2: Neighborhood Library & Book Lending Database\r\n\r\n> **Project Mission**: Build a fair, friendly community library system. Track the books and how many copies are on the shelf, the members, and every borrowing with its due date, so that reminders go out on time.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA neighbourhood library runs on trust. Members borrow a book for two weeks and bring it back. The volunteers want three things: to know which copies are on the shelf, to send a polite reminder when a book is past its due date, and to see which books the community loves most. There are no fines. A kind reminder is enough. This project builds the database behind that library.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Design a lending system with three linked tables.\r\n- Use `NULL` to mean \"not returned yet\".\r\n- Find books that are out and books that are past their due date.\r\n- Keep copy counts correct with a transaction.\r\n- Rank books by popularity with `LEFT JOIN` and `GROUP BY`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗺️ Step 1: Plan the Tables\r\n\r\nMembers and books have a many-to-many relationship: one member borrows many books, and one book is borrowed by many members over time. The junction table is `borrow_records`. Each row is one borrowing.\r\n\r\n```\r\nmembers          books\r\n┌────────────┐   ┌──────────────────┐\r\n│ PK id      │   │ PK id            │\r\n│ card_number│   │ isbn             │\r\n│ full_name  │   │ title, author    │\r\n│ phone      │   │ category         │\r\n│ joined_date│   │ total_copies     │\r\n│ is_active  │   │ available_copies │\r\n└─────┬──────┘   └────────┬─────────┘\r\n      └──────┐    ┌───────┘\r\n             ▼    ▼\r\n       borrow_records\r\n       ┌─────────────────────┐\r\n       │ PK id               │\r\n       │ FK member_id        │\r\n       │ FK book_id          │\r\n       │ borrow_date         │\r\n       │ due_date            │\r\n       │ return_date (NULL   │\r\n       │   while still out)  │\r\n       │ status              │\r\n       └─────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe store a phone number only because the library needs it for reminders. Nothing else personal is collected (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ Step 2: Create the Database\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS community_library_db\r\n  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4\r\n  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n\r\nUSE community_library_db;\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS members (\r\n    id           INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    card_number  VARCHAR(15) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    full_name    VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    phone        VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\r\n    joined_date  DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    is_active    BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\r\n    id               INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    isbn             VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    title            VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL,\r\n    author           VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    category         VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'General',\r\n    total_copies     INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (total_copies >= 0),\r\n    available_copies INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (available_copies >= 0)\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS borrow_records (\r\n    id           INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    member_id    INT NOT NULL,\r\n    book_id      INT NOT NULL,\r\n    borrow_date  DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    due_date     DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    return_date  DATE NULL,\r\n    status       VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Borrowed',\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_loan_member FOREIGN KEY (member_id) REFERENCES members(id),\r\n    CONSTRAINT fk_loan_book   FOREIGN KEY (book_id)   REFERENCES books(id)\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `return_date DATE NULL` is allowed to be empty. `NULL` here means \"still borrowed\".\r\n- `CHECK (available_copies >= 0)` makes it impossible to lend a copy that is not on the shelf.\r\n- Foreign keys use the safe default (`RESTRICT`), so a member with borrowing history cannot be deleted by accident. Use `is_active = FALSE` instead.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📥 Step 3: Add Sample Data\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nINSERT INTO members (card_number, full_name, phone, joined_date) VALUES\r\n  ('LIB-1001', 'Aisha Siddiq',  '0300-0000001', '2026-01-10'),\r\n  ('LIB-1002', 'Bilal Mansoor', '0300-0000002', '2026-02-14'),\r\n  ('LIB-1003', 'Fatima Zahra',  '0300-0000003', '2026-03-01'),\r\n  ('LIB-1004', 'Hamza Tariq',   '0300-0000004', '2026-04-12');\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO books (isbn, title, author, category, total_copies, available_copies) VALUES\r\n  ('978-01', 'Web Development for Juniors',  'S. Ahmed',   'Technology', 5, 4),\r\n  ('978-02', 'Ethics in the Digital Age',    'Dr. Amina',  'Ethics',     3, 2),\r\n  ('978-03', 'Stories from Islamic History', 'R. Hussain', 'History',    8, 8),\r\n  ('978-04', 'Clean Code & Craftsmanship',   'K. Malik',   'Technology', 1, 0);\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO borrow_records (member_id, book_id, borrow_date, due_date, return_date, status) VALUES\r\n  (1, 1, '2026-09-01', '2026-09-15', '2026-09-14', 'Returned'),\r\n  (1, 2, '2026-09-28', '2026-10-12', NULL,         'Borrowed'),\r\n  (2, 4, '2026-10-05', '2026-10-19', NULL,         'Borrowed'),\r\n  (3, 1, '2026-08-20', '2026-09-03', '2026-09-02', 'Returned'),\r\n  (3, 1, '2026-09-20', '2026-10-04', NULL,         'Borrowed');\r\n```\r\n\r\nAll names, numbers and books are sample data. Check that the counts agree: three records are still out (books 1, 2 and 4), and each of those books shows one copy fewer on the shelf.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📊 Step 4: Everyday Queries\r\n\r\n### Query 1: What is out right now?\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT m.card_number, m.full_name AS borrower, b.title,\r\n       r.borrow_date, r.due_date\r\nFROM borrow_records r\r\nJOIN members m ON r.member_id = m.id\r\nJOIN books b   ON r.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE r.return_date IS NULL\r\nORDER BY r.due_date;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| card_number | borrower | title | borrow_date | due_date |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| LIB-1003 | Fatima Zahra | Web Development for Juniors | 2026-09-20 | 2026-10-04 |\r\n| LIB-1001 | Aisha Siddiq | Ethics in the Digital Age | 2026-09-28 | 2026-10-12 |\r\n| LIB-1002 | Bilal Mansoor | Clean Code & Craftsmanship | 2026-10-05 | 2026-10-19 |\r\n\r\n### Query 2: Who needs a reminder?\r\n\r\nLoans past their due date on 13 October 2026. `DATEDIFF(a, b)` is a MySQL function that gives the number of days from date `b` to date `a`:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT m.full_name, b.title, r.due_date,\r\n       DATEDIFF('2026-10-13', r.due_date) AS days_late\r\nFROM borrow_records r\r\nJOIN members m ON r.member_id = m.id\r\nJOIN books b   ON r.book_id = b.id\r\nWHERE r.return_date IS NULL\r\n  AND r.due_date < '2026-10-13'\r\nORDER BY days_late DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name | title | due_date | days_late |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\r\n| Fatima Zahra | Web Development for Juniors | 2026-10-04 | 9 |\r\n| Aisha Siddiq | Ethics in the Digital Age | 2026-10-12 | 1 |\r\n\r\nIn a real app, write `CURRENT_DATE` (today's date) instead of `'2026-10-13'`. We used a fixed date so your result matches the book.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Sandbox note:** SQLite has no `DATEDIFF`. There you would write `julianday('2026-10-13') - julianday(due_date)`.\r\n\r\n### Query 3: The community's favourite books\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT b.title, COUNT(r.id) AS times_borrowed\r\nFROM books b\r\nLEFT JOIN borrow_records r ON b.id = r.book_id\r\nGROUP BY b.id, b.title\r\nORDER BY times_borrowed DESC, b.title;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| title | times_borrowed |\r\n| :--- | ---: |\r\n| Web Development for Juniors | 3 |\r\n| Clean Code & Craftsmanship | 1 |\r\n| Ethics in the Digital Age | 1 |\r\n| Stories from Islamic History | 0 |\r\n\r\nThe `LEFT JOIN` keeps *Stories from Islamic History*, so the volunteers can see it deserves a display table.\r\n\r\n### Query 4: Members who have not borrowed yet (subquery)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT full_name FROM members\r\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT member_id FROM borrow_records);\r\n```\r\n\r\n| full_name |\r\n| :--- |\r\n| Hamza Tariq |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Step 5: Lending and Returning Safely\r\n\r\nLending a book changes **two** tables. A transaction (Chapter 14) keeps them in step.\r\n\r\n### Lend a book\r\n\r\nHamza borrows *Stories from Islamic History* (book 3) for 14 days:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nUPDATE books\r\nSET available_copies = available_copies - 1\r\nWHERE id = 3 AND available_copies > 0;\r\n\r\nINSERT INTO borrow_records (member_id, book_id, borrow_date, due_date)\r\nVALUES (4, 3, '2026-10-13', '2026-10-27');\r\n\r\nCOMMIT;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `UPDATE` reports `Rows matched: 1  Changed: 1`, so a copy was free. Book 3 now shows 7 available copies, and the new record has `return_date` = `NULL` and `status` = `'Borrowed'` (the default).\r\n\r\n### When no copy is free\r\n\r\nBook 4 has 0 copies on the shelf. The same `UPDATE` with `AND available_copies > 0` reports `Rows matched: 0  Changed: 0`. Seeing 0, the volunteer runs `ROLLBACK;` instead of adding the record. (Without the `AND` test, the `CHECK` rule would stop it anyway: ``ERROR 4025: CONSTRAINT `books.available_copies` failed``.)\r\n\r\n### Return a book\r\n\r\nFatima brings back her copy (record 5):\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSTART TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\nUPDATE borrow_records\r\nSET return_date = '2026-10-13', status = 'Returned'\r\nWHERE id = 5;\r\n\r\nUPDATE books\r\nSET available_copies = available_copies + 1\r\nWHERE id = 1;\r\n\r\nCOMMIT;\r\n```\r\n\r\nBook 1 is back to 5 of 5 copies on the shelf.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake: updating only one table.**\r\n\r\nA volunteer adds a borrow record but forgets to lower `available_copies`. The catalogue now says a copy is on the shelf when it is not, and the next member is disappointed.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** always lend and return inside one transaction, as in Step 5, and check the \"Rows matched\" number before you commit.\r\n\r\n**Mistake: testing `return_date = NULL`.** It never matches (Chapter 10). Use `return_date IS NULL`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAisha returns *Ethics in the Digital Age* (record 2). Write the transaction, then re-run Query 1 and Query 2.\r\n\r\n*Hint: update `borrow_records` (record 2) and `books` (book 2). Afterwards, only Bilal's loan is still out, and nobody needs a reminder.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `borrow_records` links members and books; `return_date IS NULL` means \"still out\".\r\n- `CHECK (available_copies >= 0)` makes lending a missing copy impossible.\r\n- Joins answer \"what is out?\"; date tests answer \"who needs a reminder?\"\r\n- `LEFT JOIN` + `COUNT` ranks books and keeps unborrowed ones visible.\r\n- Lending and returning change two tables, so wrap them in a transaction.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What does `return_date = NULL` mean in this design, and how do you search for it?\r\n2. Why does `books` store both `total_copies` and `available_copies`?\r\n3. Why is a transaction used when lending a book?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. The book is still borrowed. Search with `WHERE return_date IS NULL`.\r\n2. `total_copies` is how many the library owns; `available_copies` is how many are on the shelf now.\r\n3. Lending changes two tables (a new borrow record and one fewer copy). Both must happen, or neither.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nShow each book category with its total copies and the copies on the shelf (use the starting data).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT category,\r\n       SUM(total_copies)     AS copies,\r\n       SUM(available_copies) AS on_shelf\r\nFROM books\r\nGROUP BY category\r\nORDER BY category;\r\n```\r\n\r\n| category | copies | on_shelf |\r\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\r\n| Ethics | 3 | 2 |\r\n| History | 8 | 8 |\r\n| Technology | 6 | 4 |\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nAdd a **renewal** feature: a member may extend a loan by 14 days, once.\r\n\r\n1. Add a column `renewed` (`BOOLEAN`, default `FALSE`) to `borrow_records`.\r\n2. Write one `UPDATE` that renews record 3 only if it is still out and has not been renewed. Use `DATE_ADD(due_date, INTERVAL 14 DAY)`, which adds 14 days to a date.\r\n3. Explain how a renewal limit keeps the library fair to everyone.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nALTER TABLE borrow_records\r\nADD COLUMN renewed BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE;\r\n\r\nUPDATE borrow_records\r\nSET due_date = DATE_ADD(due_date, INTERVAL 14 DAY),\r\n    renewed  = TRUE\r\nWHERE id = 3\r\n  AND return_date IS NULL\r\n  AND renewed = FALSE;\r\n```\r\n\r\nBilal's due date moves from 2026-10-19 to 2026-11-02. Running the same `UPDATE` again changes 0 rows, because `renewed` is now `TRUE`.\r\n\r\n3. Popular books have few copies. A limit stops one member from keeping a book for months, so others get their turn too.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "README.md": "# Class 9 MySQL: Databases & SQL Queries — Textbook Curriculum\n\nThe official, production-grade digital textbook for mastering relational databases and SQL queries, formalized under the **Junior Coders** package.\n\n---\n\n## Pedagogical & Ethical Framework\n\nThis textbook is constructed strictly in alignment with the **Core Islamic Ethical Framework** codified in root `GEMINI.md`:\n- **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: Teaching databases as digital tools for community service, education, ethical commerce, and public health.\n- **Data Trust & Privacy (*Amānah*)**: Instilling that user databases are sacred trusts. Enforcing parameterized statements, secure hashing, and access authorization.\n- **Halal Commerce**: Rejection of usury (*Ribā*), gambling (*Maysir*), or deceptive schemas. Examples use student records, organic honey inventory, library book lending, and charity (*Zakat*) distribution.\n\n---\n\n## Curriculum Structure\n\n- **Part 1: Introduction to Databases**\n  - `ch01-what-is-a-database.md`: Filing cabinet analogy, spreadsheets vs. databases, RDBMS and SQL.\n  - `ch02-tables-rows-and-columns.md`: Table structure, fields, records, and the 3 golden design rules.\n  - `ch03-setting-up-mysql.md`: XAMPP MySQL service, phpMyAdmin, and MySQL CLI commands.\n\n- **Part 2: Creating Databases & Tables (DDL)**\n  - `ch04-create-database.md`: `CREATE DATABASE`, `USE`, `utf8mb4` character set, and `DROP`.\n  - `ch05-create-table.md`: `CREATE TABLE` syntax, schema blueprints, and `DESCRIBE`.\n  - `ch06-data-types.md`: `INT`, `VARCHAR`, `TEXT`, `DECIMAL(10,2)`, `DATE`, `TIMESTAMP`.\n  - `ch07-primary-keys.md`: Primary Keys, `AUTO_INCREMENT`, `UNIQUE`, `NOT NULL`, and `CHECK`.\n\n- **Part 3: Inserting & Querying Data (DML)**\n  - `ch08-insert-into.md`: Single and batch `INSERT INTO` statements.\n  - `ch09-select-basics.md`: Column projections, `AS` aliases, calculated columns, and `DISTINCT`.\n  - `ch10-where-clause.md`: Filtering with `WHERE`, comparison operators, `AND`, `OR`, `BETWEEN`, `IN`, `IS NULL`.\n  - `ch11-order-by-and-limit.md`: Sorting with `ORDER BY (ASC/DESC)` and pagination with `LIMIT` & `OFFSET`.\n\n- **Part 4: Updating & Deleting Data (DML)**\n  - `ch12-update-statement.md`: `UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE`, batch modifications, and missing WHERE dangers.\n  - `ch13-delete-statement.md`: `DELETE FROM`, `TRUNCATE TABLE`, `DROP TABLE`, and Soft Deletes.\n  - `ch14-safe-updates.md`: `SQL_SAFE_UPDATES`, ACID principles, and `COMMIT` / `ROLLBACK` transactions.\n\n- **Part 5: Table Relationships & JOINs**\n  - `ch15-foreign-keys.md`: Relational theory, parent-child tables, normalization, and Foreign Keys.\n  - `ch16-what-is-a-join.md`: Combining tables, table aliases, and the `ON` condition.\n  - `ch17-inner-join.md`: `INNER JOIN` in depth, Venn diagram matching, and 3-table joins.\n  - `ch18-left-and-right-joins.md`: `LEFT JOIN`, `COALESCE`, and detecting orphaned records (anti-joins).\n\n- **Part 6: Aggregations & Advanced Queries**\n  - `ch19-aggregate-functions.md`: `COUNT()`, `SUM()`, `AVG()`, `MIN()`, `MAX()`.\n  - `ch20-group-by-and-having.md`: Grouping categories with `GROUP BY` and group filtering with `HAVING`.\n  - `ch21-like-and-wildcards.md`: Pattern searching with `LIKE`, `%` and `_` wildcards, and escaping.\n  - `ch22-subqueries.md`: Subqueries in `WHERE` and `FROM` clauses, and dynamic aggregations.\n\n- **Part 7: Capstone Projects & Reference**\n  - `proj01-student-management-db.md`: School Student & Grade Management Database (4 relational tables).\n  - `proj02-library-lending-db.md`: Community Library & Book Lending System.\n  - `mysql-cheatsheet.md`: Rapid reference guide for DDL, DML, JOINs, and data security checklist.\n\n---\n\n## Offline Bundling\n\nRun the bundler script anytime markdown chapters are updated:\n```bash\nnode scripts/bundle-books.js\n```\nThis generates `chapters-bundle.js` for instant, offline, zero-CORS reading in any standard browser.\n",
  "reference/mysql-cheatsheet.md": "# MySQL Quick Reference Cheatsheet\r\n\r\n> **Quick Navigation**: A rapid reference for everyday MySQL syntax, DDL, DML, JOINs, aggregations, and security best practices.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Database Operations (DDL)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Create database with universal character encoding\r\nCREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS school_db\r\n  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 \r\n  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;\r\n\r\n-- Switch active database\r\nUSE school_db;\r\n\r\n-- List all databases on server\r\nSHOW DATABASES;\r\n\r\n-- Show currently selected database\r\nSELECT DATABASE();\r\n\r\n-- Delete database and all tables (Permanent!)\r\nDROP DATABASE IF EXISTS school_db;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Table Schema Operations (DDL)\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Create table with constraints\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS students (\r\n    id            INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    full_name     VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\r\n    email         VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\r\n    age           INT NOT NULL CHECK (age >= 5),\r\n    grade_level   INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 9,\r\n    enrolled_date DATE NOT NULL,\r\n    is_active     BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,\r\n    created_at    TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n) ENGINE=InnoDB;\r\n\r\n-- Show all tables in active database\r\nSHOW TABLES;\r\n\r\n-- Inspect columns and data types\r\nDESCRIBE students;\r\n\r\n-- Delete table completely\r\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;\r\n\r\n-- Truncate table (Instant wipe + reset AUTO_INCREMENT)\r\nTRUNCATE TABLE students;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Data Manipulation: CRUD (DML)\r\n\r\n### Create (INSERT)\r\n```sql\r\n-- Insert single row\r\nINSERT INTO students (full_name, email, age, enrolled_date)\r\nVALUES ('Aisha Siddiq', 'aisha@srinagar.edu.in', 15, '2025-08-15');\r\n\r\n-- Batch insert multiple rows\r\nINSERT INTO students (full_name, email, age, enrolled_date) VALUES\r\n('Bilal Mansoor', 'bilal@srinagar.edu.in', 15, '2025-08-15'),\r\n('Fatima Zahra', 'fatima@srinagar.edu.in', 16, '2025-08-16');\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Read (SELECT)\r\n```sql\r\n-- Select specific columns with aliases\r\nSELECT id, full_name AS student_name, grade_level \r\nFROM students \r\nWHERE grade_level = 9 AND is_active = TRUE\r\nORDER BY full_name ASC \r\nLIMIT 10 OFFSET 0;\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Update (UPDATE)\r\n```sql\r\n-- Selective update (ALWAYS use WHERE!)\r\nUPDATE students \r\nSET email = 'new.email@srinagar.edu.in', grade_level = 10 \r\nWHERE id = 1;\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### Delete (DELETE)\r\n```sql\r\n-- Selective delete (ALWAYS use WHERE!)\r\nDELETE FROM students \r\nWHERE id = 5;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. SQL JOINs Matrix\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- INNER JOIN: Only rows existing in BOTH tables\r\nSELECT s.full_name, c.course_name\r\nFROM students s\r\nINNER JOIN courses c ON s.course_id = c.id;\r\n\r\n-- LEFT JOIN: Keeps ALL left rows, fills missing right columns with NULL\r\nSELECT s.full_name, c.course_name\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN courses c ON s.course_id = c.id;\r\n\r\n-- Anti-Join: Find students with NO course enrolled\r\nSELECT s.full_name\r\nFROM students s\r\nLEFT JOIN courses c ON s.course_id = c.id\r\nWHERE c.id IS NULL;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Aggregate Functions & Grouping\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nSELECT \r\n    grade_level,\r\n    COUNT(*) AS total_students,\r\n    ROUND(AVG(age), 1) AS average_age,\r\n    MIN(enrolled_date) AS earliest_enrollment\r\nFROM students\r\nWHERE is_active = TRUE\r\nGROUP BY grade_level\r\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 10\r\nORDER BY total_students DESC;\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Security & Ethical Checklist (*Amānah*)\r\n\r\n- [ ] **Parameterized Statements**: Always use PDO prepared statements in PHP (`$stmt->prepare(\"SELECT ... WHERE email = ?\")`). Never concatenate raw `$_POST` strings into SQL!\r\n- [ ] **Password Hashing**: Never store plain-text passwords in `VARCHAR`. Always use `password_hash()` in PHP and store the 60+ character hash.\r\n- [ ] **No Floating-Point Money**: Always declare financial and Zakat balances as `DECIMAL(10, 2)`, never `FLOAT` or `DOUBLE`.\r\n- [ ] **Granular Permissions**: Restrict web application MySQL users to `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`. Never grant `DROP` permissions to production web application accounts!\r\n"
};
